      "sourceText": "うちに (JLPT N3) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN3 Lesson 1: 5/23\n内\n(\nうち\n)\nに\nWhile, During\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nうちに\nWhile, During\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb +\nうちに\nVerb［ている］\n+\nうちに\n［い］Adjective\n+\nうちに\n［な］Adjective\n+\nな\n+\nうちに\nNoun +\nの\n+\nうちに\nDetails\nRegister\nStandard\nRare Kanji\n裡に\nAbout うちに\nEnglish\nJapanese\nWhen うち, (sometimes seen using the kanji\n内\n(\nうち\n)\n, or\n裡\n(\nうち\n)\n) is paired with the\ncase marking particle\nに\n, it expresses a period/space 'within' which something else will/should happen/exist. It quite often (but not always) carries the nuance of (A) being the 'best', or the 'optimal' time/space within which (B) could occur/exist. In English,\nうちに\ncan simply be translated as 'while', or 'during'.\nうちに\nmay be paired with the dictionary or ている form of verbs, the plain form of\nい-Adjectives\n, nouns followed by の, or\nな-Adjectives\nthat are followed by な.\nUnlike\n間\n(\nあいだ\n)\nに\n,\nうちに\nexpresses something that can happen/exist anywhere 'within' the span of (A), and is therefore non-specific as to where (B) is located.\nFun Fact\nThe kanji\n裡\n(\nうち\n)\nrefers literally to the inner walls/lining of something, and anything that is enclosed within that space. This can be thought of as where the 'non-specificity' of うち comes from, in regard to where within that space something is.\nRelated\nExpand All\nながら\nWhile ~ing, During, As\nN4 Grammar\nながら attaches to verb stems to express performing one action simultaneously with another, e.g. 音楽を聴きながら走る 'listen to music while running'. In contrast, うちに can follow various words or phrases to express doing something while a certain state or condition persists, e.g. 若いうちにマラソンを走りたい ('I want to run a marathon while I'm young.').\nているところだ\n～ているところだ\nIn the process of doing ~ right now/at this moment\nN4 Grammar\nているところだ conveys that an action is in progress at that moment, with the nuance that the speaker is occupied with it, e.g. 晩ご飯を食べているところだ ('I'm in the middle of eating dinner.'). In contrast, うちに describes doing an action while a certain state is ongoing, often with the nuance that it's the ideal time, e.g. 熱いうちに食べましょう ('Let's eat it while it's hot.').\n中\nちゅう\nDuring, While, Throughout, In the process of\nN3 Grammar\nBoth expressions refer to a continued state, but in different ways. うちに means 'do (B) while (A),' that is, during the ideal time or condition for (A), e.g. 熱いうちに食べてね ('Eat it while it's hot.') In contrast, 中 attaches to nouns and simply means 'during (A)' or 'in the middle of (A)', e.g. 会議中 ('in the middle of a meeting'), 授業中に携帯が鳴った ('My phone rang during class.')\n最中に\nさいちゅうに\nIn the middle of\nN3 Grammar\nBoth can be translated as 'while', but differ in nuance. うちに emphasizes doing something before a condition changes, e.g. 熱いうちに食べてね ('Eat it while it's hot.') 最中に highlights something occurring in the very middle of another action, e.g. 会議の最中に電話が鳴った ('The phone rang in the middle of the meeting.')\nの間に\nのあいだに\nWhile, During, Between, Period\nN3 Grammar\nBoth の間に and うちに can express 'while' or 'during', but are used in different contexts. の間に simply conveys the idea of 'while (A), (B)', e.g. 映画を観ている間に眠ってしまった ('I fell asleep while/during watching a movie.') In contrast, うちに carries the nuance of doing something during an ideal time or condition, e.g. 明るいうちに帰ろう ('Let's go home while it's light out.')\nついでに\nついでに\nWhile you are at it, On the occasion\nN3 Grammar\nBoth can be translated as 'while', but with different implications. ついでに means 'while you're at it' and adds a convenient extra action, e.g. 買い物のついでに銀行に寄った ('I stopped by the bank while shopping.') うちに emphasizes doing something before a condition changes, e.g. 熱いうちに食べてね ('Eat it while it's hot.')\nないうちに\nないうちに\nBefore it becomes, Before ~ occurs, Without\nN3 Grammar\nうちに means 'while' a state persists, implying that an action is taken during that time, e.g. 明るいうちに帰る ('Go home while it's still light.') ないうちに means 'before' a negative state ends, emphasizing the importance of acting before something happens, e.g. 雨が降らないうちに出かける ('Leave before it rains.') Both suggest taking action while a condition remains unchanged.\nとき\nとき\nWhen, At the time of\nN4 Grammar\nとき expresses the time of an action or event, like 'when' in English. It can be used with nouns as in 雨の時 'when it rains', verbs as in 父が帰ってきた時 'when Dad came home', or adjectives as in 暑い時 'when it's hot', and can describe one-time or repeated actions. うちに means 'while' or 'during,' often with the nuance that it's the ideal or only time to act, e.g. 熱いうちに食べてね ('Eat it while it's hot.').\nているあいだに\n～ているあいだに\nWhile, During the time that ~\nN4 Grammar\nBoth patterns can express the idea of 'while,' but differ in nuance. ているあいだに refers to something happening during another ongoing action. For example, 勉強しているあいだに電話がかかってきた means 'The phone rang while I was studying.' うちに emphasizes doing something while a particular state still lasts. For example, 早いうちに帰ろう means 'Let's go home while it's still early.'\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n近\n(\nちか\n)\nい\n内\n(\nうち\n)\nに\n連絡\n(\nれんらく\n)\nします。\nI'll be in touch\nwithin\nthe near future.\n鉄\n(\nてつ\n)\nは\n熱\n(\nあつ\n)\nい\n内\n(\nうち\n)\nに\n打\n(\nう\n)\nて。\nStrike\nwhile\nthe iron is hot.\n授業\n(\nじゅぎょう\n)\nを\n受\n(\nう\n)\nけている\n内\n(\nうち\n)\nに\n、\n宿題\n(\nしゅくだい\n)\nを\n完成\n(\nかんせい\n)\nさせた。\nI was able to finish my homework\nwhile\ntaking a class.\n赤\n(\nあか\n)\nちゃんは\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべている\n内\n(\nうち\n)\nに\n寝\n(\nね\n)\nてしまった。\nThe baby fell asleep\nwhile\neating.\n日本\n(\nにほん\n)\nにいる\n内\n(\nうち\n)\nに\n東京\n(\nとうきょう\n)\nに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nってみたい。\nWhile\nI am in Japan, I want to see Tokyo.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nDifference between 前に and うちに\nJStackExchange\nOffline\n[AIAIJ] An Integrated Approach to Intermediate Japanese\nPage 208\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 512\nTobira\nPage 95\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nうちに – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (18 in total)\ndharlequin\nSeems like over the year ChatGPT got a little better in this particular example:\n1.\n運転している間に眠くなったので、コンビニで休憩をした。\n→\nI got sleepy\nat some point while\nI was driving, so I took a break at a convenience store.\n“間に”\nemphasizes the\ntime span\nof driving.\nIt means:\n“During that period of driving, this (getting sleepy) happened.”\nNeutral, factual, and time-based.\nImplication: sleepiness was\nka5\nIs this LLM explanation based on anything though? Can it cite an authoritative source, like a textbook equivalent, or a post aimed at Japanese language professionals (TV/book editors and such)?\nI don’t know if I’ve seen the ideas that “うちに implies a change or development\nover time\n”, or “emphasize that one state\ngradually\nled to another: → うちに” before.\nI’d consider うちに・間に essentially synonymous in this example. And if one is really searching for a nuance, that’d be that with うちに driving is a situation especially conductive of sleepiness. So if one would stop driving, that would also automatically prevent sleepiness.\nNewkidi\nHey, i hope everyone is doing well i just have a hard time  regarding うちに this applies for 間に as well i get everything with the adjective and noun usage but when it comes to verb I’m lost. apparently with ているうちに can mean while your doing something something happens. cool i got that 私が寝てるうち・間に弟が来た but in cases like this  彼と毎日会ううちに、だんだんと彼のことが好きになってきた。confuses 