      "sourceText": "ことになる (JLPT N3) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN3 Lesson 3: 10/22\n事\n(\nこと\n)\nになる\nIt will end up being, It is the result of, It has been decided that\nThe \"something has been decided\" meaning only applies to verbs!\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nことになる\nIt will end up being, It is the result of, It has been decided that\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nVerb +\nこと\nになる\n［い］Adjective\n+\nこと\nになる\n［な］Adjective +\nな\n+\nこと\nになる\nDetails\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout ことになる\nEnglish\nJapanese\nことになる\nis an expression that combines the nominalizing use of こと (creating a noun-phrase), with the\ncase marking particle\nに\n, and the う - Verb なる 'to become'. This expression can be translated as 'to become (A)', but has the nuance that someone/thing caused (A) to become that way. Due to this, it may also be translated as 'it has been decided that (A)', or 'it will end up being (A)'.\nことになる\nis primarily used with verbs and\nい-Adjectives\nin their\nattributive form\n. It may also be used with\nな-Adjectives\nfollowed by な.\nThe 'it has been decided that (A)' meaning only applies to verbs, as only verbs will be enacted by someone/thing.\nFun Fact\nThis structure is a good example of why なる is often viewed as the intransitive form of\nする\n.\nする\nconveys that someone is performing an action (transitive), while なる implies that something is the result of an action (intransitive). This is why the (A)  phrase in\nことになる\nis usually determined to have been decided/performed by someone else, while still focusing on exactly 'what' (A) became.\nRelated\nExpand All\nにする\nTo decide on, To choose, To make something into, To view something as\nN5 Grammar\nBoth にする and ことになる relate to decisions but differ in agency. にする is used when the speaker actively chooses something, often in daily situations like deciding what to eat, similar to 'I'll go with X.' ことになる, on the other hand, expresses decisions made by others or by circumstance, like saying 'It's been decided that X.'\nことにする\nことにする\nTo decide on, Have decided to\nN3 Grammar\nThese expressions are similar in that both are used to express decisions. ことになる conveys that something has been decided or will happen due to external factors, e.g. 来月転勤することになった ('It's been decided that I'll be transferred next month'), while ことにする describes something the speaker has decided of their own power, e.g. 来月仕事を辞めることにした ('I've decided to quit my job next month').\nことになっている\nことになっている\nTo be expected to, To be scheduled to\nN2 Grammar\nThese are closely related patterns with a slight but clear nuance difference. ことになる expresses that something has been decided or will happen, often beyond the speaker's control, e.g. 転勤することになった ('It's been decided that I'll be transferred'), while ことになっている is typically used to refer to an established or habitual rule, e.g. 会議を行うことになっている ('It's been decided we'll hold a meeting').\nに決まっている\nにきまっている\nSurely, Bound to, Certainly, Be decided\nN2 Grammar\nThese patterns are related in that both center around the idea of something being decided. ことになる expresses that something has been decided or will happen, often beyond the speaker's control, e.g. 転勤することになった ('It's been decided that I'll be transferred'), while に決まっている expresses that something is decisively true, e.g. 勝つに決まっている ('They'll definitely win').\nようになる\nようになる\nTo reach the point that, To come to be that, To turn into\nN4 Grammar\nBoth ようになる and ことになる express something becoming a certain way, but with different nuances. ようになる describes a change in state, e.g. 話せるようになった ('I became able to speak.') ことになる, on the other hand, conveys a decision made, often outside the speaker's control, e.g. 辞めることになった ('It was decided that I/he/she would quit.').\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nどうしてこんな\n事\n(\nこと\n)\nになった\nの？\n意味\n(\nいみ\n)\nがわからないよ。\nWhy\ndid it end up\nlike this? I don't get it.\n大変\n(\nたいへん\n)\nな\n事\n(\nこと\n)\nになった\nぞ。\n何\n(\nなに\n)\nか\n作戦\n(\nさくせん\n)\nを\n考\n(\nかんが\n)\nえなければ。\nWe\nended up\ngetting in trouble. We have to figure out a plan.\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nが\n会議\n(\nかいぎ\n)\nに\n出\n(\nで\n)\nる\n事\n(\nこと\n)\nになった\n。\nIt was decided\nthat I will attend the meeting.\n連絡\n(\nれんらく\n)\nがなければ、\n僕\n(\nぼく\n)\nが\n仕事\n(\nしごと\n)\nに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nく\n事\n(\nこと\n)\nになります\n。\nIf we do not hear anything, I\nwill end up\ngoing to work.\n困\n(\nこま\n)\nった\n事\n(\nこと\n)\nになった\nな。もう\n電池\n(\nでんち\n)\nが\n切\n(\nき\n)\nれそうだ。\nI\nended up\nin a jam. It seems that my battery is about to die.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nHow do ことになる and ようになる differ?\nJStackExchange\nUsing 「なる」 and 「する」 with verbs\nTae Kim\nことになる\nE-Japanese\nことになる/する\nMaggieSensei\nOffline\n[AIAIJ] An Integrated Approach to Intermediate Japanese\nPage 15\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 202\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 131\nTobira\nPage 172\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nことになる – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (1 in total)\nTalos\nWhile showing this grammar point to my Japanese-native tutor, she claimed that several of the sentences didn’t make sense to her.\n明日が雨ならば、１週間降り続くことになる\nAfter I explained the meaning in English she said it would have made more sense ending in 続いたことになる 。\nインフルエンザにかかってしまったので、今日の大会が出れないことになった\nShe explained that 出れない normally only takes a human subject, so I should replace が with に to get  今日の大会に出れない\nFinally, my tutor also felt that this grammar point covered two very different usages and suggested that the “was decided” meaning acts as a single grammatical unit\nことになる\nand tends to be used in the context of a business doing the deciding. (Your example sentences might support this depending on context.)\nFrom example sentences with\ndecided\nmeaning\nMust return to America (from a business trip?)\nGot questions about ことになる?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
