      "sourceText": "せいで (JLPT N3) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN3 Lesson 10: 5/20\nせいで\nBecause of, Due to, Blame\nCan express blame or fault, で can be omitted at the end of a sentence\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nせいで\nBecause of, Due to, Blame\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb +\nせい\nで\n［い］Adjective\n+\nせい\nで\n［な］Adjective +\nな\n+\nせい\nで\nNoun +\nの\n+\nせい\nで\nDetails\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout せいで\nEnglish\nJapanese\nAlthough written almost exclusively in hiragana, せい is a\nnoun\nthat may be better understood from its kanji,\n所為\n(\nせい\n)\n, meaning 'doings'. せい is used to convey negative results that occurred due to the behavior or actions of another person/thing. Because of this, common translations of せい are 'fault', 'due to', or 'because of'.\nAs せい is a noun, it may be used after the\nattributive form\nof any other word. The\ncase marking particle\nで will also regularly be seen after せい, especially when conveying the (B) result that occurred 'with' or 'by means of' those 'doings'.\nFun Fact\nThe 'doings' that\n所為\n(\nせい\n)\nrepresents is similar to the negative 'doings' that is used in English to place blame. This can be seen in phrases such as 'it was the weather's doings', or 'ah, so the missing cookies were your doings!'\nRelated\nExpand All\nおかげで\nおかげで\nThanks to, Because of\nN3 Grammar\nBoth mean 'because of', but with opposite tone. おかげで expresses a thankful cause, e.g. 先生のおかげで合格できた ('Thanks to my teacher, I passed.') せいで places blame for something negative, e.g. 雨のせいで試合が中止になった ('The game was canceled because of the rain.')\nために\nために\nDue to, Because of, As a result of\nN3 Grammar\nために and せいで can both mean 'due to (A)', but with a difference in tone. せいで almost always implies a negative or undesirable result. ために can be used with either positive or negative nuance. Also, ために can mean 'for the purpose of (A)', which せいで cannot.\nそのため(に)\nそのために\nFor that reason, Because of that, To that end\nN3 Grammar\nBoth せいで and そのため(に) show cause and effect, but せいで follows a word or phrase and implies a negative result or blame, e.g. 台風のせいで試合が中止になった ('The game was canceled because of the typhoon'), while そのため(に) is used at the beginning of the sentence and neutrally shows a logical result of whatever was previously stated. そのため(に) can also express 'for the purpose of (A)', which is a nuance せいで cannot have.\nにつき\nにつき\nDue to, On account of, Each or every\nN2 Grammar\nBoth せいで and につき express cause, but with different nuance and tone. せいで is more colloquial and typically introduces a cause for a negative result, e.g. 雨のせいで試合が中止になった ('The game was canceled because of the rain.'). につき states a reason more neutrally and is often used in formal written announcements, e.g. 工事中につき通行止め ('Closed to traffic due to construction.').\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nトムは\n暑\n(\nあつ\n)\nさ\nのせいで\n気\n(\nき\n)\nを\n失\n(\nうしな\n)\nった。\nTom passed out\nbecause of\nthe heat.\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\nのせい\nでもないと\n思\n(\nおも\n)\nいます。\nI don't think it's\nbecause of\nanyone. (\nno one to blame\n)\n湿気\n(\nしっけ\n)\nのせいで\nカビがひどい。\nThe mold is out of control\nbecause of\nthe humidity.\nストレス\nのせいで\n病気\n(\nびょうき\n)\nになってしまった。\nI became ill\ndue to\nstress.\n物忘\n(\nものわす\n)\nれがひどいのは、たぶん\n年\n(\nとし\n)\nのせい\nです。\nYou are quite forgetful probably\nbecause of\nyour age.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nExpressing that something is to blame or is the cause of\nJ-Nihongo\nThe difference between おかげで and せいで\n日本語の森\nOffline\n[AIAIJ] An Integrated Approach to Intermediate Japanese\nPage 228\nTobira\nPage 195\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nせいで – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (4 in total)\nmrnoone\n@narfi\nHey\nIt is not allowed here, because で in せいで is てform of a noun/なadjective, and the てform is used to connect parts of the sentence (clauses). When せい is used at the end of the sentence (so-called predicate position), there is no other part that will be joined so てform is not needed and as with any other noun/なadjective だ・です・and so on follow.\nI will fix the structure section so it will be more clear\nCheers,\nI hope it helps!\nmor1\nHey!\nReading I’ve come upon the “せいか”-grammar!\nI tried looking it up and found this grammar structure instead, but it seems like it kinda means the same - maybe せいか being a bit less direct?\nIs there a grammar chapter I’ve overlooked or should it even be added here?\nGreetings\nJGaynor\nSorry to ping you over something you probably know by now, but I thought it’d be good for people who stumble across your comment.\nIf you are sure of the reason, use せい\nIf you are unsure of the reason (but still think it), use せいか\nGot questions about せいで?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
