      "sourceText": "ぎみ (JLPT N3) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN3 Lesson 10: 8/20\n気味\n(\nぎみ\n)\nLooking, Feeling a little, Touch of, Tending to, Slightly\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nぎみ\nLooking, Feeling a little, Touch of, Tending to, Slightly\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb［stem］\n+\n気味\n(\nぎみ\n)\nNoun +\n気味\n(\nぎみ\n)\nDetails\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout ぎみ\nEnglish\nJapanese\nComing from the\nnoun\n気味\n(\nきみ\n)\nmeaning 'sensation',\nぎみ\nis a\nsuffix\nthat is used to convey weak signs of (A).\nぎみ\nmay be used with nouns, or the\nconjunctive form\nof verbs. Often,\nぎみ\nis translated as 'looking (A)', 'a touch of (A)', 'feeling a little (A)', or 'slightly (A)'.\nぎみ\nrefers to the senses of the person who is experiencing them, and therefore may be used to refer to feelings about other people/things, or feelings about oneself.\nAs\nぎみ\nexpresses 'weak signs of (A)', it is often used to soften a statement, so that the speaker does not appear overconfident.\nCaution\nAlthough it comes from\n気味\n(\nきみ\n)\n,\nぎみ\nwill always be said with a ぎ, and not a き. This is a sound change used to distinguish the noun and the\nsuffix\n.\nRelated\nExpand All\nげ\nげ\nSeemingly, Giving appearance of, [Expresses emotions or feelings]\nN2 Grammar\nぎみ (often written in kanji as 気味) and げ both describe observable states, but differ in focus. ぎみ suggests a slight or subtle tendency, often related to physical or emotional states, e.g. 疲れ気味 ('a bit tired') or 風邪気味 ('have a bit of a cold'). げ expresses perceived emotions of others, e.g. 寂しげな顔 ('a lonely-looking face').\nっぽい\n-ish, -like, Characteristic of, Typical of, Tendency to\nN3 Grammar\nBoth ぎみ (often written in kanji as 気味) and っぽい describe tendencies or characteristics, but with different nuance. ぎみ suggests a slight or subtle tendency, often related to physical or emotional states, e.g. 疲れ気味 ('a bit tired') or 風邪気味 ('have a bit of a cold'). っぽい implies a more innate trait that gives the appearance or impression of something, e.g. 子どもっぽい ('childish') or 忘れっぽい ('forgetful').\n的\nてき\n~ly・~like・~al, Typical\nN3 Grammar\n的 and ぎみ (気味) are similar in that both can be attached to nouns to turn them into adjectives, but they differ in nuance and usage. 的 has a wider range of uses, similar to English endings like '-al', '-ly', etc., e.g. 感情 'emotion' vs. 感情的 'emotional'. In contrast, ぎみ has the specific meaning of 'slightly (A)' or 'showing tendencies of (A)', and typically expresses a subjective judgment about a condition, e.g. 疲れ気味 'a bit tired'.\nような気がする\nようなきがする\nHave a feeling that\nN3 Grammar\nBoth ような気がする and ぎみ (from 気味) use 気 expressions to convey subtle states or impressions, but with different nuance and usage. ような気がする follows a phrase to express a subjective hunch like 'Have a feeling that (A)', e.g. 来るような気がする 'I have a feeling they'll come.' ぎみ attaches to nouns to express a slight tendency or hint of something observable, often (but not always) negative, e.g. 疲れ気味だ 'I'm feeling a bit tired.'\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n彼女\n(\nかのじょ\n)\nは\n風邪\n(\nかぜ\n)\n気味\n(\nぎみ\n)\nだと\n言\n(\nい\n)\nった。\nShe said she was\nfeeling a little\nsick.\n最近\n(\nさいきん\n)\n、\n彼女\n(\nかのじょ\n)\nは\n太\n(\nふと\n)\nり\n気味\n(\nぎみ\n)\nらしい。\nI heard that lately, she is\na bit plump\n.\n貧血\n(\nひんけつ\n)\n気味\n(\nぎみ\n)\nなので、\n採血\n(\nさいけつ\n)\nはやめておきましょう。\nSince you are\nlooking somewhat\nanemic, let's not draw any blood.\n暗\n(\nくら\n)\nいニュースばかりで\n食傷\n(\nしょくしょう\n)\n気味\n(\nぎみ\n)\nだ。\nI\nfeel\nsick listening to nothing but negative news.\n台風\n(\nたいふう\n)\nのせいで\n天気\n(\nてんき\n)\nが\n荒\n(\nあ\n)\nれ\n気味\n(\nぎみ\n)\nだ。\nThe weather is\na bit stormy\ndue to the typhoon.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nHow to use がち and ぎみ\nMaggie Sensei\nOffline\nThere are no Offline resources listed for 「ぎみ」.\nYou can\nsubmit a resource for review\n. Resources are constantly updated, come back later to see new additions!\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nぎみ – Grammar Discussion\nThere's currently no discussion for ぎみ\nAsk questions and learn together with other Bunpro users!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
