      "sourceText": "っぽい (JLPT N3) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN3 Lesson 10: 9/20\nっぽい\n-ish, -like, Characteristic of, Typical of, Tendency to\nOften has negative connotation.\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nっぽい\n-ish, -like, Characteristic of, Typical of, Tendency to\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nVerb［stem］\n+\nっぽい\n［い］Adjective［\nい\n］\n+\nっぽい\n［な］Adjective\n+\nっぽい\nNoun +\nっぽい\nDetails\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout っぽい\nEnglish\nJapanese\nっぽい\n(occasionally also seen as ぽい) is a\nsuffix\nthat may be attached to many different types of words, in order to create new\nい-Adjectives\n. In all cases,\nっぽい\nindicates something that is 'exhibiting characteristics of (A)'. This is most often in reference to the way (A) is acting, or the way (A) looks. In English,\nっぽい\nmay be translated as '(A)ish', '(A)like', or 'tendency to (A)'.\nっぽい\nquite often carries a negative connotation.\nAs\nっぽい\ncreates\nい-Adjectives\nfrom other words, it may be used with the\nconjunctive form\nof verbs, or the\n語幹\n(\nごかん\n)\n(stem form) of any other word.\nCaution\nAs\nっぽい\ncreates new\nい-Adjectives\n,\nっぽい\nitself may be conjugated in exactly the same way as regular\nい-Adjectives\n.\nCaution\nThe fundamental meaning of\nっぽい\nwill change depending on what type of word it is being used with. The general rules are as follows:\nVerb +\nっぽい\n- To have a tendency to do (A).\nAdjective +\nっぽい\n- To exhibit all the signs of being (A).\nNoun +\nっぽい\n- To obviously not be (A), although show signs of being (A).\nFrom this, we can see that\nっぽい\nis generally based on the opinion of the speaker, rather than the innate qualities of what is being observed. Thus, with verbs, adjectives, and nouns, the speaker feels like (A) is in excess, compared to what would be expected. This 'being in excess of what is normal' in the opinion of the speaker is what creates the negative connotation.\nRelated\nExpand All\nがち\nApt to do, Tend to do, Prone to do\nN3 Grammar\nBoth がち and っぽい describe tendencies or characteristic qualities, but differ in nuance. がち is neutral and used for tendencies that happen often, e.g. つい忘れがちだ ('I tend to forget.'). っぽい expresses an inherent quality or the appearance of something, similar to the English '~ish', e.g. 子供っぽい性格 ('a childish personality').\nげ\nげ\nSeemingly, Giving appearance of, [Expresses emotions or feelings]\nN2 Grammar\nBoth っぽい and げ describe perceived qualities, but differ in nuance. っぽい refers to innate behaviors or traits that give a certain impression. For example, 子どもっぽい ('childish') or 怒りっぽい ('quick to anger'). In contrast, げ highlights perceived emotions in others. For example, 寂しげな顔 ('a lonely-looking face').\nふりをする\nふりをする\nTo pretend, To act as if\nN3 Grammar\nBoth ふりをする and っぽい describe imitation or resemblance, but with a key difference in nuance. ふりをする implies pretending to be or do something one is not, as in 知ったふりをする ('pretend to know'). In contrast, っぽい conveys a natural resemblance or tendency, often unconscious or inherent, as in 子供っぽい ('childish').\nぎみ\nぎみ\nLooking, Feeling a little, Touch of, Tending to, Slightly\nN3 Grammar\nBoth ぎみ (often written in kanji as 気味) and っぽい describe tendencies or characteristics, but with different nuance. ぎみ suggests a slight or subtle tendency, often related to physical or emotional states, e.g. 疲れ気味 ('a bit tired') or 風邪気味 ('have a bit of a cold'). っぽい implies a more innate trait that gives the appearance or impression of something, e.g. 子どもっぽい ('childish') or 忘れっぽい ('forgetful').\nらしい ②\ntypical of, -ish・like, appropriate for, becoming of, befitting\nN4 Grammar\nっぽい and らしい② both express resemblance. っぽい is casual, like '-ish' in English, e.g. 子どもっぽい 'childish' (usually with a negative nuance). らしい② implies that someone or something embodies expected traits, e.g. 男らしい 'manly' with a nuance of fulfilling cultural expectations of what a man should be.\n的\nてき\n~ly・~like・~al, Typical\nN3 Grammar\n的 and っぽい are similar in that both can be attached to nouns to turn them into adjectives (or adverbs, in the forms 的に and っぽく). They differ grammatically (的 functions as a な-adjective, while っぽい is an い-adjective), and in nuance. 的 has a wider range of uses, similar to English endings like '-al', '-ly', etc., e.g. 感情 'emotion' vs. 感情的 'emotional'. っぽい is narrower in use, and often carries a derogatory meaning, e.g. 子供っぽい 'childish'.\nらしい ①\nSeems like, Apparently, I heard\nN4 Grammar\nっぽい and らしい ① both describe being 'like' something, but with different nuances and formality. っぽい, similar to the English '-ish,' refers to having the characteristics of something without being that thing, and is very casual, e.g. 子どもっぽい態度 'childish behavior', e.g. from an adult. In contrast, らしい ① implies embodying a characteristic in a more fitting or expected way, e.g. 子どもらしい笑顔 'a smile just as you'd expect from a child'.\nそう\nLook like, Appear, Seem, Have a feeling that\nN4 Grammar\nBoth っぽい and そう express appearance or impression, but differ in nuance and usage. そう attaches to word stems and is neutral and often based on observation, e.g. 雨が降りそう ('It looks like it'll rain.'). っぽい is casual and attaches to nouns to mean '~ish' or '~like', e.g. 子供っぽい 'childish'. Unlike そう, it often carries a negative or demeaning tone.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n寅\n(\nとら\n)\nさんは\n惚\n(\nほ\n)\nれっぽく\n、\n飽\n(\nあ\n)\nきっぽい。\nTora-san has the\ntendency to fall in love\n, and also has the tendency to lose interest. (\n~ish\n)\nなんかこの\n牛乳\n(\nぎゅうにゅう\n)\nは\n水\n(\nみず\n)\nっぽい\nな。\nThis milk is somewhat\nwatery\n… (\n~ish\n)\n怒\n(\nおこ\n)\nりっぽい\n人\n(\nひと\n)\nはあまり\n好\n(\nす\n)\nかれない。\nPeople that have a\ntendency to get angry\nare not liked much. (\n~ish\n)\nトムは\n頭\n(\nあたま\n)\nいいけど、\n忘\n(\nわす\n)\nれっぽい\nよ。\nTom is smart, but he is\nforgetful\n. (\n~ish\n)\nこういう\n時\n(\nとき\n)\nは\n黒\n(\nくろ\n)\nっぽい\nスーツを\n着\n(\nき\n)\nるべきか？\nShould I wear a\nblackish\nsuit for this occasion?\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\n〜げ・〜がち・〜気味\nNihon5Kyoushi\nContrasting っぽい、らしい、みたい\nJapanese Stack Exchange\nSlang expression of similarity\nTae Kim\nOffline\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 267\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nっぽい – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (6 in total)\nKert\n彼の話は理屈っぽいから、あまり話をしたくはない。\nIsn’t は at the end of the sentence excessive? As I understand it’s shitai -> shitakunai, but there’s no は\nnekoyama\nしたくない is a direct way to say “I don’t want to do it”.\nしたくはない (this is the particle は) is a bit softer, “I don’t\nwant\nto do it, but…”. Implying that the person may still do it. They don’t like it, but perhaps it’s necessary or hard to avoid. It’s also a bit more considerate because it’s not as direct a rejection as outright saying “I don’t want to”.\nKert\nThanks for the explanation!\nIs there a bunpro grammar point on this? Maybe it’s after N3 somewhere?\nUPD: found it in N2 https://www.bunpro.jp/grammar_points/402\nGot questions about っぽい?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
