      "sourceText": "ものだから (JLPT N2) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN2 Lesson 8: 13/23\nものだから\nBecause, The reason is\nMore informal → もんだから/もんで, *ことだから ≠ ものだから\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nものだから\nBecause, The reason is\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb +\nもの\nだ\nから\n(1)\n+  Phrase\n［い］Adjective\n+\nもの\nだ\nから\n(1)\n+  Phrase\n［な］Adjective +\nな\n+\nもの\nだ\nから\n(1)\n+  Phrase\nNoun +\nな\n+\nもの\nだ\nから\n(1)\n+  Phrase\n(1)\nもので、もんで、もんだ\nから\nDetails\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout ものだから\nEnglish\nJapanese\nものだから\nis a strong phrase that is used when explaining something or giving an excuse for one's reasoning. This grammar structure can simply be thought of as an extension of から 'because', with the noun もの 'thing' having its usual role of emphasizing what is said before it. Common translations include 'because (A)', and 'the reason is (A)'.\nものだから\nmay appear directly after verbs and い-Adjectives, or な-Adjectives and nouns that are followed with な.\nOccasionally,\nものだから\nwill be abbreviated to\nもので\n, or\nもんで\n, without any change in the overall meaning. The change of だ to its conjunctive form で indicates that there is something coming after, despite the fact that what comes after may sometimes be omitted if it is obvious.\nFun-fact\nAlthough it is not a rule,\nものだから\nwill primarily be used when explaining one's actions, as a way of emphasizing that the situation of (A) specifically led to the speaker doing (B).\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nので\nBecause, So, Since, The reason being\nN5 Grammar\nBoth ので and ものだから give reasons or explanations, but with slightly different nuances. ので is normally more objective and direct, used to state that fact (A) is the reason for (B). ものだから, on the other hand, is more subjective, expressing personal or emotional reasons for the speaker's actions.\nから\nBecause, Since\nN5 Grammar\nから expresses a reason or cause, similar to 'because' or 'so' in English, with the structure (A) から (B). In contrast, ものだから is more emphatic or emotional, often used to explain or justify actions. Both can be used in subjective statements, where the speaker presents their perspective or judgment.\nRelated\nExpand All\nことだから\nことだから\nIt is exactly because\nN2 Grammar\nBoth give reasons, but differ in tone. ことだから emphasizes a reason that is obvious or characteristic, as in 田中さんのことだから大丈夫だろう (Since it’s Tanaka, it should be fine). ものだから is often used to explain or excuse, as in 熱があるものだから休みます (I have a fever, so I’ll take a day off).\nものですから・もので\nものですから\nBecause, My excuse is, My explanation is\nN2 Grammar\nAll mean because, but nuance differs. ものですから is the most polite. ものだから is plain and often explanatory. もので is slightly softer and less direct. For example: 遅れたのは事故にあったものですから (I was late because there was an accident).\nもの・もん\nBecause, Cause\nN3 Grammar\nThese two are closely related and use the same もの (with もん as a more casual variant) to express reason. もの・もん is often used at the end of a sentence in a casual or even childish tone to give a personal reason, e.g. 'because (A)'. ものだから is a slightly more formal expression, often used to explain or justify an action or situation in a softer, less direct way.\nものだ\nSupposed to, Should, That's the way it is\nN3 Grammar\nBoth expressions use the same ものだ, with the only difference being the presence or absence of から 'because/since'. Therefore, ものだ is used at the end of a sentence to express the idea of how something is or should be. In contrast, ものだから presents a reason or an explanation for something, and is often used when justifying a certain action or situation. ものだ can also be used to express obligation, while ものだから is not used this way.\nからこそ\nEmphasized because, Precisely because\nN3 Grammar\nからこそ and ものだから are similar in that both can be used in expressing a reason. They differ in that からこそ is used to specifically emphasize the reason, like saying 'it is precisely because of (A) that (B)', while ものだから is not as emphatic, and is generally used when providing an explanation or excuse for one's actions or a situation.\nなぜなら〜から\nなぜなら + から\nBecause, The reason is, That is because\nN3 Grammar\nThese phrases are related in that both can be used to express a reason, though they differ in tone, nuance, and usage. なぜなら〜から is more formal and objective, and is used at the beginning of a sentence to provide a reason for something previously said. In contrast, ものだから is used at the end of a phrase or sentence, and typically has a nuance of providing a justification or excuse for one's behavior or the situation.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n郊外\n(\nこうがい\n)\nに\n家\n(\nいえ\n)\nを\n買\n(\nか\n)\nった\nものだから\n、\n通勤\n(\nつうきん\n)\nに\n２\n(\nに\n)\n時間\n(\nじかん\n)\nもかかる。\nBecause\nI bought a house in the suburbs, the commute to work takes two hours.\n税関\n(\nぜいかん\n)\nに\n引\n(\nひ\n)\nっかかった\nものだから\n、\n遅\n(\nおそ\n)\nくなってしまいました。\nBecause\nI was stopped by the customs officer, I ended up being late.\n素人\n(\nしろうと\n)\nが\n制作\n(\nせいさく\n)\nした\nものだから\n、やっぱりすぐに\n壊\n(\nこわ\n)\nれてしまった。\nBecause\nit is something created by a novice, as expected, it broke right away.\n英語\n(\nえいご\n)\nが\n苦手\n(\nにがて\n)\nなものだから\n、\n海外\n(\nかいがい\n)\n旅行\n(\nりょこう\n)\nに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nくのは\n楽\n(\nたの\n)\nしくない。\nBecause\nI am bad at English, taking trips abroad isn't fun.\n墨\n(\nすみ\n)\nで\n黒\n(\nくろ\n)\nく\n塗\n(\nぬ\n)\nってしまった\nものだから\n、\n何\n(\nなん\n)\nて\n書\n(\nか\n)\nいてあったか\n分\n(\nわ\n)\nからない。\nBecause\nit is painted black with ink, I don't know what was written.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\n〜ものだから 〜もの What are the differences?\nJapanese StackExchange\n〜ものだから・もので VS 〜あまり\n日本語の森 [video]\nFurther breakdown and example sentences\nNihongo Master\nGrammar Notes and Breakdown\n日本語 NET\nものだから vs. ことだから\nRiki Nihongo Dayo [video]\n接続・意味・使い方・例文\nにほんごの里\nOffline\nThere are no Offline resources listed for 「ものだから」.\nYou can\nsubmit a resource for review\n. Resources are constantly updated, come back later to see new additions!\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nものだから – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (2 in total)\nmegatron0000\nThe reading section points to a 日本語の森 video that is actually private\nPushindawood\n@megatron0000 Thank you for letting us know! I have updated the link. Cheers!\nGot questions about ものだから?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
