      "sourceText": "たところで (JLPT N1) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN1 Lesson 1: 2/17\n～たところで\nEven if ~ cannot or will not, Even though ~ not\nThe た-form is generally not used in the clause following ところで\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nたところで\nEven if ~ cannot or will not, Even though ~ not\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb［た］\n+\nところで\n+Phrase［ない］\nDetails\nRegister\nStandard\nRare Kanji\n所\nAbout たところで\nEnglish\nJapanese\n～たところで\nis a grammar construction that combines the simple-past form of a verb, the noun ところ 'place', and the case-marking particle で.\nAs with most other grammar patterns that ところ is seen with, the focus is on the situation of the verb occurring.\n～たところで\nis often translated as 'even if (A), not (B)', or 'even if (A), it won't be (B)'.\nThis 'even if' nuance is created by the fact that ～たところで sentences will never have something in the past tense in the second half of the sentence, despite using the past tense before ところ. This is similar to the English 'even if (A) were to happen, (B) would still not occur'.\nAs can be seen by the examples above,\n～たところで\nwill convey things that are counter to what the speaker either wants to, or wishes would happen. Due to this, the (B) half of the sentence will almost always be something unfavorable.\nFun Fact\nDue to\n～たところで\nalways marking some kind of negative result in (B), (A) will often be preceded by words such as\n今\n(\nいま\n)\nさら, いくら, or どんなに. These usually just emphasize the frustration that the speaker feels about the inevitable result.\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nにせよ・にしろ\nにせよ\nEven if, Even though, No matter, Whether… or\nN2 Grammar\nBoth share an even if nuance. にせよ・にしろ asserts that the conclusion doesn’t change, as in 成功しないにせよ挑戦すべきだ (Even if you don’t succeed, you should try). たところで adds futility, as in 今さら謝ったところで遅い (Even if you apologize now, it’s too late).\nRelated\nExpand All\nたって\nEven if, Even though, No matter how\nN2 Grammar\nBoth translate as even if, but differ in nuance. たって is a casual equivalent of ても, as in 雨が降ったって行く (I’ll go even if it rains). たところで appears in negative or futile contexts, as in 頑張ったところで無駄だ (Even if you try hard, it’s useless).\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n徹夜\n(\nてつや\n)\nで\n勉強\n(\nべんきょう\n)\nしたところで\n、\n合格\n(\nごうかく\n)\nし\nない\n。\nEven if\nI studied all night, I\nwould not\npass.\n「いまごろ\n車\n(\nくるま\n)\nに\n乗\n(\nの\n)\nって\n行\n(\nい\n)\nったところで\n間\n(\nま\n)\nに\n合\n(\nあ\n)\nいようが\nない\n。」\nEven if\nyou went by car right now, you would\nnot\nto be on time.\n「\n謝\n(\nあやま\n)\nってみたところで\n、\n彼女\n(\nかのじょ\n)\nが\n許\n(\nゆる\n)\nしてくれるわけ\nじゃない\nけど。」\n'\nEven if\nI try to apologize, it\ndoesn't\nmean she will forgive me.'\n「たとえ\n失敗\n(\nしっぱい\n)\nしてしまったところで\n、\n問題\n(\nもんだい\n)\nは\nありません\n。」\n'\nEven if\nyou fail, there will\nnot\nbe a problem.'\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nが\n告白\n(\nこくはく\n)\nしたところで\n、\n彼女\n(\nかのじょ\n)\nは\n応\n(\nおう\n)\nじ\nない\nだろう。\nEven if\nI confessed my love, she\nwouldn't\nrespond.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nBreakdown and Examples\nJapaneseTest4You\nBreakdown and Grammar Notes\nJLPT 先生\nHow to use verb + ところ\nMaggie Sensei\nOffline\nThere are no Offline resources listed for 「たところで」.\nYou can\nsubmit a resource for review\n. Resources are constantly updated, come back later to see new additions!\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nたところで – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (2 in total)\nbrodric\nBunpro describes the grammar structure as describing a futile action (A) that will have no bearing over negative result (B), but I was left confused by the example sentence 「たとえ失敗してしまったところで、問題はありません。」. Am I supposed to assume there is some hidden context behind 「問題はありません。」? And does that 失敗してしまう is a futile attempt at changing that?\nwestinahh\nIn line with the previous comment, I am wondering why one of the quiz sentences is showing a positive result; surely not failing is a positive thing.\nGot questions about たところで?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
