      "sourceText": "とはいえ (JLPT N1) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN1 Lesson 2: 16/18\nとは\n言\n(\nい\n)\nえ\nThough, Although (I admit that), Be that as it may, Nonetheless\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nとはいえ\nThough, Although (I admit that), Be that as it may, Nonetheless\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb +\nと\nは\nいえ\n+ Phrase\n［い］Adjective\n+\nと\nは\nいえ\n+ Phrase\nNoun + （\nだ\n）+\nと\nは\nいえ\n+ Phrase\n［な］Adjective\n+ （\nだ\n）+\nと\nは\nいえ\n+ Phrase\nPhrase (A)。\nと\nは\nいえ\n+ Phrase (B)\nDetails\nRegister\nFormal\nAbout とはいえ\nEnglish\nJapanese\nとは\n言\n(\nい\n)\nえ\nis one of the standard grammar patterns in Japanese that makes use of the\nimperative form\nin order to strongly state a point. It will often be translated as 'although (A), (B) will not change', or 'be that as it may, (B)'. Literally, it is just making use of the case-marking particle と in addition to the\nadverbial particle\nは to present (A) as a quote that the speaker acknowledges, but may not actually accept.\nAs this structure begins with と, it may be used at the end of any standard statement.\nIn most situations, the way that\nとはいえ\nwill be used is like the English 'say that it is (A), that still doesn't change (B)'. (A) will generally be something that is true, or true as far as the speaker knows.\nRelated\nExpand All\nといえども\nEven (if), Although, Even though\nN1 Grammar\nThese are both formal/written-style expressions that have the same fundamental meaning of 'even if one says'. In practice, both are used as a literary way to say 'although’, even if there is no explicit intention of quoting something that was said, e.g. ここは\n治安がいい\n(\nといえども・とはいえ\n)\n油断してはいけない ('Even though it's safe here, you mustn't be careless.').\nにせよ・にしろ\nにせよ\nEven if, Even though, No matter, Whether… or\nN2 Grammar\nBoth show concession, but differ in nuance. にせよ・にしろ makes a strong assertion that something holds regardless, as in 結果が悪いにしろ挑戦するべきだ (Even if the result is bad, you should try). とはいえ concedes a point but softens with that said, as in 難しいとはいえ不可能ではない (While it may be difficult, it’s not impossible).\nにしろ～にしろ\nにしろ〜にしろ\nWhether… or\nN2 Grammar\nにしろ～にしろ lists alternatives where the outcome stays the same, as in 成功するにしろ失敗するにしろ挑戦する価値がある (Whether you succeed or fail, it’s worth trying). とはいえ acknowledges something but introduces a contrasting fact, as in 難しいとはいえ不可能ではない (That said, it’s not impossible).\nであれ〜であれ\nWhether or, Or, No matter which, In either case\nN1 Grammar\nとはいえ 'although (one could say)' specifically qualifies a statement by saying one thing might be true, but there is more to the matter, e.g. 日本人とはいえ、フランスで生まれ育った ('Though she may be Japanese, she was born and raised in France.'). であれ～であれ is specifically used when comparing multiple options, e.g. 日本人であれ外国人であれ、法律は守らないといけない ('No matter if you're Japanese or a foreigner, one must obey the law.').\nであれ\nEven if, Even, No matter\nN1 Grammar\nとはいえ and であれ are similar in that they both express a sort of contrast, but とはいえ 'although (one could say)' specifically qualifies a statement by saying one thing might be true, but there is more to the matter, e.g. 日本人とはいえ、フランスで生まれ育った ('Though she may be Japanese, she was born and raised in France.'). Though they can overlap in some cases, であれ 'even'/'no matter' is often used together with question words, e.g. 理由は何であれ 'no matter the reason', while とはいえ cannot be used this way.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n馬\n(\nうま\n)\nは\n肉\n(\nにく\n)\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべることができる。\nとは\n言\n(\nい\n)\nえ\n、\n普通\n(\nふつう\n)\nは\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべない。\nHorses are perfectly capable of eating meat.\nHaving said that\n, they generally do not eat it. (\nbe that as it may\n)\n犯人\n(\nはんにん\n)\nは\n逮捕\n(\nたいほ\n)\nされた\nとは\n言\n(\nい\n)\nえ\n有罪\n(\nゆうざい\n)\nと\n宣告\n(\nせんこく\n)\nされるまで\n時間\n(\nじかん\n)\nがかかるだろう。\nAlthough (I admit that)\nthe criminal has been caught, it will be some time before they are declared guilty. (\nbe that as it may\n)\n彼\n(\nかれ\n)\nは\n社長\n(\nしゃちょう\n)\nとは\n言\n(\nい\n)\nえ\n名前\n(\nなまえ\n)\nばかりだ。\n権限\n(\nけんげん\n)\nは\n取締役会\n(\nとりしまりやくかい\n)\nにある。\nAlthough\nhe is admittedly the company president, it is in name only. It is the board of directors that holds the power. (\nbe that as it may\n)\n今\n(\nいま\n)\nでは、パンデミックの\n進行状況\n(\nしんこうじょうきょう\n)\nは\n遅\n(\nおそ\n)\nい\nとは\n言\n(\nい\n)\nえ\n、まだ\n安心\n(\nあんしん\n)\nするわけにはいかない。\nAlthough (I admit that)\nthe progress of the pandemic is slow, we cannot afford to feel at ease just yet. (\nbe that as it may\n)\n彼女\n(\nかのじょ\n)\nがあなたにとって\n実\n(\nじつ\n)\nの\n母親\n(\nははおや\n)\nである\nとは\n言\n(\nい\n)\nえ\n、\n礼儀\n(\nれいぎ\n)\nを\n欠\n(\nか\n)\nくべきではない。\nAlthough\nshe is, for you, your real mother, you should not lack manners.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nBreakdown and examples\nJapaneseTest4You\nExamples with audio\nE-Japanese\nOffline\nA Dictionary of Advanced Japanese Grammar\nPage 664\nShin Kanzen Master N1 Bunpou\nPage 36\nSou Matome Bunpou N1\nPage 50\nTry N1\nPage 90\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nとはいえ – Grammar Discussion\nThere's currently no discussion for とはいえ\nAsk questions and learn together with other Bunpro users!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n