      "sourceText": "も (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 1: 4/12\nも\nAlso, Too, As well, Even, Either, Neither\nも's translation will change, depending on if the sentence is positive, or negative\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nも\nAlso, Too, As well, Even, Either, Neither\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nNoun +\nも\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nLinking Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout も\nも\ncan mean 'too/also', or 'even', or 'neither/either', depending on if the sentence is affirmative or negative. Multiple meanings like this are quite common for Japanese particles, as there is no need to use different words simply to agree with other statements in the sentence (unlike English).\nAs you can see here, English requires 'either/neither (when negative)', or 'too/also', but Japanese only requires\nも\n. You can think of it as simply meaning 'Whatever is true for (A), is also true for (B)'.\nも\nis part of many other compound grammar structures in which it keeps its original meaning, but shifts the nuance a little bit. Some of these include\nでも\n,\nにも～ない\n,\nとも\n. As we can see, it is frequently paired with other particles!\nAlthough this was a pairing of\nで\nand\nも\nas individual structures in the past, in modern Japanese, でも is considered its own word. This is most often translated simply as 'but' in English, despite the nuance sometimes being 'While (A), also (B)', which highlights more clearly the historical concept.\nTopics\nWhat Are You Talking About?\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about two 'particles'. は (pronounced わ ’wa’) and も.\n2m\nRelated\nExpand All\nすら\nEven\nN1 Grammar\nすら and も are fundamentally similar in that both can serve to add emphasis. In cases like 子どもですらできる 'even a child can do it' 子どもでもできる (same meaning) they are essentially interchangeable except that すら is more formal and more emphatic. In examples where も is simply serving to add additional information, e.g. 田中さんも来た ('Mr. Tanaka also came.'), も could not be replaced with すら (unless there is some reason to believe that Tanaka coming would be an extremely rare or unexpected happening).\nと\nAnd\nN5 Grammar\nも and と are two different particles with different meanings. も means 'also' or 'too' and is used to add information, like in 田中さんも来ました ('Tanaka came, too'). と can be used to connect nouns like 'and' (バナナとりんご = 'a banana and an apple') or to mean 'with' (友だちと遊ぶ = 'to play with a friend').\nでもある\nBe also, It also is\nN3 Grammar\nでもある and も can both express 'also' (the も of でもある is the same as the particle も), but there is a key difference to note. AもB means '(A) is also (B)', while Aでもある means 'it is also (A)'. For example, compare 田中さんは学生です。鈴木さんも学生です ('Tanaka is a student. Suzuki is also a student') vs. 田中さんは学生です。日本人でもあります ('Tanaka is a student. He is also Japanese').\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nあなた\nも\nトム\nです\n。\nYou are\nalso\nTom.\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nも\n。\nMe\ntoo\n.\n彼\n(\nかれ\n)\nも\n先生\n(\nせんせい\n)\nです\n。\nHe is\nalso\na teacher.\n服\n(\nふく\n)\nも\n古\n(\nふる\n)\nい\n。\nThe clothes,\ntoo\n, are old.\n君\n(\nきみ\n)\nも\n、\n忙\n(\nいそが\n)\nし\nい\n。\nYou are\nalso\nbusy.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nThe 「も」 inclusive topic particle\nTae Kim\nOffline\nA Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 247\nGenki I 1st Edition\nPage 37\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 65\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 62\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 35\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 14 [CH 1]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nも – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (16 in total)\nMarcus_Aseth\nRegarding the sentence “車は速い、でも危ない” ,  I went ahead and did some more digging:\nI went and checked “A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar”, page 111 (\nでも\n).\nThe description of this grammar point is \"\nThe te-form of desu + mo “even”\n, example sentences:\n先生でも間違う・間違います。\nEven a teacher makes mistakes.\n私は難しい仕事でもする・します。\nI will even do a difficult job.\nここからでも富士山が見える・見えます。\nEven from here you can see Mt. Fuji.\nNotice that in all 9 example sentence this\nでも\nalways follow a noun, or a particle as in the case of\nからでも\n, which makes sense because if we wanted to use this “\neven\n” with an i-adjective it would turn into\nても\n, for example:\n安くても美味しい。\nEven if it’s cheap, it’s tasty.\nSo let’s go back to the sentence 車\nは速い、でも危ない\n.\nIf you really want to force this\nでも\nmeaning on it would be\n車は速くても危ない。<...\nizubizu\nhello , I am new to Bunpro\nI want to ask about the very first exampler- 私も先生です Does it mean -“Me as well (as you), I am a teacher” , or \" I am a teacher as well as other things\"\nka5\nWelcome to the forums!\nJapanese particles are post-positional, meaning they apply to the word that’s right before the particle, in this case to 私.\nSo the meaning is the first one: “me as well as (you/somebody else), I’m a teacher”.\nTo construct the other meaning “I am a teacher as well as other things” you’ll need to put も after 先生 in one way or another. But that requires some grammar you learn a little later. For example 「私は先生もやっています」“I also work as (do the job of) a teacher”.\nGot questions about も?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
