      "sourceText": "る-Verb (Negative) (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 2: 8/13\nる-Verb (Negative)\nWill/Does/Do (not)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nる-Verb (Negative)\nWill/Does/Do (not)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nExamples:\n​​\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nる\n+\nない\n​​\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nる\n+\nない\n寝\n(\nね\n)\nる\n+\nない\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nVerb\nWord Type\nAuxiliary Verb\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout る-Verb (Negative)\nThe negative form of\nる-Verbs\nare conjugated based on the same set of rules used for other forms of\nる-Verb\nconjugation (removal of る, followed by the addition of something else). In the case of negation, the additional component will be\nない\n(casual), or\nません\n(polite).\nWhile the common translation of\nない\nand\nません\nis simply 'not', they actually have several possible nuances. These nuances are 'don't' (usually not), 'won't' (intentionally not), or 'can't' (unintentionally not).\nBoth\nない\nand\nません\nare\nauxiliary verbs\n(\nません\nis a conjugation of ます), meaning that they have their own set of conjugation rules.\nA semi-polite form may also be used. This is constructed by using ない, and then simply adding です. です is used to mark politeness here, and ないです sounds less formal and stiff than ません.\nCaution\nThe\nない\nattached to make the\nnegative form of adjectives\nis a different word, and therefore follows different conjugation rules. When attached to adjectives\nない\nitself is an\nい-Adjective\n, and not an\nauxiliary verb\n.\nPractice る-Verb (Negative) in\nKaijugation\nThe only conjugation game with collateral damage!\nPlay!\nTopics\nI Won't Be Doing That! (Politely)\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn how to take auxiliary verbs and attach them to regular verbs.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nじゃない\nIs not, Isn't\nN5 Grammar\nThe negative form of a る-verb indicates 'does not' or 'will not' do something (e.g., 食べない = 'I don't eat' or 'I won't eat'). In contrast, じゃない (a casual form of ではない, the negative of だ) negates nouns and な-adjectives (e.g., 元気じゃない = 'not feeling good'). While both express negation, they apply to different types of words.\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nう-Verb (Past)\nう - Verbs (Past tense)\nN5 Grammar\nThe past form of a verb expresses completed actions or past states, while the negative form expresses actions or states that do not occur. る-Verbs form their negative by changing る to ない (e.g., 食べる → 食べない), whereas う-Verbs form the past tense differently (e.g., 買う → 買った). Remember, not all verbs ending in る are る-Verbs, for example, 帰る ('to return') is an う-verb.\nRelated\nExpand All\nる-Verb (Dictionary)\nる + ます (Ichidan Verbs)\nN5 Grammar\nThe dictionary form is the base form of the verb, used for habitual or future actions. The negative form expresses that an action doesn't happen or a state doesn't exist. For る-Verbs, the negative is formed by changing る to ない (e.g., 食べる → 食べない). う-Verbs have different conjugation patterns. Be careful: not all verbs ending in る are る-Verbs—for example, 帰る ('to return') is actually an う-Verb.\nVerb + て\n～て (Conjunction)\nAnd, Then (Linking events)\nN5 Grammar\nThe negative form of a る-verb expresses that someone does not or will not perform an action (e.g., 食べない = 'I do not eat' or 'I will not eat'). The Verb + て form, on the other hand, connects verbs with other sentence elements, expressing sequences, requests, or permission. For example, 食べて can mean 'Eat, please' in a casual request.\nる-Verb (Negative-Past)\nる - Verbs (Negative-past tense)\nN5 Grammar\nThese forms express the negative ('does not' or 'will not') of る-Verbs, with a difference in tense. The る-Verb (Negative) expresses a lack of future or habitual action (e.g., 食べない = 'I don't eat' or 'I won't eat'). The る-Verb (Negative-Past) expresses a lack of past action (e.g., 食べなかった = 'I didn't eat'). Both are plain forms used in casual speech or other grammatical patterns.\nVerbs (Non-past)\nVerb (Non-Past)\n(Non-past)\nN5 Grammar\nThe non-past (or dictionary) form is the base form of a verb, used to express habitual or future actions (e.g., 食べる = 'I eat' or 'I will eat'). To express the negative, use the negative form. For most える/いる-Verbs, change the final る to ない (e.g., 食べない = 'I do not eat' or 'I will not eat'). Both the non-past and negative forms are plain forms used in casual conversation or other grammar patterns.\nう-Verb (Dictionary)\nう + ます (Godan Verbs)\nN5 Grammar\nThe dictionary form is the base form of the verb, used for habitual or future actions. The negative form expresses that an action doesn't happen or a state doesn't exist. For る-Verbs, the negative is formed by changing る to ない (e.g., 食べる → 食べない). う-Verbs have different conjugation patterns. Be careful: not all verbs ending in る are る-Verbs—for example, 帰る ('to return') is actually an う-Verb.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n寿司\n(\nすし\n)\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべない\n。\n寿司\n(\nすし\n)\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nない\nです\n。\n寿司\n(\nすし\n)\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべません\n。\nI\ndon't eat\nsushi.\nテレビ\nを\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nない\n。\nテレビ\nを\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nないです\n。\nテレビ\nを\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nません\n。\nI\ndon't watch\nTV.\nお\n金\n(\nかね\n)\nを\n借\n(\nか\n)\nりない\n。\nお\n金\n(\nかね\n)\nを\n借\n(\nか\n)\nりない\nです\n。\nお\n金\n(\nかね\n)\nを\n借\n(\nか\n)\nりません\n。\nI\nwill not borrow\nmoney.\nパン\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべない\n。\nパン\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべないです\n。\nパン\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべません\n。\nI\ndon't eat\nbread.\nホラー\n映画\n(\nえいが\n)\nは\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nない\n。\nホラー\n映画\n(\nえいが\n)\nは\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nない\nです\n。\nホラー\n映画\n(\nえいが\n)\nは\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nません\n。\nI\ndon't watch\nhorror movies.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nAeron Buchanan's Japanese Verb Chart\nWikipedia Commons\nInformal Past Negative form of Verbs\nJapanese Ammo\nJapanese verb conjugation\nKanji Link\nNegation of masu verbs ません\nJapanese Ammo\nNegative Verbs\nTae Kim\nう-Verbs\nImabi 2\nる-Verbs\nImabi\nOffline\nGenki I 2nd Edition\n88 & 190\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 47\nTry N5\nPage 20\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 110 [CH 17]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nる-Verb (Negative) – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (31 in total)\nPablunpro\nHi!\nIf you read the grammar point carefully, it always speaks about -ない (casual) and -ません (polite). Actually, -ないです (another possibility for polite, perfectly explained by @nekoyama here) is not mentioned anywhere in the grammar point but only in the usage box in the upper left.\nFurther to this, the several nuances referred to are applicable\nto the negative form as such\n, irrespective of the level of language. As usual, it all boils down to context.\n(The example sentences are unfortunate, though, as one is casual and the other polite so that it is easy to imply that\ncasual = unintentional\nand\npolite = intentional\n, which is not the case. I guess these are the source of the misunderstanding.)\nHTH!\nLederhosen42\nHere to say that I also found this explanation and the examples unhelpful and potentially misleading. I still have no idea what dictates whether a negative form of a verb is intentional or not, but I almost got tricked into thinking that somehow polite/casual form might be a factor even though that didn’t make much sense to me.\nI tried looking at a few of the additional resource pages linked at the bottom and didn’t find any mention at all of what indicates intentional/unintentional. Maybe it’s mentioned in one of the videos that were linked, but I prefer reading/scanning over waiting through a 15+ minute video to see if my question is even answered. A citation might be useful here.\nEven just the addition of “Whether a negative form verb is interpreted as intentional or not is entirely dependent on context not covered here,” would be helpful in stopping folks from trying to make meaning from the two example sentences that isn’t there.\nKingkanjithemighty\nI just went through this learning point, first time learning about it, and I was likewise very confused and almost came away thinking that the politeness register (-nai, -naidesu, -masen) has an impact on the distinction between “don’t vs won’t vs can’t”.\nThis really should be rewritten to clarify that the politeness register has no impact on the meaning.\nGot questions about る-Verb (Negative)?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
      "checkedAt": "14 October 2026"
