      "sourceText": "う-Verb (Negative) (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 2: 9/13\nう-Verb (Negative)\nWill/Does/Do (not)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nう-Verb (Negative)\nWill/Does/Do (not)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nExamples:\n座\n(\nすわ\n)\nる\n+\nら\nない\n歌\n(\nうた\n)\nう\n+\nわ\nない\n歩\n(\nある\n)\nく\n+\nか\nない\n話\n(\nはな\n)\nす\n+\nさ\nない\n打\n(\nう\n)\nつ\n+\nた\nない\n死\n(\nし\n)\nぬ\n+\nな\nない\n飛\n(\nと\n)\nぶ\n+\nば\nない\n休\n(\nやす\n)\nむ\n+\nま\nない\n泳\n(\nおよ\n)\nぐ\n+\nが\nない\nExceptions:\nある ￫\nない\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nVerb\nWord Type\nAuxiliary Verb\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout う-Verb (Negative)\nThe negative form of\nう-Verbs\nare conjugated based on the same set of rules used for other forms of\nう-Verb\nconjugation (changing the last kana, followed by the addition of something else). In the case of negation, the additional component will be\nない\n(casual), or\nません\n(polite).\nAs can be seen here, the kana change differs depending on whether\nない\nor\nません\nis being used. If\nない\nwill be used, the last kana will always change from the kana ending with a う sound, to the kana ending with an あ sound within the same row. If\nません\nwill be used, the change will be from the う sound kana to the い sound kana. The only exception to this rule are verbs that end in the kana う. Instead of changing to あ (the あ sound kana in the same row), う will become わ, when used with\nない\n.\nWhile the common translation of\nない\nand\nません\nis simply 'not', they actually have several possible nuances. These nuances are 'don't' (usually not), 'won't' (intentionally not), or 'can't' (unintentionally not).\nBoth\nない\nand\nません\nare\nauxiliary verbs\n(ません is a conjugation of ます), meaning that they have their own set of conjugation rules.\nA semi-polite form may also be used. This is constructed by using ない, and then simply adding です. です is used to mark politeness here, and ないです sounds less formal and stiff than ません.\nCaution\nThe\nない\nattached to make the\nnegative form of adjectives\nis a different word, and therefore follows different conjugation rules. The\nない\nattached to adjectives is an\nい-Adjective\nitself, and not an\nauxiliary verb\n.\nPractice う-Verb (Negative) in\nKaijugation\nThe only conjugation game with collateral damage!\nPlay!\nTopics\nI Won't Be Doing That! (Politely)\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn how to take auxiliary verbs and attach them to regular verbs.\n3m\nRelated\nExpand All\nVerbs (Non-past)\nVerb (Non-Past)\n(Non-past)\nN5 Grammar\nThe non-past or dictionary form is the base form of a verb, used to express habitual or future actions To express the negative, change the final kana and add ない (e.g., 買わない = 'I do not buy' or 'I will not buy'). Both the non-past and negative forms are plain forms used in casual conversation or other grammar patterns.\nう-Verb (Negative-Past)\nう - Verbs (Negative-past tense)\nN5 Grammar\nThese forms express the negative ('does not' or 'will not') of う-Verbs, with a difference in tense. The う-Verb (Negative) expresses a lack of future or habitual action (e.g., 買わない = 'I don't buy' or 'I won't buy'). The う-Verb (Negative-Past) expresses a lack of past action (e.g., 買わなかった = 'I didn't buy'). Both are plain forms used in casual speech or other grammatical patterns.\nMisc.\nExpand All\nじゃない\nIs not, Isn't\nN5 Grammar\nThe negative form of an う-verb indicates 'does not' or 'will not' perform an action (e.g., 買わない = 'I don't buy' or 'I won't buy'). On the other hand, じゃない (casual form of ではない, the negative of だ) negates nouns and な-adjectives (e.g., 元気じゃない = 'not feeling good'). Both express negation, but each applies to different word types.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nお\n金\n(\nかね\n)\nは\nない\n。\nお\n金\n(\nかね\n)\nは\nない\nです\n。\nお\n金\n(\nかね\n)\nは\nあり\nません\n。\nI\ndon't have\nmoney.\n車\n(\nくるま\n)\nは\n持\n(\nも\n)\nたない\n。\n車\n(\nくるま\n)\nは\n持\n(\nも\n)\nたない\nです\n。\n車\n(\nくるま\n)\nは\n持\n(\nも\n)\nちません\n。\nI\nwon't have\na car.\n木\n(\nき\n)\nは\n歩\n(\nある\n)\nかない\n。\n木\n(\nき\n)\nは\n歩\n(\nある\n)\nかない\nです\n。\n木\n(\nき\n)\nは\n歩\n(\nある\n)\nきません\n。\nTrees\ndo not walk\n.\nトミー\nは\n話\n(\nはな\n)\nさない\n。\nトミー\nは\n話\n(\nはな\n)\nさない\nです\n。\nトミー\nは\n話\n(\nはな\n)\nしません\n。\nTommy\ndoes not talk\n.\nボール\nを\n打\n(\nう\n)\nたない\n。\nボール\nを\n打\n(\nう\n)\nた\nない\nです\n。\nボール\nを\n打\n(\nう\n)\nち\nません\n。\nto\nnot hit\na ball\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nAeron Buchanan's Japanese Verb Chart\nWikipedia Commons\nInformal Past Negative form of Verbs\nJapanese Ammo\nJapanese verb conjugation\nKanji Link\nNegation of masu verbs ません\nJapanese Ammo\nNegative Verbs\nTae Kim\nう-Verbs\nImabi 2\nる-Verbs\nImabi\nOffline\nGenki I 2nd Edition\n88 & 190\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 47\nTry N5\nPage 20\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 110 [CH 17]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nう-Verb (Negative) – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (10 in total)\nAsher\nChanging this now so that they are the same. Just to be clear though, whether something is ない or ません itself will not change the nuance apart from the politeness. The nuance itself will need to be understood from other context (if there is any).\nJonEPizza\nWhy do I get this lesson after already being quizzed on the negative forms and not before?\nLike, I did actually piece the rules of the negative article together from getting the questions wrong, and then hitting see answer, but why\nnecessitate\na negative feedback on this material by not even showing it to me first?\nJamesBunpro\nHello and welcome to the forums!\nIf you learnt this through the regular ‘Learn’ queue then this shouldn’t have happened. I am trying to think of ways the scenario you are describing may have happened. Did you perhaps add this grammar point directly to your review queue instead of first seeing it through ‘Learn’? Or perhaps you mean you were quizzed on the negative form in other questions? If so, are you using the default Bunpro order or perhaps a textbook order like Genki?\nIf you can give some more details as to what happened and how you’re using Bunpro I can hopefully tell you what went wrong (or if this is a bug!). Cheers!\nGot questions about う-Verb (Negative)?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
