      "sourceText": "くる (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 4: 6/13\n来\n(\nく\n)\nる\nTo come\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nくる\nTo come\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nくる\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nVerb\nWord Type\nIndependent Word\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout くる\n来\n(\nく\n)\nる\nis one of only two main verbs in Japanese that do not follow the standard rules of conjugation for\nGodan\nand\nIchidan\nverbs. The other primary verb being\nする\n.\n来\n(\nく\n)\nる\nmeans 'to come', or 'to be coming'.\n来\n(\nく\n)\nる\nwill always have the particle\nに\nor\nへ\nattached to the location that someone 'will come', or 'has come'.\nThe reason that\n来\n(\nく\n)\nる\nis called a か-column irregular conjugation verb in Japanese is because the る in\n来\n(\nく\n)\nる\ndoes not conjugate, but is dropped. This leaves the く being the part that conjugates. Because of this, 来る has qualities of both\nる-Verbs\n(the る being dropped), and\nう-Verbs\n(the remaining く changing depending on the conjugation). Here are some examples of how\n来\n(\nく\n)\nる\nchanges.\n（き）た, （く）る, （こ）ない.\n来\n(\nく\n)\nる\nwill often be seen as\n来\n(\nき\n)\nている\nwhen someone has come to a place and remains there. This is due to\nている\nmeaning 'to exist in the state of an action', rather than being exactly the same as '(A)~ing' in English. Because of this,\n来\n(\nき\n)\nている\ncan mean either 'to be somewhere that one has come', or 'to be coming'.\nSometimes the difference between these two will not be clear unless there is further context.\nCaution\n来\n(\nき\n)\nている\n(for existing at a place) is only used for places that people 'temporarily' go, so will not be used when you are at your home/work.\nPractice 来る in\nKaijugation\nThe only conjugation game with collateral damage!\nPlay!\nTopics\nEasy Come, Easy Go\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to 'get moving'. We will learn more about three essential verbs in the Japanese language.\n行\n(\nい\n)\nく 'to go',\n来\n(\nく\n)\nる 'to come', and する 'to do'. We will also be introduced to a new particle, へ, which indicates the direction that something is heading in.\n4m\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nへいく\nへいく\nTo go to, To head toward\nN5 Grammar\nくる and いく are both movement verbs often used with directional particles to express moving toward a particular location. The difference lies in the perspective of the speaker and directionality: くる typically expresses movement toward the speaker's location, while いく expresses movement away from the speaker's location.\nRelated\nExpand All\nてくる\n～てくる\nTo come to, To become, To continue, To be starting to, Has been ~ing\nN4 Grammar\nThe verb くる commonly expresses movement toward the speaker, similar to the English 'come.' When combined with the て form of a verb, it can be used literally (e.g., 持ってくる = 'bring') or figuratively to express progression or change over time, like the English 'come to be' (e.g., 雨が降ってくる = 'it will come to rain').\nいらっしゃる\nTo be (Honorific), To come (Honorific), To go (Honorific)\nN4 Grammar\nBoth いらっしゃる and くる express movement toward the speaker, similar to the English 'come.' The key difference is politeness: くる is the neutral verb used in everyday contexts, while いらっしゃる is an honorific verb used to show respect for someone's movement. Note that いらっしゃる also serves as the honorific form of いる, いく, and くる.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nトム\nは\nここ\nに\nくる\n。\nTom\nwill come\nhere.\nバス\nが\nくる\n。\nThe bus\nwill come\n.\n彼\n(\nかれ\n)\nは\nバス\nで\nきます\n。\nHe\nwill come\nby bus.\n友達\n(\nともだち\n)\nも\nきます\nか\n。\nWill\nyour friend\ncome\ntoo?\n明日\n(\nあした\n)\nきます\n。\nI\nwill come\ntomorrow.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nConjugations of「くる」\nJapanese Language Guide\nSpecial uses of「くる」\nSelf Taught Japanese\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 219\nGenki I\nPage 59\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 89\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 86\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 38 [CH 5]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nくる – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (9 in total)\nJamesBunpro\nGenerally words which talk about time from a relative position (today, tomorrow, etc) do not use に. They can be considered as being used adverbially, not as nouns, in such cases. So, 明日に来ます would normally be considered unnatural.\nFrom the perspective of what is being tested in the grammar reviews for this point, you don’t need to worry about adding a particle anyway!\nstupidguy\nUnderstood, thank you! You also answered my omitted second question with that (“adverbially, not as nouns”).\nMalmsteen007\nI don’t understand why the example sentence カナは駅に来ている? is translated as “is Kana\ncoming\nto the station?”, when earlier there’s a similar example sentence サヤの家に来ている translated as \" I have\ncome\nto Saya’s house.\" Can “kitteiru” be used only for the “result of the state of coming” (I have come), or can it be also to say you’re “coming somewhere” in general?\nGot questions about くる?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
