      "sourceText": "る-Verb (Negative-Past) (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 5: 7/12\nる-Verb (Negative-Past)\nる - Verbs (Negative-past tense)\nWhen a past negative sentence has a connection with the present, for example, something was expected to happen but has not happened for the moment, the verb[て] + いません construction is used instead\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nる-Verb (Negative-Pas...\nる - Verbs (Negative-past tense)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nExamples:\n​​\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nる\n+\nなかった\n​​\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nる\n+\nなかった\n​​\n寝\n(\nね\n)\nる\n+\nなかった\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nVerb\nWord Type\nAuxiliary Verb\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout る-Verb (Negative-Past)\nIn order to form the negative past form of\nる-Verbs\n, you will need to remove the る, and then follow it with the\nauxiliary verb\nない\n(or ます), conjugated into their past forms. This will either be\nなかった\n(casual), or\nませんでした\n(polite).\nA semi-polite form may also be used. This is constructed by using\nなかった\n, and then simply adding\nです\n. This form is not grammatically correct, but is so common that most sources (and native speakers) would consider it correct.\nPractice る-Verb (Negative-Past) in\nKaijugation\nThe only conjugation game with collateral damage!\nPlay!\nTopics\nI Didn't Do It\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn how to make the negative-past form of verbs. As usual, the polite and the standard forms are different. Upon learning this, we will have covered the complete set of tenses (past and non-past) and polarities (positive and negative) for verbs.\n3m\nRelated\nExpand All\nる-Verb (Negative)\nWill/Does/Do (not)\nN5 Grammar\nThese forms express the negative ('does not' or 'will not') of る-Verbs, with a difference in tense. The る-Verb (Negative) expresses a lack of future or habitual action (e.g., 食べない = 'I don't eat' or 'I won't eat'). The る-Verb (Negative-Past) expresses a lack of past action (e.g., 食べなかった = 'I didn't eat'). Both are plain forms used in casual speech or other grammatical patterns.\nる-Verb (Past)\nる - Verbs (Past tense)\nN5 Grammar\nThese forms are used with る-Verbs to express the idea of having done or not having done a particular action in the past. The difference is whether the meaning is affirmative ('did') or negative ('did not'). For example, 食べた means 'I ate,' while 食べなかった means 'I did not eat.' Both are plain forms used in casual speech or as part of other grammatical forms.\nう-Verb (Negative-Past)\nう - Verbs (Negative-past tense)\nN5 Grammar\nBoth forms express the negative past ('did not'), but the formation differs depending on whether the verb is a る-Verb or う-Verb. For example, 食べなかった (for 食べる, a る-Verb) means 'I did not eat,' while 買わなかった (for 買う, a う-Verb) means 'I did not buy.' Both are plain forms used in casual speech or other grammatical patterns.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n寿司\n(\nすし\n)\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべなかった\n。\n寿司\n(\nすし\n)\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべなかった\nです\n。\n寿司\n(\nすし\n)\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべませんでした\n。\nI\ndidn't eat\nsushi.\nテレビ\nを\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nなかった\n。\nテレビ\nを\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nなかった\nです\n。\nテレビ\nを\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nませんでした\n。\nI\ndidn't watch\nTV.\nお\n金\n(\nかね\n)\nを\n借\n(\nか\n)\nりなかった\n。\nお\n金\n(\nかね\n)\nを\n借\n(\nか\n)\nりなかった\nです\n。\nお\n金\n(\nかね\n)\nを\n借\n(\nか\n)\nりませんでした\n。\nI\ndid not borrow\nmoney.\n早\n(\nはや\n)\nく\n起\n(\nお\n)\nきなかった\n。\n早\n(\nはや\n)\nく\n起\n(\nお\n)\nきなかった\nです\n。\n早\n(\nはや\n)\nく\n起\n(\nお\n)\nきませんでした\n。\nI\ndidn't wake up\nearly.\nこれ\nは\n教\n(\nおし\n)\nえなかった\n。\nこれ\nは\n教\n(\nおし\n)\nえなかった\nです\n。\nこれ\nは\n教\n(\nおし\n)\nえませんでした\n。\nI\ndidn't tell\nyou this.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nPast-negative tense for all verbs\nTae Kim's Guide to Learning Japanese\nAeron Buchanan's Japanese Verb Chart\nWikipedia Commons\nIchidan Verbs　一段活用動詞\nImabi\nInformal Past Negative form of Verbs\nJapanese Ammo\nPast Tense and Present Perfect Tense\nWasabi\nOffline\nGenki I 2nd Edition\n110 & 212\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 214\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\n87 & 52\nTry N5\n39 & 101\nみんなの日本語 I, Translation & Grammatical Notes\n34\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nる-Verb (Negative-Past) – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (4 in total)\nDaru\nIt’s a slight difference in nuance!\n見なかった is just saying that you didn’t watch the movie, a flat statement. Nothing else.\n見たことがない focuses on the fact that you haven’t watched the movie. While it seems similar, the nuance here is that seeing that movie is important or relevant to the conversation.\nDepending on the context, it could be as if you were talking about movies, and you said (a bit literally) that you\nhaven’t had the experience\nof watching that movie, implying it’s something you reckon you ought do or plan to.\nHope this helps!\nhetore\nI see!\nI was thinking something along those lines. Like,\n見なかった\nbeing\n“I didn’t watch this movie that came out just last week”\n, and\n見たことがない\nbeing more like\n“I have never watched this classic movie from the 90s”\n, similar to your example of\n“not having the experience of watching it”\nThanks!\nDaru\nHappy to help! Good luck with your studies!\nGot questions about る-Verb (Negative-Past)?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
