      "sourceText": "で (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 5: 1/12\nで\nWith, By (using)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nで\nWith, By (using)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nNoun +\nで\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nCase Marking Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout で\nで\nis a particle that has several different uses in Japanese. At its core, it is always used to highlight something that is 'required' to perform some sort of action/reach some kind of goal. One of these uses relates to 'things'. This comes across as 'by' or 'with' in English.\nIn these sentences, the item is considered essential for the action to be performed. While other items may be used,\nで\nsimply highlights the one that was chosen.\nで\nis also used when groups of people perform actions together, this is something that many people learning Japanese have trouble with initially (because\nと\nmay also be used as 'with' for people). However, the use of\nで\nis actually the same as this by/with, meaning that the speaker considers the group as the tool used for performing the action.\nThe difference between\nと\nand\nで\nas 'with', is that\nで\nis used when everyone in the group has the same objective/end goal.\nと\nis used when each person is performing the action toward each other, not toward an external objective.\nTopics\nA Means to an End\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about how the case-marking particle で can be used to show a 'means'. That is, how it is used to mark the tool or method that is used to perform an action.\n2m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nで\nAt, In\nN5 Grammar\nThe particle で has two main uses: the first is indicating the location where an action takes place (e.g., 家で遊ぶ = 'play at home'). The second is marking the means or instrument used to perform an action (e.g., ボールで遊ぶ = 'play with a ball'). These differences can usually be inferred from context, so it's recommended to not stress this too much.\nRelated\nExpand All\nをもって\nAs of, With, Effective from\nN1 Grammar\nを以て can be though of as a formal version of the basic particle で when used to mean 'at this point in time', e.g. これ（で・を以て）閉会します ('With this, we will end the meeting.'). Note that をもって would not typically be used with the meaning of で indicating 'place of an action', e.g. 大手企業で働く 'to work at a major corporation'.\nを以て\nをもって\nBy means of, With, By, Via, In view of\nN1 Grammar\nを以て can be though of as a highly formal/written-style version of the basic particle で when used to mean 'with'/'by means of', e.g. 鉛筆で書く 'write with a pencil' vs. 死を以て償う 'to atone (e.g. for a sin) with one's death' or 'at this point in time', e.g. これ（で・を以て）閉会します ('With this, we will end the meeting.'). Note that を以て would not typically be used with the meaning of で indicating 'place of an action', e.g. 大手企業で働く 'to work at a major corporation'.\nVerb[て]・Noun[で] + B\n～て (Means and Manner)\nBy, With, On, By means of, ~ing with\nN4 Grammar\nThe particle で follows a noun to describe the means or method of an action (e.g., ペンで書く = 'to write with a pen'). A verb in the て form can express a similar nuance (e.g., ペンを使って書く = 'to write using a pen,' 歩いて行く = 'to go by walking'). The meaning is the same, but the form differs depending on whether the 'method' is expressed with a noun or a verb.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nバス\nで\n行\n(\nい\n)\nく\n。\nI go\nby\nbus.\nバス\nで\n学校\n(\nがっこう\n)\nに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nく\n。\nI go to school\nby\nbus.\n自転車\n(\nじてんしゃ\n)\nで\n、\nいい\nです\n。\nBy\nbicycle is fine.\nUsually で would not be used this way, but we can assume there is some prior context here where the person is saying this as a casual abbreviation of a longer sentence.\n自転車\n(\nじてんしゃ\n)\nで\n。\nBy\nbicycle.\n電車\n(\nでんしゃ\n)\nで\n来\n(\nき\n)\nます\nか\n。\nAre you coming\nby\ntrain?\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nThe contextual 「で」 particle\nTae Kim\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 106\nGenki I\nPage 198\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 236\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 238\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 58\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 38 [CH 5]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nで – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (6 in total)\nPushindawood\n@Bambamlindsay Welcome to the community! It\ndid\ntake us a little while, but we actually added a whole new grammar page to cover the other uses of て/で when it is used to express “by means of” or “in the manner of.” Cheers!\njesse_dylan\nThis whole thing was super illunimating.  However, there’s that nice explanation of で vs と for with… but then none of the examples address that; I think the examples are still pretty much transportation-oriented.  But maybe this is addressed later on.  I’m good with it, just still don’t quite understand the で vs と nuance.\ncafelatte\nOne of the ways it was explained to me in terms of people is that if it’s “de” you’re more part of the unit and the focus is on what the unit did, and if it’s “to” it’s just like we did it together\nI go to the restaurant with my family (de) → the focus is on the group, of which you are part, doing the action. We are doing the thing.\nI go to the restaurant with my family (to) → the focus is being together with the family to eat. You + family are doing the thing.\nGot questions about で?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
