      "sourceText": "まえに (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 8: 3/13\n前\n(\nまえ\n)\nに\nBefore, In front of\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nまえに\nBefore, In front of\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb +\n前\n(\nまえ\n)\nに\nNoun +\nの\n+\n前\n(\nまえ\n)\nに\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nAdverb\nWord Type\nAdverb Phrase\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout まえに\n前\n(\nまえ\n)\nに\nis used when describing something that is either 'before something' (in time), or 'in front of something' (a location). It is used with verbs (in their non-past form), or nouns. When used with nouns,\nの\nis required before\n前\n(\nまえ\n)\nに\n.\nCaution\nAnother similar construction to\n前\n(\nまえ\n)\nに\nis\n手前\n(\nてまえ\n)\nに\n.\n前\n(\nまえ\n)\nに\ncan be used for something that is in front of any location. However,\n手前\n(\nてまえ\n)\nに\nmay only be used when things in front of you (or someone/something else) are being described physically.\nTopics\nRoughly Before That\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about the two words which allow us to talk about relative value or position. くらい can be used as an adverbial particle or noun to express something is roughly 'about' a certain value.\n前\n(\nまえ\n)\nis a noun which can be used adverbially to mean 'before'.\n3m\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nあとで\nあとで\nAfter, Later\nN4 Grammar\nあとで and まえに are both used to describe the timing of actions, but with opposite meanings. まえに usually follows a plain (non-past) verb and means 'before doing A' (e.g., 食べる前に = 'before eating'). In contrast, あとで typically follows the past tense form of a verb and means 'after doing B' (e.g., 食べたあとで = 'after eating').\nRelated\nExpand All\n手前\nてまえ\nBefore, In front of, From one's standpoint or side\nN2 Grammar\nBoth まえに and 手前 can express a meaning similar to ''before' or 'in front of' in English. The difference is that まえに simply refers to happening earlier in time, or being physically located in front of something else. In contrast, 手前 adds nuance, often implying that, because one is faced with a certain situation (A), a particular action or outcome (B) follows as a result.\n前者は・後者は\nぜんしゃは～こうしゃは\nThe former ~ the latter, The first ~ the second\nN3 Grammar\nまえに is used to express the relative timing of actions and often follows a plain (non-past) verb to mean 'before doing A.' In contrast, 前者 (the former) and 後者 (the latter) refer to things previously mentioned. They're used to avoid repeating a word or phrase, similar to how we use 'the former' and 'the latter' in English.\nないうちに\nないうちに\nBefore it becomes, Before ~ occurs, Without\nN3 Grammar\nないうちに and まえに both mean doing something before another event, but with different nuances. まえに simply marks one action happening before another. ないうちに adds the sense of doing something while there's still time. For example, 暗くならないうちに帰ろう means 'Let's go home before it gets dark,' with the nuance that it's better to leave while it's still light out.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nる\nまえに\n「いただ\nきます\n」\nと\n言\n(\nい\n)\nう。\nBefore\neating we say 'いただ\nきます\n'.\n帰\n(\nかえ\n)\nる\nまえに\n勉強\n(\nべんきょう\n)\nを\nする\n。\nBefore\ngoing home I will study.\nまえに\nここ\nに\n来\n(\nき\n)\nました\n。\nI've been here\nbefore\n.\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nる\nまえに\n手\n(\nて\n)\nを\n洗\n(\nあら\n)\nいます\n。\nI will wash my hands\nbefore\nI eat.\nテレビ\nのまえに\n、\n机\n(\nつくえ\n)\nがあります\n。\nThere is a desk\nin front of\nthe TV.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nBefore and after\nTae Kim\nSee Notes\nJGram\nOffline\n[AIAIJ] An Integrated Approach to Intermediate Japanese\nPage 114\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 231\nGenki II 1st Edition\nPage 101\nGenki II 2nd Edition\nPage 127\nGenki II 3rd Edition\nPage 122\nMarugoto Elementary 1 (A2) Rikai\nPage 82, 138\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 116 [CH 18]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nまえに – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (12 in total)\nka5\n(A)の前に just means in front of (A). So 病院の前に would be “in front of hospital”, and コンビニの前に would be “in front of convenience store”.\nAnd then the noun that A is in front of comes in through direct noun modification… mm not sure if there is a more specific point on bunpro, but let’s link this one: がある + Noun (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nSo, together (A)の前にある(B) means “(B), that exists in front of (A)”. You can’t mix them up.\nHope this helps?\nhoma\nOk folks, hear me out: お前の前にいるのは、千年以上生きた魔法使いだ\nIf I want to add something one to the Bunpro, it is this sentence, so maybe something could be replaced)\nThe111\nWhy is the word “particle” at the top of this review? IIUC, 前に is not a particle. The lesson does not even present it as such; it presents it as an adverb phrase. That phrase is composed of a noun and a particle; the former is the more noteworthy part for this lesson.\nGot questions about まえに?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
