      "sourceText": "～になる・～くなる (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 8: 9/13\nになる・くなる\nTo become (Adjective)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\n～になる・～くなる\nTo become (Adjective)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\n［な］Adjective\n+\nに\n+\nなる\n［い］Adjective［く］\n+\nなる\nNoun +\nに\n+\nなる\nExceptions:\nいい\n￫\nよく\nなる\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nExpression\nWord Type\nVerb\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout ～になる・～くなる\nになる\n(with\nな-Adjectives\n), or\nくなる\n(with\nい-Adjectives\n) is an expression used in Japanese to show that something is 'becoming (A)'. It can be used with any form of\nなる\n, to highlight that something will become (non-past), is becoming (continuous), or has become (past). It can even be used with negative forms, to show that something 'will not become (A)'.\nIn the example with the tea getting cold, we can see how ている sometimes highlights something that exists in an ongoing state, rather than changing any further. In other words, 'the tea has gotten cold, and remains to be cold'.\nAdditionally, the adjective\nいい\n'good' is an exception to the regular conjugation rules for\nい-Adjectives\n, and will conjugate as よくなる, not いくなる.\nTopics\nI've Decided to Become Good (at Japanese)\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about how to use verbs する and なる in more advanced ways. Specifically, we're going to learn about the にする pattern (used to say something is 'decided on') and the になる and くなる patterns (used to say that something 'becomes' something else).\n2m\nRelated\nExpand All\n化する\nかする\n-ification, -ization, To make something, To change, To become, Transform into\nN4 Grammar\n化する and ～になる・～くなる both express change. 化する attaches to nouns and means '~ify' or '~ize', e.g. データ化する 'to digitize' (literally, 'to turn into data'). ～になる and ～くなる are more general-purpose ways to express becoming something, e.g. 綺麗になる 'become clean' or 白くなる 'to become white'.\nてくる\n～てくる\nTo come to, To become, To continue, To be starting to, Has been ~ing\nN4 Grammar\nBoth ～になる／～くなる and てくる describe changes in state but differ in usage. ～になる／～くなる are used with nouns and adjectives to show something becoming a certain condition. てくる, used with verbs, suggests a gradual change over time. They can also be combined. For example, 面白くなってきた means 'It gradually became interesting.'\n～にする・～くする\nにする・くする\nAdjective + する, To make something/someone (more)\nN4 Grammar\n～にする・～くする and ～になる・～くなる express change but differ in focus. ～にする and ～くする imply making something a certain way, e.g. 綺麗にする 'make clean', 甘くする 'make sweet'. ～になる and ～くなる describe something changing on its own, e.g. 綺麗になる 'become clean', 甘くなる 'become sweet'. The difference is between focusing on the agent causing the change, or simply framing it in terms of the thing itself changing.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n難\n(\nむずか\n)\nしくなる\n。\nTo become difficult.\n嬉\n(\nうれ\n)\nしくなる\n。\nTo become happy\n.\n早\n(\nはや\n)\nくなる\n。\nTo become fast\n.\nよくなる\n。\nTo become better\n.\n綺麗\n(\nきれい\n)\nになる\n。\nTo\nbecome pretty/clean\n.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\n「くなる」\nJapaneseTest4You\n「なる」\nTae Kim\nOffline\nGenki I\nPage 196\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 234\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 236\nMarugoto Elementary 2 (A2) Rikai\nPage 149\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 129 & 130\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 122 [CH 19]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\n～になる・～くなる – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (46 in total)\nFuga\nThe difference between 冷たくなっているよ and 冷たくなったよ is the nuance and similar to how ‘has become’ and ‘became’ have a slight difference in nuance. 冷たくなっているよ has the nuance of ‘it has become cold’, and 冷たくなったよ has the nuance of ‘it became cold’. With 冷たくなっているよ the tea is still in the process of getting cold, while with 冷たくなったよ, just focuses on the fact that it got cold sometime in the past.\nTo express ‘the tea is (in the state of) getting cold’, 冷たくなっている would be best to use!\nI hope this clears it up!\ntzj\nIcyIceBear\nVolitional form of しまう\nBunpro\nVerb[よう] (JLPT N4) | Bunpro\nよう (and う) are grammar constructions that function in a similar way to ましょう, or to be more precise, ましょう is the polite (conjugated) form of these auxiliary......\nGot questions about ～になる・～くなる?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
