      "sourceText": "にする (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 8: 8/13\nにする\nTo decide on, To choose, To make something into, To view something as\nSometimes written as とする, which focuses on the result, rather than the process of 'making'\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nにする\nTo decide on, To choose, To make something into, To view something as\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nNoun +\nに\n+\nする\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nExpression\nWord Type\nVerb\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout にする\nにする\nis a structure that combines the\nに\nparticle (a location/target), with the verb\nする\n. It means to 'make/decide' something in a certain way. The nuance with\nにする\nis that the speaker has direct control over the outcome. This is where the strong feeling of 'choice' comes from. The noun that comes before\nにする\nis the thing that is being decided.\nIn this example, although the common translation is 'to decide', it is actually much closer to the slang expression 'to do (A)' in English. This means that the literal translation is actually 'I'll do a beer', or 'I'll make it a beer'.\nSometimes the particle\nと\nis used instead of に. Although this has the same meaning, it changes the nuance slightly. When\nと\nis used, it takes away the feeling of being a direct decision.\nとする\ntends to be used in more formal situations, but this is only because\nと\nis less 'definite', so results in the phrase sounding less pushy.\nIf you were going to compare these two to an English equivalent, it would be similar to the difference between the following sentences.\nに\n- I'll buy a burger. (A burger is the direct target of 'buy')\nと\n- I'll go with a burger. (You aren't 'going' anywhere, it just sounds softer)\nThis grammar point may be used for more abstract nouns, and does not specifically need to be 'items'.\nTopics\nI've Decided to Become Good (at Japanese)\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about how to use verbs する and なる in more advanced ways. Specifically, we're going to learn about the にする pattern (used to say something is 'decided on') and the になる and くなる patterns (used to say that something 'becomes' something else).\n2m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nことにする\nことにする\nTo decide on, Have decided to\nN3 Grammar\nBoth にする and ことにする share the same core meaning: making a choice or decision. にする is used when choosing a thing and follows a noun, like ビールにする ('I'll have a beer'). ことにする follows plain-form verbs and expresses the speaker's decision to do or not do something, like 行くことにする or 行かないことにする.\nRelated\nExpand All\nことになる\nことになる\nIt will end up being, It is the result of, It has been decided that\nN3 Grammar\nBoth にする and ことになる relate to decisions but differ in agency. にする is used when the speaker actively chooses something, often in daily situations like deciding what to eat, similar to 'I'll go with X.' ことになる, on the other hand, expresses decisions made by others or by circumstance, like saying 'It's been decided that X.'\nに決まっている\nにきまっている\nSurely, Bound to, Certainly, Be decided\nN2 Grammar\nBoth にする and に決まっている relate to decisions but differ in use. にする expresses making a choice, often in daily contexts like picking what to eat, similar to 'I'll go with X.' に決まっている, despite using 決まる, often just shows strong belief, meaning 'It's surely X' or 'It's bound to be X,' rather than an actual decision.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n何\n(\nなに\n)\nにする\n？\nWhat will you\ndecide on\n?\n今日\n(\nきょう\n)\nの\n晩\n(\nばん\n)\nごはん\nは\n何\n(\nなに\n)\nにする\n？\nWhat are you going\nto have\nfor dinner tonight? (\nDecide on\n)\nこの\nかばん\nにします\n。\nTo decide on\nthis bag.\n明日\n(\nあした\n)\nの\n朝\n(\nあさ\n)\nごはん\nは\n魚\n(\nさかな\n)\nにします\n。\nTo decide on\nfish for breakfast tomorrow.\nこの\nレストラン\nにします\n。\nTo decide on\nthis restaurant.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nExamples\nRenshuu\nUsing する and なる with the に particle\nTae Kim\nOffline\n[AIAIJ] An Integrated Approach to Intermediate Japanese\nPage 72\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 310\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 129\nみんなの日本語 II\nPage 120 [CH 44]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nにする – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (14 in total)\nmrnoone\nHey @mielke\nWe will likely replace the sentence, by the way, if you tap on the ましょうか it will show the definition\nCheers!\nComebacktomorrow\nI’m curious as to why にとする is softer. What role is the と taking on? Is it simply the quotation particle in a capacity outside of standard say/heard/think or is it an odd exception?\nMy bit of reading makes me think it has to do with:\n… acting as if to make a motion to do [verb]. As we have already seen, this translates to “attempt to do [verb]”.\nFrom Tae Kim\nIt feels like a framing device that doesn’t really have any direct English equivalent (that I can think of), which I guess would make sense since we don’t have anything like a quotation particle!\nAzuraKING607\nHi Jake, is it a good idea to think of this sentence 今日はここまでとする as being more literally “Today, we will decide to do it up to here?” And 今日はここまでにする as being more like “Today, I will do it up to here?”\nI was wondering where the “end” word from the translation fit into this sentence.\nGot questions about にする?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
