      "sourceText": "のなかで～がいちばん～ (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 8: 10/13\nの\n中\n(\nなか\n)\nで～が\n一番\n(\nいちばん\n)\nOut of ~, ~ is the best/most ~\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nのなかで～がいちばん～\nOut of ~, ~ is the best/most ~\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\n(Set of Elements) +\nの\n中\n(\nなか\n)\nで\n一番\n(\nいちばん\n)\nその\n(1)\n+\n中\n(\nなか\n)\nで\n一番\n(\nいちばん\n)\n(1)\nこの\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nExpression\nWord Type\nExpression\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout のなかで～がいちばん～\nIn Japanese '(A)\nの\n中\n(\nなか\n)\nで\n(B)\nが\n一番\n(\nいちばん\n)\n(C)', is a regular expression that is used for showing that something is 'the most (C)', usually an adjective. This structure can be used with almost any adjective, whether it is positive or negative. Each part of this grammar point represents the following:\n(A) - A group.\n(B) - A single thing/person within that group.\n(C) - An adjective, or general description of that thing/person.\nThis expression is also frequently used with demonstratives such as\nこの\nand\nその\n.\nThe construction '\n(A) の\n中\n(\nなか\n)\nで\n' is regularly translated by itself as '\namong (A)\n', and may be used in many different expressions. This will be covered in a\nseparate grammar point\n.\nTopics\nOut of These, I Prefer That\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about two phrases which are made up of more than one part. These are sentence-level patterns which combine smaller parts to create comparisons.\n2m\nRelated\nExpand All\nのなかで\nのなかで\nIn, Among, Within\nN4 Grammar\nと～と、どちらが\nBetween ~ which one\nN4 Grammar\nBoth of these patterns can be used to express preferences or comparisons. The main difference is that the と～と、どちらが pattern is used when asking about or expressing a choice between two options, while のなかで～がいちばん～ involves comparing a group of three or more options.\n～のうち(で)\nのうちで\nOf the, Among(st)\nN2 Grammar\nBoth ～のうち(で) and のなかで express the idea of 'among' or 'out of,' used when selecting or describing something from a group. のうち is often (though not exclusively) used with number + counter phrases, as in 10人のうち5人が車を持っている ('Out of 10 people, 5 own a car'). のなかで is more common and casual in speech, while のうち has a slightly more formal tone.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nこの\n中\n(\nなか\n)\nで\n一番\n(\nいちばん\n)\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\nな\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\n物\n(\nもの\n)\nは\n何\n(\nなに\n)\n？\nAmong these\n, what is your\nnumber one\nfavorite food?\n中華\n(\nちゅうか\n)\n料理\n(\nりょうり\n)\nの\n中\n(\nなか\n)\nで\n何\n(\nなに\n)\nが\n一番\n(\nいちばん\n)\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\nです\nか\n。\nOut of all\nChinese dishes, what is your\nnumber one\nfavorite dish?\n飲\n(\nの\n)\nみ\n物\n(\nもの\n)\nの\n中\n(\nなか\n)\nで\n一番\n(\nいちばん\n)\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\nな\nのは\n何\n(\nなん\n)\nです\nか\n。\nOut of all\ndrinks, what is your\nnumber one\nfavorite drink?\nお\n茶\n(\nちゃ\n)\nの\n中\n(\nなか\n)\nで\n一番\n(\nいちばん\n)\n美味\n(\nおい\n)\nし\nい\nのは\n何\n(\nなん\n)\nです\nか\n。\nOut of all\nteas, what is the\nnumber one\nmost delicious tea?\n国\n(\nくに\n)\nの\n中\n(\nなか\n)\nで\n一番\n(\nいちばん\n)\n人口\n(\nじんこう\n)\nが\n多\n(\nおお\n)\nい\nのは\nどれ\nです\nか\n。\nOut of all\ncountries, which country has\nthe number one\nlargest population?\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\n～いちばん～\nJapaneseTest4You\n～のなかでＡがいちばん～\nE-Japanese\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 148\nGenki I\nPage 194\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 232\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 234\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 80 [CH 12]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nのなかで～がいちばん～ – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (14 in total)\ndoublesbrian\nI just got to this grammar point. In the information it says\nIn Japanese ‘(A) の中で (B) が一番 (C)’, is a regular expression that is used for showing that something is ‘the most (C)’, usually an adjective.\nAnd that’s the layout I’ve been seeing, but the first sentence I got in the quiz is a bit different.\n動物\nどうぶつ\nの\n中\nなか\nで\n一番\nいちばん\n好\nす\nきな\n動物\nどうぶつ\nは\n何\nなん\nですか。\nOut of all\nanimals, what is your\nnumber one\nfavorite animal?\nIt threw me for a loop because it doesn’t have the (B) が portion of the structure I’m used to and I didn’t realize you can put のなかで and いちばん together like that.\nIn my mind the sentence would be closer to 動物の中で何が一番好きですか\nCan someone explain the nuances of the sentence structure Bunpro used? Because I don’t really understand it.\nThanks!\nDaru\nHey @doublesbrian!\ndoublesbrian:\nI didn’t realize you can put のなかで and いちばん together like that.\nIt’s basically because 一番 is working as an adverb that modifies the following adjective here.\n高い Expensive → 一番高い The most expensive\n大きい Big → 一番大きい The biggest\n好き Likeable → 一番好き The most likeable (or, your favorite!)\n[動物の中で] Out of all the animals\n[一番好きな動物] The most likeable animal\n[は] Speaking of this (the most likeable animal out of all the animals)\n[何ですか？] Which one is it?\ndoublesbrian\nThanks for the reply! I knew the way the site was using it was probably more natural but didn’t understand it until you broke it down for me! I appreciate it!\nGot questions about のなかで～がいちばん～?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
