      "sourceText": "なにか・なにも (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 8: 12/13\n何\n(\nなに\n)\n+ か\nSomething, Nothing, Anything, Not anything, Some kind of, Not any kind of\nThe meaning of these words will change, based on whether the sentence is positive, or negative\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nなにか・なにも\nSomething, Nothing, Anything, Not anything, Some kind of, Not any kind of\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\n何\n(\nなに\n)\nか\n+ Phrase\n何\n(\nなに\n)\nも\n+ Phrase［ない］\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nNoun\nWord Type\nAdverbial Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout なにか・なにも\n何\n(\nなに\n)\nか\nand\n何\n(\nなに\n)\nも\nare constructions that are used in Japanese for expressing 'something', 'anything', or 'nothing'. The main difference between the two being that\n何\n(\nなに\n)\nも\nis used with negative phrases (phrases that use\nない\nor\nありません\n), while\n何\n(\nなに\n)\nか\nis used in other cases.\nTo use this expression, we simply need to put\n何\n(\nなに\n)\nか\nor\n何\n(\nなに\n)\nも\nbefore the desired phrase.\nCaution\nThere are several casual forms of these expressions that are used in speech, or in certain dialects. These forms are\nなんにか\n,\nなんか\n, or\nなんにも\n,\nなんも\n.\nTopics\nWho Did What Now?!\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about how か and も can be added to question words to turn them into non-specific nouns.\n2m\nRelated\nExpand All\nでもなんでもない\nNot at all, By no means, Definitely not, Nothing like that, Not really\nN1 Grammar\nThe なにか・なにも pattern combines the question word なに with particles to express ideas like 'something' or 'nothing.' でもなんでもない is a variation of なんでも used after a noun to emphasize 'not A or anything like it.' It can be used with both positive (迷惑でもなんでもない = 'It's not a bother or anything') and negative (好きでもなんでもない = 'I don't like it or anything') nuance.\n誰か・どこか・誰も・どこも\nだれか・どこか・だれも・どこも\nSomeone, Somewhere, Not anyone, Not anywhere\nN5 Grammar\nBoth なにか・なにも and 誰か・どこか・誰も・どこも combine question words (like なに, 誰, どこ) with particles to express ideas such as 'something,' 'nothing,' 'someone,' or 'somewhere.' Their meanings shift depending on whether the sentence is affirmative or negative.\nQuestion-phrase + か\n(Question-Phrase) + か\nIncluding questions within sentences\nN4 Grammar\nThe なにか・なにも pattern combines the question word なに with particles to express ideas like 'something' or 'nothing.' This structure is based on how the particle か is used with question words within a sentence. For example, 何が食べたいか分かりますか？ means 'Do you know what (they) want to eat?'\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nなにか\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nる\n？\nDo you want to eat\nsomething\n?\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nが\nなにか\n嫌\n(\nいや\n)\nな\nこと\nを\nし\nました\nか\n？\nDid I do\nsomething\nyou didn't like?\nいいえ、\nなにも\nし\nてい\nません\nよ\n。\nNo, I\ndidn't\ndo\nanything\n(wrong).\n日本\n(\nにほん\n)\nに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nっ\nて\n、\nなにか\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nたい\nもの\nがあります\nか\n。\nWhen you go to Japan, is there\nsomething\nthat you want to eat?\n頑張\n(\nがんば\n)\nっ\nて\n勉強\n(\nべんきょう\n)\nした\nけど\nなにも\nわから\nない\n。\nI studied diligently, but I\ndon't\nunderstand\nanything\n.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nDifference between 何も and 何でも\nJapanese StackExchange\nHow to use なんか\nMaggie Sensei\nUsing question words\nTae Kim\nOffline\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 196\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 196\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 100\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 87 [CH 13]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nなにか・なにも – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (17 in total)\nTensor\nWhy isn’t 「何かを食べる」 grammatical?\nIt seems to me that 何か is the direct object of 食べる but it seems that this isn’t accepted by the grammar checker.\nI understand the idea that 「何もを食べない」definitely sounds and looks ungrammatical, but I’d rather have a more detailed explanation than through analogy with this form’s “negation.”\nIcyIceBear\nBoth も and か are particles here attached to 何, so if one looks wrong, so does the other. 何か isn’t one word, it’s 何+か to equal the idea of “something”. The particle を is dropped when these other particles are in use.\nYou can probably find better explanations in the resource tab of the grammar point, or by doing an Internet search of why not to use を after 何か/何も. I don’t tend to get hung up on the why of these points, just that it isn’t really done and that’s enough for me, so I can’t tell you more. Welcome to the community\nStainy\nGot questions about なにか・なにも?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
