      "sourceText": "誰か・どこか・誰も・どこも (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 8: 13/13\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\nか・どこか・\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\nも・どこも\nSomeone, Somewhere, Not anyone, Not anywhere\nThe meaning of these words will change, based on whether the sentence is positive, or negative\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\n誰か・どこか・誰も・どこも\nSomeone, Somewhere, Not anyone, Not anywhere\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nWH-Word\n(1)\n+\nか\n+\nParticle\n(2)\nWH-Word\n(1)\n+\nParticle\n(2)\n+\nも\n(1)\nどこ、\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\n(2)\nへ\n、\nに\n、\nと\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nPronoun\nWord Type\nAdverbial Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout 誰か・どこか・誰も・どこも\nThe constructions\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\nか\n, and\nどこか\nare used when you want to ask, or answer questions about 'someone', or 'somewhere' (positive sentences). Alternatively, 'no one', or 'nowhere' based statements are made with\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\nも\n, and\nどこも\n(negative sentences). Structures like this that use\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\nwere briefly discussed in the\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\ngrammar point.\nUsing\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\n, or\nどこ\nis not limited to the\nか\nand\nも\nparticles.\nへ\n,\nに\n, or\nと\nmay also be partnered with\nか\nor\nも\n, to give a more specific meaning.\nAs can be seen with these examples,\nか\nwill always precede any other particle in a positive sentence. However, the opposite is true for\nも\n.\nも\nwill always follow any other particles in negative sentences. This is because\nか\nwill always attach directly to the noun (when altering the meaning of that noun), but\nも\nalters the whole phrase, not just the noun.\nHowever, It should be noted that\nも\nis not limited to appearing in negative sentences, as can be seen with the final example.\nTopics\nWho Did What Now?!\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about how か and も can be added to question words to turn them into non-specific nouns.\n2m\nRelated\nExpand All\n誰\nだれ\nWho\nN5 Grammar\n誰 is a noun that refers to an unknown person, similar to the English 'who.' A common use is to ask about someone's identity, as in あの人は誰ですか ('Who is that person?') 誰 can also combine with other particles to express concepts like 誰か ('somebody'), 誰の ('whose'), and 誰も ('everyone' or 'no one' when used with a negative).\nなにか・なにも\nなに + か\nSomething, Nothing, Anything, Not anything, Some kind of, Not any kind of\nN5 Grammar\nBoth なにか・なにも and 誰か・どこか・誰も・どこも combine question words (like なに, 誰, どこ) with particles to express ideas such as 'something,' 'nothing,' 'someone,' or 'somewhere.' Their meanings shift depending on whether the sentence is affirmative or negative.\nQuestion-phrase + か\n(Question-Phrase) + か\nIncluding questions within sentences\nN4 Grammar\nThe 誰か・どこか・誰も・どこも pattern combines question words like 誰 ('who') and どこ ('where') with particles to express ideas like 'someone' or 'somewhere.' These forms are also related to how the particle か is used with question phrases in embedded questions. For example, 誰が来るか分かりますか？ means 'Do you know who will come?'\nでも\nOr something,  Any... (with question words), No matter (who)\nN4 Grammar\nThe 誰か・どこか・誰も・どこも pattern combines question words like 誰 ('who') and どこ ('where') with particles to express ideas like 'someone' or 'somewhere.' Similarly, でも can also be attached to question words to express the idea of 'anyone' (誰でも), 'anywhere' (どこでも), and so forth.\nなん + counter + か\nなん + (Counter) + か\nSome, Several, A few\nN4 Grammar\nなん + counter + か is a pattern that combines the question word 何 (なん or なに) with a counter to mean 'a few (A)' or 'some amount of (A)'. For example, 何人か 'a few people.' This is essentially the same as the question word + particle, or 誰か・どこか・誰も・どこも, structure, which is used in expressions such as 何か 'something', 誰か 'someone', and so forth.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n「\nどこ\nかに\n友達\n(\nともだち\n)\nがいる\nのです\nか\n。」\n「\nどこ\nに\nも\nいません\n。」\n'Do you have friends\nsomewhere\n?'\n'I\ndon't\nhave friends\nanywhere\n.'\n「\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\nか\nに\nその\nこと\nを\n言\n(\nい\n)\nい\nました\nか\n。」\n「いいえ、\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\nにも\n言\n(\nい\n)\nっ\nていません\n。」\n'Did you tell\nsomeone\nabout that?'\n'No, I\ndidn't\ntell\nanyone\n.'\n「\nどこ\nかに\nある\nでしょう\n。」\n「\nどこ\nに\nも\nありません\nよ\n。」\n'It is probably\nsomewhere\n.'\n'It is\nnot\nanywhere\n(I'm telling you).'\n「\n紙\n(\nかみ\n)\nを\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\nか\nに\n渡\n(\nわた\n)\nし\nました\nか\n。」\n「\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\nにも\n渡\n(\nわた\n)\nし\nていません\n。」\n'Did you hand off the paper to\nsomeone\n?'\n'I have\nnot\nhanded off the paper to\nanyone\n.'\n「\nどこ\nかへ\n出\n(\nで\n)\nかけ\nます\nか\n。」\n「\nどこ\nへ\nも\n行\n(\nい\n)\nき\nたくない\nよ\n。」\n'Do you want to go\nsomewhere\n?'\n'I\ndon't\nwant to go\nanywhere\n, man.'\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nQuestion Words + も + Negative Form\nWasabi\nUsing question words\nTae Kim\nだれでも vs. だれにも\nMaggie Sensei\nOffline\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 235\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 237\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 101\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 38 [CH 5]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\n誰か・どこか・誰も・どこも – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (19 in total)\nRorroh\nThis grammar point is one of the more confusing ones for me, probably because the it looks like it’s trying to teach one thing but the example sentences look like they’re trying to teach something else.  I think I understand the patterns that it’s trying to convey but only now that I’ve really taken another look at it.\nThe other problem that I’m facing is that I feel like I can answer the reviews “automatically” without really thinking much about it.  I glance at 「にも」 for instance to know the answer is 「かに」 and vice versa, but without a real understanding of what it means, it’s just not sticking for me.\nI’m not sure how either of these issues could be remedied, but perhaps adding in sentences that require to fill in the associated WH-word as well, and some with only the one side without the other?  I’m gonna try adding some self-study sentences to the best of my ability to see how well that helps me.\njesse_dylan\nThis is one of those that I kinda/sorta understood by intuition, yet still only kinda/sorta understand even after the explanation and example sentences.\nThe example sentences all being dialog kind of confused me too; not that it’s even a bad thing, but it seems unusual compared to everything else so far.  We’ll see how I do in reviews.\nsrthom22\nどこかへ食べに行きますか。\nFor this could you also say どこかへ食べに行きたいですか\nGot questions about 誰か・どこか・誰も・どこも?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
