      "sourceText": "～ましょうか (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 9: 2/13\nましょうか\nShall we, Shall I\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\n～ましょうか\nShall we, Shall I\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb［stem］\n+\nましょう\nか\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nExpression\nWord Type\nSentence Ending Particle\nRegister\nPolite\nAbout ～ましょうか\nAs covered in our\nましょう\nlesson,\nましょう\nis one of the conjugations of the\nauxiliary verb\nます, and is a common (polite) way to suggest that something be done 'together'.\nAdding the particle\nか\nto this expression simply changes the nuance from 'let's', to 'shall we?', and is more of a direct question than\nましょう\nby itself.\nLike\nましょう\n, this expression can be added to the ます stem form of any verb.\nOne thing to note with these examples is that they are 'suggestions', not 'invitations'. In Japanese there is a slight difference between the two, in the same way that there is in English. If you want to invite someone to do something, the most natural way would be to use\nませんか\n, a phrase that is similar to\nましょうか\n.\nThe difference here is that 'won't you do (A)?' has a stronger nuance of being a direct invitation, while\nましょうか\nis closer to just 'let's do (A)!'\nTopics\nShall We?\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about how to make invitations and suggestions using different forms of the auxiliary verb ます. Specifically, we'll see the patterns ましょう and ましょうか, used for suggestions, and ませんか, used for invitations. With these, we'll be able to make social plans and soften our statements.\n2m\nRelated\nExpand All\nませんか\nWon't you, Would you not, Why don't we\nN5 Grammar\nましょう and ませんか are both polite forms based on the ます form and used to make suggestions or invitations. ましょう typically suggests doing something together (e.g. 食べましょう = 'Let's eat'), while ませんか invites the listener to join (e.g. 食べませんか = 'Won't you eat [some]?') ましょう is more assertive, while ませんか is softer and more inviting.\nましょう\nLet's, Shall we (Polite volitional)\nN5 Grammar\nBoth ～ましょうか and ましょう are polite expressions used to suggest or propose an action. ましょう is more assertive, similar to 'Let's (do this)' in English. In contrast, ～ましょうか is softer and seeks the listener's approval or agreement, like 'Shall I...?' or 'Shall we...?' ましょう can also function as a gentle command, such as when a teacher says 'Let's be quiet' to mean 'Be quiet.'\nVerb[よう]\nう・よう (Volitional)\nLet's, I shall (Casual volitional)\nN4 Grammar\nましょうか and Verb [よう] are both volitional forms, with ましょうか being polite and Verb [よう] (sometimes with か) casual. ～ましょうか is used to make polite suggestions like 'Shall we...?' (e.g. 行きましょうか = 'Shall we go?') 行こうか is more casual and introspective, often expressing the speaker's internal thoughts (e.g. 'Should I go?')\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n日本語\n(\nにほんご\n)\nで\n話\n(\nはな\n)\nしましょうか\n。\nShall we speak\nin Japanese?\n明日\n(\nあした\n)\nビーチに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nきましょうか\n。\nShall we go\nto the beach tomorrow?\nインド\n料理\n(\nりょうり\n)\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべにいきましょうか\n。\nShall we go and eat\nIndian food?\n９時\n(\nくじ\n)\n前\n(\nまえ\n)\nに\n帰\n(\nかえ\n)\nりましょうか\n。\nShall we go home\nbefore nine o'clock?\n図書館\n(\nとしょかん\n)\nで\n勉強\n(\nべんきょう\n)\nしましょうか\n。\nShall we study\nat the library?\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\n～ましょうか\nE-Japanese\nMaking a motion to do something using the volitional form (polite)\nTae Kim\nましょう and ましょうか\nRenshuu - 2-2\nOffline\nGenki I\nPage 103\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 135 and 154\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 136\nMarugoto Elementary 1 (A2) Rikai\nPage 99\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 152\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 92 [CH 14]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\n～ましょうか – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (1 in total)\nPushindawood\nShouldn’t it also be possible to say it with を?\nYou are absolutely right! I have updated the alternative answers for this sentence. Cheers!\nGot questions about ～ましょうか?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
