      "sourceText": "のがへた (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 10: 5/12\nのが\n下手\n(\nへた\n)\nTo be bad at, Poor at, Unskillful\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nのがへた\nTo be bad at, Poor at, Unskillful\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb +\nの\nが\n+\n下手\n(\nへた\n)\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nExpression\nWord Type\nAdjectival Noun\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout のがへた\n～のが\n下手\n(\nへた\n)\nis a phrase that behaves in almost exactly the same way as\n～のが\n上手\n(\nじょうず\n)\n, but rather than meaning 'to be good at (A)', it means 'to be bad at (A)', (which is a much more handy expression, if you are like me!). This particular expression is used with the plain (dictionary) form of verbs only.\nIn these examples, we can see that there is no difference between Ichidan verbs and Godan verbs. Both will appear in their plain form. In this expression,\nのが\nis performing the same function as in the regular\nのは, and のが\nconstructions. This function is nominalization (turning a phrase into a noun).\nCaution\nIf you want to express that you were bad at something (in the past), the verb will still remain in the plain form, but\n下手\n(\nへた\n)\nwill change to the past tense.\nTopics\nSkill Check\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic we're going to learn about the phrases のが\n下手\n(\nへた\n)\nand のが\n上手\n(\nじょうず\n)\n. These phrases are used to talk about being good or bad at certain things. We will also discuss some other related vocabulary.\n2m\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nのがすき\nのがすき\nLike doing, Love doing\nN5 Grammar\nThese phrases combine the nominalizer の with the な-Adjectives 好き and 下手. They are used to express liking something (好き) or not being good at something (下手). In Japanese culture, boasting is rare, so while 下手 can be used to humbly describe a lack of ability, 好き serves as a more modest alternative to the boastful-sounding 上手.\nのがじょうず\nのがじょうず\nTo be good at, Proficient\nN5 Grammar\nThese two opposite terms are used after verbs to express being good or bad at certain actions. のが上手 is used to say someone is good at something, as in 絵を描くのが上手 ('She is good at drawing'). In contrast, 絵を描くのが下手 means 'She is not good at drawing.' Note that in Japanese culture, it's uncommon to use 上手 to describe yourself, as it can come across as boastful.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\nチェス\nを\nする\nのがへた\nだ\n。\nI'm\nbad at\nchess. (\nunskillful\n)\nカタカナ\nを\n覚\n(\nおぼ\n)\nえ\nる\nのがへた\n。\nI'm\npoor at\nremembering Katakana. (\nunskillful\n)\nサッカー\nを\nする\nのがへた\nです\n。\nI'm\nbad at\nplaying soccer. (\nunskillful\n)\n彼女\n(\nかのじょ\n)\nは\n勉強\n(\nべんきょう\n)\nする\nのがへた\n。\nShe is\nbad at\nstudying. (\nunskillful\n)\n彼\n(\nかれ\n)\nは\n漢字\n(\nかんじ\n)\nを\n書\n(\nか\n)\nく\nのがへた\n。\nHe is\nbad at\nwriting kanji. (\nunskillful\n)\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nAdditional Examples and Listening\nEJapanese\nBad At\nImabi\nOffline\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 194\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 194\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 62 [CH 9]\nみんなの日本語 II\nPage 12 [CH 26]\nみんなの日本語 II\nPage 84 [CH 38]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nのがへた – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (8 in total)\nMelanthe\nWith “I’m\nnot good at\nremembering katakana”, why is\nのがへただ\nnot a correct answer? It only accepts\nのがへた\n, but I thought へた is a な-adjective and therefore だ can be used in casual sentences?\nPushindawood\n@Melanthe Sorry about that! Looks like we were missing the alternative answer for this review question.  Thank you for drawing this to our attention so that we could get it fixed. Cheers!\nMyria\n彼\nかれ\nは、\n自転車\nじてんしゃ\nを\n乗\nの\nる\nのがへた\nだろう。\nI think it should be 自転車に乗る? Or is this a recent development where both を and に are starting to get feasible for 乗る.\nGot questions about のがへた?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
