      "sourceText": "なくちゃ・なきゃ (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 9: 12/13\nなくちゃ・なきゃ\nMust do, Have to do\nCommon abbreviations\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nなくちゃ・なきゃ\nMust do, Have to do\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nVerb［\nない\n］ +\nな\nきゃ\n(1)\n+ いけない\nVerb［\nない\n］ +\nな\nくちゃ+ いけない\n(1)\nなけりゃ\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nExpression\nWord Type\nInformal Speech\nRegister\nCasual\nAbout なくちゃ・なきゃ\nなくちゃ\n(short for\nなくては\n), and\nなきゃ\n(short for\nなければ\n) are the most common colloquial abbreviations used to convey that something 'must', or 'has to' be done.\nLike\nてはいけない\n(must not),\nなくてはいけない\n(must), and\nなくてはならない\n(must), these abbreviation forms can be followed by\nいけない\n,\nならない\n, or だめ. However, they are usually omitted, without changing the meaning.\nStructures like this are referred to as\nspoken/colloquial language\n. These casual variations can come across in a very similar way to words like 'gotta' in English.\nIn these examples, a variety of forms have been used naturally. There is no 'most natural' form, and it will often depend on the speaker as to which they choose.\nAs with almost all grammatical structures, the shortest variations (not including\nいけない\n, etc.) are the most casual/used the most often.\nThere are several other ways that these common abbreviations can appear based on region, or even an individual speaker's preference. An example of this is なけりゃ, which is a (very) casual form of\nなければ\n.\nIn this example, we can see that some of these less common variations are used specifically by certain genders (in general). なけりゃ itself tends to be used by male speakers, and may be considered a bit rougher than\nなきゃ\n, or\nなくちゃ\n, which can be used by anybody.\nTopics\nNeeds Must\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about a variety of ways to say that someone must or must not do something. We'll learn how all these phrases come down to the same core idea, and how they vary based on formality.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nなければいけない\nMust do, Have to do\nN4 Grammar\nBoth なくちゃ・なきゃ and なければいけない express obligation or necessity, meaning something 'must' or 'needs to' be done. なければいけない is more formal and often appears in writing or official contexts, while なくちゃ・なきゃ are casual contractions (of なくては and なければ) used in everyday speech with friends and family.\nなくてはならない\nMust do, Have to do\nN5 Grammar\nなくちゃ, なきゃ, and なくてはならない express obligation, meaning something 'must' or 'needs' to be done. なくてはならない is more formal, while なくちゃ・なきゃ (shortened forms of なくては and the similar なければ) are casual alternatives. なくてはならない is often seen in writing or official statements, while なくちゃ・なきゃ would be used colloquially with friends and family.\nなければならない\nMust do, Have to do\nN4 Grammar\nBoth なくちゃ・なきゃ and なければならない express obligation or necessity, meaning something 'must' or 'needs to' be done. なければならない is more formal and commonly found in writing or official statements. In contrast, なくちゃ・なきゃ are casual contractions (of なくては and なければ) used in everyday speech with friends and family.\nなくてはいけない\nMust do, Have to do\nN5 Grammar\nなくちゃ・なきゃ and なくてはいけない express obligation or necessity, saying that something 'must' or 'needs to' be done. なくてはいけない is more formal and often appears in writing or official contexts. In contrast, なくちゃ・なきゃ are casual contractions (of なくては and なければ) used in everyday speech with friends and family.\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nてはいけない\n～てはいけない\nMust not, May not\nN5 Grammar\nてはいけない expresses prohibition, meaning something 'must not' or 'cannot' be done. In contrast, なくちゃ and なきゃ are casual contractions of なくてはいけない and なければいけない, which express obligation—saying that something 'must' be done. The two forms differ in both formality (formal vs. casual) and meaning ('must not' vs. 'must').\nべきではない\nShould not, Must not , Ought not to\nN3 Grammar\nなくちゃ and なきゃ are casual ways to express obligation, similar to 'I need to…' or 'I have to…' in English. In contrast, べきではない has the opposite meaning—'should not' or 'ought not'—and is more formal. It conveys a sense of societal expectation or what is considered proper, rather than reflecting the speaker's immediate or personal needs.\nRelated\nExpand All\nないと\nMust, Have to\nN4 Grammar\nBoth ないと and なくちゃ・なきゃ express necessity or obligation in casual speech, similar to 'I have to' or 'I need to' in English. They are very close in meaning and formality, and are generally interchangeable in everyday conversation with little to no difference in nuance.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n日本語\n(\nにほんご\n)\nを\n勉強\n(\nべんきょう\n)\nしなくちゃいけない\n。\nYou\nmust study\nJapanese.\n暑\n(\nあつ\n)\nい\nので\n水\n(\nみず\n)\nを\n飲\n(\nの\n)\nまなきゃいけない\n。\nSince it is hot, you\nmust drink\nwater.\nもっと\n文法\n(\nぶんぽう\n)\nを\n勉強\n(\nべんきょう\n)\nしなくちゃいけない\n。\nI\nmust study\nmore grammar.\n買\n(\nか\n)\nいに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nかなきゃいけない\n。\nI\nmust go shopping\n.\n明日\n(\nあした\n)\nテストだ\nから\n、もっと\n勉強\n(\nべんきょう\n)\nしなくちゃいけない\n。\nSince there is a test tomorrow I\nmust study\nmore.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\n〜なきゃ＆〜なくちゃ\nMaggie Sensei\nVarious short-cuts for the lazy\nTae Kim\nOffline\nGenki I\nPage 233\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 273\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 279\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 144\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nなくちゃ・なきゃ – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (35 in total)\nFuga\nHey @Roppo !\nSince なくちゃis an abbreviation for なくては and なきゃ for なければ, they do not have their own past tense forms like ちゃう which is an abbreviation for てしまう! This means that なくちゃand なきゃ needs to be paired with だめ or いけない when conjugating them in the past form.\nHowever, in casual conversation you may here them used without だめ or いけない.\nI hope this answers your question!\nRoppo\nPerfect, thank you very much for the clarification!\nmachinaeZER0\nProbably a dumb question, but I thought きゃ was pronounced “kya” - most/all of the audio examples for this grammar point seem to pronounce it “keh/kay.” Is this just a regional thing?\nGot questions about なくちゃ・なきゃ?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
