      "sourceText": "る-Verb (Dictionary) (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 2: 4/13\nる-Verb (Dictionary)\nる + ます (Ichidan Verbs)\nShort form is also often called 'casual form' (because it is used in casual language), or 'plain form'\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nる-Verb (Dictionary)\nる + ます (Ichidan Verbs)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nExamples:\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nる\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nる\n寝\n(\nね\n)\nる\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nVerb\nWord Type\nIndependent Word\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout る-Verb (Dictionary)\nる-Verbs\nare verbs that have only one possible conjugation pattern. This pattern simply involves the replacement of る with another construction, depending on the tense of the verb (\npast\n,\nnegative\n, etc). This conjugation pattern that only includes る is where the Japanese name Ichidan (\n一段\n(\nいちだん\n)\n) 'one-level' comes from.\nIn these examples, we can see that る is removed from the verb to create the polite ます form. The る form of the verb is often called 'plain form', 'casual form', or 'short form', depending on the source/book.\nAll\nる-Verbs\nend in the kana る, there are no exceptions to this rule.\nCaution\nWhile all\nる-Verbs\nend in る, not all verbs that end in る are\nる-Verbs\n. These verbs can be identified by the る changing, rather than being removed when conjugated.\nAs the る in\n取\n(\nと\n)\nる becomes り in\n取\n(\nと\n)\nります, we can determine immediately that it is not a\nる-Verb\n, but actually an\nう-Verb\n.\nPractice る-Verb (Dictionary) in\nKaijugation\nThe only conjugation game with collateral damage!\nPlay!\nTopics\nGetting Into Action\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to get our first look at Japanese verbs, and learn about two main types. る-Verbs and う-Verbs. Knowing the difference is important, as it affects how they conjugate (change so other words can attach to them).\n4m\nRelated\nExpand All\nVerbs (Non-past)\nVerb (Non-Past)\n(Non-past)\nN5 Grammar\nIn Japanese, the dictionary form and the non-past form of verbs are identical. For example, 食べる is both the dictionary form and the non-past form of the verb 'to eat.' Unlike in English, this form can be used as is to express future actions ('I will eat'), habitual actions ('I regularly eat / I eat everyday'), or general truths ('Lions eat meat').\nう-Verb (Dictionary)\nう + ます (Godan Verbs)\nN5 Grammar\nる-Verbs and う-Verbs are the two main verb types in Japanese. Their dictionary forms are the basic forms used in dictionaries and casual non-past sentences (e.g., 食べる = 'I eat' or 'I will eat'). The difference between these verb types is in their conjugation patterns, not their meaning or nuance. Be careful: not all verbs ending in る are る-Verbs (for example, 帰る 'to return' is actually an う-Verb).\nる-Verb (Negative)\nWill/Does/Do (not)\nN5 Grammar\nThe dictionary form is the base form of the verb, used for habitual or future actions. The negative form expresses that an action doesn't happen or a state doesn't exist. For る-Verbs, the negative is formed by changing る to ない (e.g., 食べる → 食べない). う-Verbs have different conjugation patterns. Be careful: not all verbs ending in る are る-Verbs—for example, 帰る ('to return') is actually an う-Verb.\nう-Verb (Past)\nう - Verbs (Past tense)\nN5 Grammar\nThe dictionary form of verbs is their base form, expressing habitual or future actions. The past form indicates completed actions or past states. The difference between る-Verbs and う-Verbs is how they conjugate into the past, not in their meaning. For example, る-Verbs change る to た (食べる → 食べた), while う-Verbs follow different patterns (読む → 読んだ). Be aware that not all verbs ending in る are る-Verbs—帰る ('to return') is an う-Verb.\nる-Verb (Past)\nる - Verbs (Past tense)\nN5 Grammar\nThe dictionary form of a る-verb is its base form, expressing current, habitual, or future actions (e.g., 食べる = 'I eat' or 'I will eat'). The past form (-た form) describes completed actions or past states (e.g., 食べた = 'I ate'). Both forms are commonly used in casual speech and various grammar patterns requiring plain verb forms, such as ～と思います ('I think').\nVerb + て\n～て (Conjunction)\nAnd, Then (Linking events)\nN5 Grammar\nThe dictionary form of a る-Verb (e.g., 食べる) is its basic, informal form used to describe present or future actions (e.g., 食べる = 'I eat' or 'I will eat'). In contrast, the て form (verb + て) connects verbs, describing sequential actions or appearing in requests and other grammar patterns (e.g., 食べてください = 'Please eat').\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nる\n→\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nます\nTo look\n寝\n(\nね\n)\nる\n→\n寝\n(\nね\n)\nます\nTo go to sleep\n覚\n(\nおぼ\n)\nえ\nる\n→\n覚\n(\nおぼ\n)\nえます\nTo memorize/remember\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべる\n→\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nます\nTo eat\n浴\n(\nあ\n)\nび\nる\n→\n浴\n(\nあ\n)\nびます\nTo wash/shower\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nAeron Buchanan's Japanese Verb Chart\nWikipedia Commons\nConjugation - Skip う-Verbs\nWasabi\nJapanese verb conjugation\nKanji Link\nNegation of masu verbs ません\nJapanese Ammo\nOverview of conjugation patterns\nImabi\nVerb basics - Skip う-Verbs\nTae Kim\nVerbs basics & を particle\nJapanese Ammo\nます Form\nJapanese Ammo\nOffline\nGenki I 1st Edition\nPage  58\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 88\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 86\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 43~47\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 87\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 116 [CH 18]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nる-Verb (Dictionary) – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (9 in total)\nsteffuld\nI’ve never thought about it before, but is it true that ichidan verbs always end in -iru or -eru?\nThis vastly simplifies telling apart ichidan and godan verbs, imo. Should be a ‘fun fact’ or something.\nFuga\nIt is true that ichidan verbs end in -iru or -eru, however there are exceptions and not all verbs that end in -iru and -eru are ichidan verbs.\nFor example 切る ‘kiru’ ends in -iru and 減る ‘heru’ ends in -eru, but these are both godan verbs.\nsharkey\nI came for this same exact question…\nIf I don’t know that vocab I am going off the form there and both are the same meaning conjugated form when I looked it up, so how would I be expected to guess correctly here, and/or is it even important in instances like this? Should this example just be accepting either? Or rather change to a different word if it is specifically trying to test ーる verbs?\nGot questions about る-Verb (Dictionary)?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
