      "sourceText": "ます (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 2: 7/13\nます\nPolite Verb Endings\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nます\nPolite Verb Endings\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb［stem］ +\nます\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nAuxiliary Verb\nWord Type\nDependent Word\nRegister\nPolite\nAbout ます\nIn traditional Japanese teaching, ます is introduced as something placed on the verbs to create the 'polite form'. While this is true, taking a bit of a closer look at what ます is by itself will help us understand the concept more clearly.\nます is an auxiliary verb, which means it is a word that connects to other words in order to change the meaning. For ます, this change occurs on verbs, and creates the polite form.\nTo use ます, attach it to the conjunctive form of the verb.\nBecause most students see ます so early in their learning, the 'conjunctive form' is often simply called the 'ます form', or the 'ます stem'  in many resources, as it may be easier to remember what to do with the verb that way.\nBasically, ます is to verbs what です is to nouns and adjectives. They are both auxiliary verbs, and together they form the basis of polite language in Japanese.\nCaution\nIn general, です and ます will only appear at the end of sentences. However, this is not a strict rule, as they are both occasionally used before conjunction particles such as が 'but' in very polite language.\nFun Fact\nIt is thought that ます originally came from\n参\n(\nまい\n)\nらす 'to humbly do'. Over time it changed to まらす, then まっす, and then finally ます.\n参\n(\nまい\n)\nる itself is a humble verb meaning 'to come' that is still used very frequently in modern Japanese.\nFun Fact\nYou will often hear the imperative form of ます, ませ in various set expressions. We will cover this in a set lesson on ませ!\nPractice ます in\nKaijugation\nThe only conjugation game with collateral damage!\nPlay!\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\n寝\n(\nね\n)\nます\n。\nI will\nsleep\n.\n彼\n(\nかれ\n)\nは\n行\n(\nい\n)\nきます\n。\nHe will\ngo\n.\n僕\n(\nぼく\n)\nも\n遊\n(\nあそ\n)\nびます\n。\nI will\nhang out\ntoo.\n名前\n(\nなまえ\n)\nを\n書\n(\nか\n)\nきます\n。\nI will\nwrite\nmy name.\n明\n(\nあ\n)\nかりを\nつけます\n。\nI will\nturn\nthe light\non\n.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\n〜ます Verb Form\nTofugu\nOffline\nThere are no Offline resources listed for 「ます」.\nYou can\nsubmit a resource for review\n. Resources are constantly updated, come back later to see new additions!\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nます – Grammar Discussion\nThere's currently no discussion for ます\nAsk questions and learn together with other Bunpro users!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
