      "sourceText": "Verb + て (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 5: 9/12\n～て (Conjunction)\nAnd, Then (Linking events)\nている is often shortened to てる. This applies to all tenses, including てる, てて, てた, and even てます\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nVerb + て\nAnd, Then (Linking events)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nExamples:\n［る\n1\n］Verb\n￫\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nる\n+\nて\n［る\n5\n］Verb\n￫\n座\n(\nすわ\n)\nる\n+\nって\n［う］Verb\n￫\n歌\n(\nうた\n)\nう\n+\nって\n［つ］Verb\n￫\n打\n(\nう\n)\nつ\n+\nって\n［く］Verb\n￫\n歩\n(\nある\n)\nく\n+\nいて\n［ぐ］Verb\n￫\n泳\n(\nおよ\n)\nぐ\n+\nいで\n［ぬ］Verb\n￫\n死\n(\nし\n)\nぬ\n+\nんで\n［ぶ］Verb\n￫\n飛\n(\nと\n)\nぶ\n+\nんで\n［む］Verb\n￫\n休\n(\nやす\n)\nむ\n+\nんで\n［す］Verb\n￫\n話\n(\nはな\n)\nす\n+\nして\nExceptions:\n行\n(\nい\n)\nく ￫\n行\n(\nい\n)\nって\nする\n￫\nして\nくる\n￫\nきて\n問\n(\nと\n)\nう ￫\n問\n(\nと\n)\nうて\n請\n(\nこ\n)\nう ￫\n請\n(\nこ\n)\nうて\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nVerb\nWord Type\nConjunctive Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout Verb + て\nIn Japanese,\nて\nis a very important conjunction particle that can be used with many different structures. When it is partnered with a verb, it has special conjugation rules, depending on whether it is being added to a\nる-Verb\nor a\nう-Verb\n. In the case of\nう-Verbs\n, it also depends on what the preceding kana is.\nThe most common translation in any situation is just 'and' or 'then', due to (B) being highlighted as something that happens/happened after (A).\nIn these examples, we can see that\nて\nbasically means '(A)\nて\n(B)' = '(A) happened, then (B)'. However, this is only when it is linked to another verb.\nSometimes the\nて\nform appears as\nで\n. This is a change that happened throughout the course of history, in order to make sentences flow more smoothly. Despite this, there is no difference in meaning between\nて\nand\nで\nas a vocal change.\nThe easiest way to identify whether to use\nて\nor\nで\nis by looking at the preceding kana. If the plain (dictionary) form of the verb finishes in ぐ, ぬ, ぶ, or む, then\nで\nwill be used.\nる-Verbs\nnever use\nで\n.\nCaution\nThere are several irregular verbs when it comes to\nて\nform conjugation. Let's look at an example of each one.\nApart from these 5 verbs, the rules for\nて\nform conjugation are 100% consistent.\nPractice ～て (Conjunction) in\nKaijugation\nThe only conjugation game with collateral damage!\nPlay!\nTopics\nAnd Then...The て-Form\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about the て-form, which is one of the most important and useful patterns in Japanese.\n3m\nRelated\nExpand All\nう-Verb (Dictionary)\nう + ます (Godan Verbs)\nN5 Grammar\nThe dictionary form of an う-verb (e.g., 買う) is its base form, describing non-past actions (e.g., 買う = 'I buy' or 'I will buy'). It's used casually or as part of other grammatical patterns. In contrast, the て-form (verb + て) connects verbs to show sequences or to form requests and various other grammar structures (e.g., 買ってください = 'Please buy...').\nう-Verb (Past)\nう - Verbs (Past tense)\nN5 Grammar\nThe う-Verb (Past) expresses a completed action or one that took place in the past, similar to '-ed' in English. The Verb + て form looks similar but with た replaced by て. It is used to connect actions in chronological order or in other grammar patterns, such as asking a favor (てください) or permission (てもいいか). The て form has no tense; tense is determined by what follows it in the sentence.\nている③\n～ている ③\nHas/have done, Is ~ing (Repetition)\nN5 Grammar\nThe Verb + て form connects verbs or parts of a sentence. One common use of this form is to connect it to the existence verb いる to express continuing or repeated actions, like going to work or going to sleep at the same time every day. Note that the specific nuance of ている can depend on the verb and context (see ている① and ている②).\nる-Verb (Negative)\nWill/Does/Do (not)\nN5 Grammar\nThe negative form of a る-verb expresses that someone does not or will not perform an action (e.g., 食べない = 'I do not eat' or 'I will not eat'). The Verb + て form, on the other hand, connects verbs with other sentence elements, expressing sequences, requests, or permission. For example, 食べて can mean 'Eat, please' in a casual request.\nている①\n～ている ①\nIs, Am, Are (~ing)\nN5 Grammar\nThe Verb + て form is used to connect a verb to another verb, or another part of the sentence. When combined with いる, it describes an ongoing action or state. For example, 食べている means 'I am eating,' and 死んでいる means 'I am dead.' Whether ている conveys an action (like 'is -ing') or a state depends on the verb. In casual speech, ている is often shortened to てる.\nている②\n～ている ②\nState of being, Has (have) done\nN5 Grammar\nThe Verb + て form connects verbs or parts of a sentence. When an 'instantaneous' verb (one that expresses a single change in state) is combined with the existence verb いる in the ている form, it expresses an ongoing state. For example, 死んでいる means '(It) is dead.' With other verbs, ている conveys a meaning similar to the English 'is...-ing' (see ている①).\nVerb + て+ B\n～て (Sequence)\nAnd, And then, After that (sequence)\nN5 Grammar\nThe Verb + て form connects verbs or parts of a sentence. One of the most common uses of this form is to describe actions that occur in sequence, similar to the English 'then' or 'and then.' For example, 水を買って、飲んだ means 'I bought (some) water, and then drank it.'\nVerbs (Non-past)\nVerb (Non-Past)\n(Non-past)\nN5 Grammar\nThe non-past or dictionary form is the base form of a verb, used for habitual or future actions. Meanwhile, the Verb + て form connects verbs, indicating actions in sequence or as part of complex grammar patterns. Note that unlike the non-past form, the Verb + て form has no tense or politeness level on its own, which are instead determined by what follows.\nる-Verb (Dictionary)\nる + ます (Ichidan Verbs)\nN5 Grammar\nThe dictionary form of a る-Verb (e.g., 食べる) is its basic, informal form used to describe present or future actions (e.g., 食べる = 'I eat' or 'I will eat'). In contrast, the て form (verb + て) connects verbs, describing sequential actions or appearing in requests and other grammar patterns (e.g., 食べてください = 'Please eat').\nVerb + てもいい\n～てもいい (Verb)\nIt's okay to, It's alright to, Can/may\nN5 Grammar\nThe Verb + て form is used to connect a verb to another verb, or another part of the sentence. When combined with もいい (meaning 'even' + 'good' or 'okay'), it asks for or grants permission. For example, 食べてもいい？with a question mark means 'Can I eat this?' (literally, 'Is it okay even if I eat this?'), while 食べてもいい with no question mark means 'you can eat it.'\nてから\n～てから\nOnce...is done, Once...happens, After doing\nN5 Grammar\nThe Verb + て form is used to connect a verb to another verb, or another part of the sentence. It's commonly used to list actions in sequence, such as 食べて寝る ('I eat, and (then) sleep'). Adding から after the て form emphasizes the sequence, as in 食べてから寝る ('I eat, and (after that) I will sleep').\nていた\n～ていた\nWas doing ~\nN4 Grammar\nThe Verb + て form connects verbs or parts of a sentence. When combined with いた, it describes a past state. For example, 食べていた means 'I was eating,' and 死んでいた means 'it was dead.' Whether ていた conveys an action ('was -ing') or a state depends on the verb. In casual speech, ていた is often shortened to てた.\n連用形\nれんようけい\nFormal Conjunctive\nN3 Grammar\nBoth 連用形 and the て form connect sequential actions, but 連用形 sounds more formal and is often used in writing. For example, 起きて becomes 起き, e.g. ８時に起きて、朝ご飯を食べました 'I got up at 8 o'clock, then ate breakfast.' → ８時に起き、朝ご飯を食べました. (Same meaning, different register.)\nMisc.\nExpand All\nVerb[て]\n～て (Casual Request)\nPlease do for me (Casual request)\nN4 Grammar\nThe Verb + て form connects verbs or parts of a sentence. It can also be used by itself as a casual request. For example, 食べて would mean 'Please eat [this.]' These casual requests are often followed by the sentence-ending particle ね to further soften them. To express a more polite request, the てください form should be used.\nてくれてありがとう\n～てくれてありがとう\nThank you for ~ ing\nN4 Grammar\nThe Verb + て form connects verbs or parts of a sentence. A common use is to express thanks by combining it with the giving verb くれる (also in the Verb + て form) and ありがとう. For example, 教えてくれてありがとう means 'Thank you for teaching me.' This is a casual form, and a more polite version would use honorific verbs (くださる or いただく) and add ございます.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n（\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nる\n）→\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべて\nTo eat → To eat\nand then\n.\n（\n洗\n(\nあら\n)\nう\n）→\n洗\n(\nあら\n)\nって\nTo wash → To wash\nand then\n.\n（\n返\n(\nかえ\n)\nす\n）→\n返\n(\nかえ\n)\nして\nTo return (an object) → To return (an object)\nand then\n.\n（\n歩\n(\nある\n)\nく\n）→\n歩\n(\nある\n)\nいて\nTo walk → To walk\nand then\n.\n（\n泳\n(\nおよ\n)\nぐ\n）→\n泳\n(\nおよ\n)\nいで\nTo swim → To swim\nand then\n.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nAeron Buchanan's Japanese Verb Chart\nWikipedia Commons\nBrief Japanese - history of て form, why is it so irregular and why の can follow and all its uses explained.\nBriefJapanese [cross-posted to Reddit]\nExpressing a sequence of verbs with the te-form\nTae Kim\nMaking requests, linking sentences, & more\nKanji Link\nThe Conjunctive Particle て I: The Affirmative\nImabi\nVideos #13 ~ #17 cover all forms\nJapanese Ammo\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 464\nGenki 1 3rd Edition\nPage 150\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 150\nMarugoto Elementary 1 (A2) Rikai\nPage 56, 65\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 92 [CH 14]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nVerb + て – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (44 in total)\nRadicalC\nI having bit of trouble distinguishing some of the て grammar because they overlap sometimes. are we sure 1 or 2 of them may not be redundant? thank you\n１て・で  sequence (N5)\n２～て (Conjunction) (N5)\n３て・で (Adjectives and Nouns) (N5)\n４て・で (Qualities and States) (N5)\n５て・で (Reasons and Causes) (N4)\n６～て・で (Non-Sequence) (N4)\n７～て・で (Casual request) (N4)\nAsher\nて is a grammatical structure in Japanese that has very broad usage. Although they all stem from the same thing, the various uses are often taught individually across both N5 and N4, just to give learners enough exposure to start to develop an intuition for it.\n@additionalramen sorry about the super late reply, it looks like your question was never answered! . If you’re just reading the translation, I can definitely see where you are coming from. Do you think maybe a translation like ‘To eat and then ~.’ would be better? To show that something would usually follow next.\nadditionalramen\nLOL I totally forgot about this! I think I get now that the point of the exercises in this grammar point is just to practice the conjugation. However, I still find it odd that the sentences in the “About Verb + て” section are full sentences while the practice sentences are fragments. My personal preference is to practice the grammar points in the context of full sentences to see how they are used IRL. And most of the Bunpro grammar points do provide full sentences to practice with for the cloze-style reviews, so I’m not sure why this one would be an exception.\nGot questions about Verb + て?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
