      "sourceText": "じゃない (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 6: 8/17\nじゃない\nIs not, Isn't\nCannot be used with, い-Adjectives\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nじゃない\nIs not, Isn't\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\n［な］Adjective\n+\nでは\n(1)\n+ ない\nNoun +\nでは\n(1)\n+ ない\n(1)\nじゃ\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nVerb\nWord Type\nAuxiliary Verb\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout じゃない\nじゃない\nis the casual form of\nではない\n. では is regularly contracted to じゃ in Japanese, so is worth remembering as its own structure.\nじゃない\n(casual), or\nではない\n(semi-polite) are the structures used for creating the negative form of nouns and\nな-Adjectives\n.\nThese structures are simply added to the base form of the noun or\nな-Adjective\n.\nじゃ and では can also be used with the negative ます form of\nある\n, to create the polite variant.\nない\nis sometimes said/written as\nね\nor\nねえ\n. However, this is considered very casual, or even a bit rough. Accordingly, it is best avoided in most conversations.\nCaution\nじゃない\nis used very frequently to ask questions. This is similar to\nじゃないか\n, a grammar point covered in another lesson. However, the nuance difference is that\nじゃない\nas a question is a little bit more blunt, like 'isn't it?' in English. On the other hand,\nじゃないか\nis more like 'don't you think?'.\nWhen\nじゃない\nis used as a question, it will usually be obvious, due to a rising tone.\nPractice じゃない in\nKaijugation\nThe only conjugation game with collateral damage!\nPlay!\nTopics\nIt Was Not the Best of Times\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn how to talk about nouns and な-Adjectives in the negative ('is not'), and how to turn them, and い-Adjectives, into the negative past ('was not'). As usual, there is a difference between the polite and standard forms. This means we will be able to say that something 'is not' and something 'was not'.\n5m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nる-Verb (Negative)\nWill/Does/Do (not)\nN5 Grammar\nThe negative form of a る-verb indicates 'does not' or 'will not' do something (e.g., 食べない = 'I don't eat' or 'I won't eat'). In contrast, じゃない (a casual form of ではない, the negative of だ) negates nouns and な-adjectives (e.g., 元気じゃない = 'not feeling good'). While both express negation, they apply to different types of words.\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nだ\nTo be, Is\nN5 Grammar\nだ follows nouns and な-adjectives, with a meaning similar to the English 'is.' For instance, これはペンだ translates to 'This is a pen.' On the other hand, じゃない (a contraction of ではない) is used to create the negative of such expressions. For example, これはペンじゃない translates to 'This isn't a pen.'\nです\nTo be, Is\nN5 Grammar\nです works like 'is' or 'to be' in English and is used to politely state something in everyday conversation. じゃない is the casual negative form meaning 'is not,' and it's often used in relaxed speech or in phrases like ～じゃないと思います ('I don't think it is...').\nRelated\nExpand All\nじゃないか\nIsn't it?, Is it not?\nN4 Grammar\nじゃない follows a noun or な-Adjective to express 'is not' or 'are not.' じゃないか, with the addition of か, changes the meaning to 'Isn't it...?' as in 本じゃない ('It isn't a book') vs. 本じゃないか ('It's a book, isn't it?') While じゃない is used only after nouns or な-Adjectives, じゃないか can follow い-Adjectives and verbs as well.\nじゃなかった\nWas not, Wasn't\nN5 Grammar\nじゃない and じゃなかった both follow nouns or な-adjectives to express the negative, but differ in tense. じゃない is present tense ('is not'), while じゃなかった is past tense ('was not'). For example, 静かじゃない means 'It isn't quiet,' while 静かじゃなかった means 'It wasn't quiet.'\nい-Adjectives くない\nくない\nNot (Adjective)\nN5 Grammar\nい-Adjectives form a negative by replacing い with くない (e.g., 赤い = 'it's red,' 赤くない = 'it's not red'). For nouns and な-Adjectives, the negative is formed by adding じゃない (e.g., 大変じゃない = 'it's not difficult,' バナナじゃない = 'it's not a banana'). Both forms are plain and used in casual conversation or other grammatical patterns.\nMisc.\nExpand All\nう-Verb (Negative)\nWill/Does/Do (not)\nN5 Grammar\nThe negative form of an う-verb indicates 'does not' or 'will not' perform an action (e.g., 買わない = 'I don't buy' or 'I won't buy'). On the other hand, じゃない (casual form of ではない, the negative of だ) negates nouns and な-adjectives (e.g., 元気じゃない = 'not feeling good'). Both express negation, but each applies to different word types.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n犬\n(\nいぬ\n)\nじゃない\n。\nIt\nis not\na dog.\n先生\n(\nせんせい\n)\nじゃない\n。\nI\nam not\na teacher.\n牛肉\n(\nぎゅうにく\n)\nじゃない\nです\n。\nIt\nis not\nbeef.\nこの\n絵\n(\nえ\n)\nは\n、\n綺麗\n(\nきれい\n)\nじゃありません\n。\nThis painting\nis not\nbeautiful.\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\nな\nスポーツ\nは\n、サッカー\nではありません\n。\nThe sport that I like\nis not\nsoccer.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nConjugating to the negative state-of-being\nTaeKim\nNegative Adjectives\nJapanese Ammo\nNegative State-of-Being\nTae Kim\nNoun Negation\nJapanese Ammo\nReview\nImabi\nじゃないです vs. じゃありません\nJapanese StackExchange\nOffline\nGenki I 1st Edition\nPage 37\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 65\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 64\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 31\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 89\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 301\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 14 [CH 1]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nじゃない – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (10 in total)\nnekoyama\nIt’s not refused. I tested it. I assumed you made the same typo you edited out of your earlier post.\nIf the example sentence uses じゃ it will display that as the solution even when you answer では, but it will not refuse では.\ngael\nWeird. I don’t think I made the typo, but let’s give that as the answer.\nI just had a restest now, and it was accepted, so your explanation is the simplest one.\nThanks!\nreeddragon\nHello, the instructions for this topic explain that じゃない is constructed from では (>じゃ) + ない or +ありません. But what is では? There is not explanation of this particle or where it comes from and I don’t believe it has been covered before.\nGot questions about じゃない?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
