      "sourceText": "です (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 1: 2/12\nです\nTo be, Is\nUnlike だ, です is often used with both い-Adjectives and な-Adjectives\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nです\nTo be, Is\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nNoun +\nです\nAdjective +\nです\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nAuxiliary Verb\nWord Type\nDependent Word\nRegister\nPolite\nAbout です\nSimilarly to\nだ\n,\nです\nis an\nauxiliary verb\nthat is an expression of determination or assertion. It states that something 'is' a certain way.\nです\nis considered to be the polite variation of\nだ\n. Keep in mind though that although their roles are similar, both\nだ\nand\nです\nare standalone structures.\nです\nconnects to words in exactly the same way as\nだ\n, but is also regularly seen attached to the end of\nい-Adjectives\n.\nCaution\nAlthough\nです\nis regularly attached to the end of\nい-Adjectives\n, some may actually consider this incorrect Japanese, and it should not be used in formal writing. In daily spoken language/texting, however, it is completely normal.\nThe above example technically should not be used in formal writing. Despite this, in recent years, it is starting to become the norm.\nTopics\nWelcome to Bunpro! 文プロへようこそ！\nN5 Topic\nWelcome to the start of the Bunpro learning path! The goal of this first Topic is to learn how to make declarative statements using the Japanese equivalents of ‘is’. This is fundamental to almost any language, and is achieved in Japanese through the use of だ and です.\n2m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nだ\nTo be, Is\nN5 Grammar\nだ and です both function like the English 'is.' The main difference is politeness: だ is casual and used in plain speech, while です is polite. だ can't be used after い-adjectives, but です can, acting as a general politeness marker. You may still see だ in set expressions like ～だと思います, even in polite sentences.\nでございます\nでございます\nTo be, Polite copula\nN4 Grammar\nです and でございます are both polite forms of 'is' or 'to be.' です is polite but commonly used in everyday conversation. でございます is extremely polite and formal, used in customer service, ceremonial speech, or other situations requiring a high level of respect.\nである\nFormal or literary だ, Authoritative, Copula\nN3 Grammar\nです is often used as the polite form of だ, which is similar in meaning to 'is' or 'to be' in English. It's polite but not overly formal, and it's often used in both speech and writing. In contrast, である is a formal (but not polite) expression mainly used in writing or formal speeches. One key difference is that である can appear in front of nouns in modifying clauses, like in 日本人である留学生 ('an exchange student who is Japanese').\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nじゃない\nIs not, Isn't\nN5 Grammar\nです works like 'is' or 'to be' in English and is used to politely state something in everyday conversation. じゃない is the casual negative form meaning 'is not,' and it's often used in relaxed speech or in phrases like ～じゃないと思います ('I don't think it is...').\nRelated\nExpand All\n～んです・のです\nんです・のです\nExplanatory, Emphasis, The fact is\nN5 Grammar\n～んです・のです are used to clarify context, provide background information, give reasons, or express curiosity. In contrast, です without の or ん is a simple declarative form used to state facts or general statements. Using ～んです・のです adds emphasis, conveys intention, or creates a more explanatory tone by suggesting a larger context.\nがある\nがある\nTo be, There is\nN5 Grammar\nThe phrase ～がある is used to show existence or possession. For example, 本がある means 'There is a book' or 'I have a book.' On the other hand, です is used to identify something, like 本です ('It is a book'). がある is more casual and often used with close friends or in certain grammar patterns, while です is polite.\nだった・でした\nWas, Were (Past tense)\nN5 Grammar\nです and だった・でした are all forms of 'is' or 'to be,' but they show different tenses. です is used for the present or future, like in あれは猫です ('That is a cat'). だった and でした are past tense—だった is casual, and でした is polite. For example, あれは猫だった means 'That was a cat.'\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nあなた\nです\n。\nIt\nis\nyou.\nペン\nです\n。\nIt\nis\na pen.\nさくら\nです\n。\n(I)\nam\nSakura.\n友達\n(\nともだち\n)\nです\n。\n(He/she)\nis\na friend.\n暑\n(\nあつ\n)\nい\nです\n。\nIt\nis\nhot.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nHow to use です\n80/20 Japanese\nThe Grammaticality of Politeness Marker ～です\nImabi\nThe Japanese version of \"to be\" or \"is\"\nMiageru\nUsing 「です」 for everything else\nTae Kim\nは and です\nJapanese Ammo\nOffline\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 42\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 41\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 88\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 14 [CH 1]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nです – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (6 in total)\nnekoyama\nI just read it as half-baked I think, just throwing around a few random quotes from dictionaries.\nI agree on bunpro’s wording being a bit too strict, in particular the claim that it doesn’t appear in literature is just incorrect. And people use it in other writing too, in particular informal channels that are close to spoken language anyway like Twitter.\ncoffee126\nPretty late jumping in here, but if い-adjective plus です is considered incorrect, what\nis\nconsidered correct?\nFuga\nHey @coffee126 !\nTo make い-adjectives polite, people used ございます. For example, 楽しいです was 楽しゅうございます, and 美味しいです was 美味しゅうございます. However, because people thought this was too long and exaggerated for daily conversation, people started to use い-adjectives + です instead. Due to this, in 1952 the Japanese Language Council stated that い-adjective + です is acceptable. However, because this is a recent change, some people still consider it incorrect and think that it sounds unnatural.\nI hope this answers your question!\nGot questions about です?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
