      "sourceText": "う-Verb (Negative-Past) (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 5: 8/12\nう-Verb (Negative-Past)\nう - Verbs (Negative-past tense)\nWhen a past negative sentence has a connection with the present, for example, something was expected to happen but has not happened for the moment, the verb[て] + いません construction is used instead\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nう-Verb (Negative-Pas...\nう - Verbs (Negative-past tense)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\n座\n(\nすわ\n)\nる\n+\nら\nなかった\n歌\n(\nうた\n)\nう\n+\nわ\nなかった\n歩\n(\nある\n)\nく\n+\nか\nなかった\n話\n(\nはな\n)\nす\n+\nさ\nなかった\n打\n(\nう\n)\nつ\n+\nた\nなかった\n死\n(\nし\n)\nぬ\n+\nな\nなかった\n飛\n(\nと\n)\nぶ\n+\nば\nなかった\n休\n(\nやす\n)\nむ\n+\nま\nなかった\n泳\n(\nおよ\n)\nぐ\n+\nが\nなかった\nExceptions:\nある ￫\nなかった\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nVerb\nWord Type\nAuxiliary Verb\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout う-Verb (Negative-Past)\nIn order to make the negative past form of\nう-Verbs\n, you will need to change the last kana of the verb into the あ sound kana from the same column (or わ if the original kana was う). After this, the あ sound kana will be followed by\nない\n(or ます), conjugated into their past forms. This will either be\nなかった\n(casual), or\nませんでした\n(polite).\nFor the\nませんでした\n(polite) form, rather than the final kana changing to the あ sound kana from the same column, it will change to the い sound kana from the same column.\nThe negative past form is used to convey that something 'did not happen', or 'was not'.\nA semi-polite form may also be used. This is constructed by using\nなかった\n, and then simply adding\nです\n. This form is not grammatically correct, but is so common that most sources (and native speakers) would consider it correct.\nある\nis an exception to the regular conjugation rules of\nう-Verbs\n. The casual past-negative is not あらなかった, but simply\nなかった\n. However, the polite past-negative form conjugates in the same way as would be expected for う-Verbs.\nありませんでした\n.\nPractice う-Verb (Negative-Past) in\nKaijugation\nThe only conjugation game with collateral damage!\nPlay!\nTopics\nI Didn't Do It\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn how to make the negative-past form of verbs. As usual, the polite and the standard forms are different. Upon learning this, we will have covered the complete set of tenses (past and non-past) and polarities (positive and negative) for verbs.\n3m\nRelated\nExpand All\nう-Verb (Past)\nう - Verbs (Past tense)\nN5 Grammar\nThese two forms are used with う-Verbs to express the idea of having done or not having done a particular action in the past. The difference is whether the meaning is affirmative ('did') or negative ('did not'). For example, 買った means 'I bought,' while 買わなかった means 'I did not buy.' Both are plain forms used in casual speech or as part of other grammatical forms.\nる-Verb (Negative-Past)\nる - Verbs (Negative-past tense)\nN5 Grammar\nBoth forms express the negative past ('did not'), but the formation differs depending on whether the verb is a る-Verb or う-Verb. For example, 食べなかった (for 食べる, a る-Verb) means 'I did not eat,' while 買わなかった (for 買う, a う-Verb) means 'I did not buy.' Both are plain forms used in casual speech or other grammatical patterns.\nう-Verb (Negative)\nWill/Does/Do (not)\nN5 Grammar\nThese forms express the negative ('does not' or 'will not') of う-Verbs, with a difference in tense. The う-Verb (Negative) expresses a lack of future or habitual action (e.g., 買わない = 'I don't buy' or 'I won't buy'). The う-Verb (Negative-Past) expresses a lack of past action (e.g., 買わなかった = 'I didn't buy'). Both are plain forms used in casual speech or other grammatical patterns.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n昨日\n(\nきのう\n)\n、\n行\n(\nい\n)\nかなかった\n。\n昨日\n(\nきのう\n)\n、\n行\n(\nい\n)\nかなかった\nです\n。\n昨日\n(\nきのう\n)\n、\n行\n(\nい\n)\nきませんでした\n。\nI\ndidn't go\nyesterday.\n遊\n(\nあそ\n)\nば\nなかった\n。\n遊\n(\nあそ\n)\nば\nなかった\nです\n。\n遊\n(\nあそ\n)\nびませんでした\n。\nI\ndidn't hang out\n.\nはなこさん\nは\n話\n(\nはな\n)\nさなかった\n。\nはなこさん\nは\n話\n(\nはな\n)\nさなかった\nです\n。\nはなこさん\nは\n話\n(\nはな\n)\nしませんでした\n。\nHanako\ndidn't talk\n.\n彼女\n(\nかのじょ\n)\nは\n事故\n(\nじこ\n)\nで\n死\n(\nし\n)\nななかった\n！\n彼女\n(\nかのじょ\n)\nは\n事故\n(\nじこ\n)\nで\n死\n(\nし\n)\nななかった\nです\n！\n彼女\n(\nかのじょ\n)\nは\n事故\n(\nじこ\n)\nで\n死\n(\nし\n)\nにませんでした\n！\nShe\ndidn't die\nin the accident!\nそれも\n知\n(\nし\n)\nらなかった\n。\nI\ndidn’t know\nthat either.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nPast-negative tense for all verbs\nTae Kim's Guide to Learning Japanese\nAeron Buchanan's Japanese Verb Chart\nWikipedia Commons\nGodan Verbs 五段活用動詞\nImabi\nInformal Past Negative form of Verbs\nJapanese Ammo\nPast Tense and Present Perfect Tense\nWasabi\nOffline\nGenki I 2nd Edition\n110 & 212\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 214\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\n87 & 52\nTry N5\n39 & 101\nみんなの日本語 I, Translation & Grammatical Notes\n34\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nう-Verb (Negative-Past) – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (15 in total)\nhoma\nI struggle with the way bunpro gives me a solution here.\ndiscopatrick\nFor me, at the beginning at least, I think this is fine if you view the card as teaching you that there is a pattern between the three ways of saying it.\nHowever, as my understanding progresses, I would expect to be challenged to recall each style individually without having the hint of the others being present. If there are no cards where BunPro does this, then that sounds like a gap in the curriculum to me.\nhoma\nYe, after posting this I realized that it’s not that big of a deal if you go beyond first ~50-100 topics as you see this latter everywhere, a lot of conjugations are based on it. So the only problem is to remember exceptions for い and え rule\nGot questions about う-Verb (Negative-Past)?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
