      "sourceText": "Verbs (Non-past) (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 6: 13/17\nVerb (Non-Past)\n(Non-past)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nVerbs (Non-past)\n(Non-past)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nExamples:\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nる\n洗\n(\nあら\n)\nう\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nVerb\nWord Type\nDictionary Form\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout Verbs (Non-past)\nIn Japanese, the\nnon-past\nform of verbs is identical to the dictionary form/casual form. Unlike English, this form of verbs is able to be used in several cases where conjugation/different constructions would be required in English.\nIn these examples, we can see the three forms of English verbs that are covered by the single\nnon-past\nform of Japanese verbs. These are verbs ending with -s (sees, hears, goes), verbs requiring will (will see, will hear, will go), and verbs that do not require conjugation (see, hear, go).\nDue to this one form of verbs covering several different nuances, it is often important to use expressions of time in the sentence, so that the meaning becomes clear.\nIf there is no word distinguishing 'time' in the sentence, the nuance will be that of stating a general fact about something that is true at any time.\nFrom all of these examples, we can see that the non-past form can be thought of as expressing one of three basic ideas. General facts, habits, or future events.\nCaution\nIn books, the\nnon-past\nform will regularly be used for events that are happening 'in the moment', but without any time word being used. In these cases it is usually obvious from context that it is happening then and there.\nTopics\nGetting Past It\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn more about Japanese tenses. Specifically, we're going to learn how, in reality, Japanese only has a past and non-past tense. One form is all we need for multiple different meanings!\n3m\nRelated\nExpand All\nる-Verb (Dictionary)\nる + ます (Ichidan Verbs)\nN5 Grammar\nIn Japanese, the dictionary form and the non-past form of verbs are identical. For example, 食べる is both the dictionary form and the non-past form of the verb 'to eat.' Unlike in English, this form can be used as is to express future actions ('I will eat'), habitual actions ('I regularly eat / I eat everyday'), or general truths ('Lions eat meat').\nう-Verb (Negative)\nWill/Does/Do (not)\nN5 Grammar\nThe non-past or dictionary form is the base form of a verb, used to express habitual or future actions To express the negative, change the final kana and add ない (e.g., 買わない = 'I do not buy' or 'I will not buy'). Both the non-past and negative forms are plain forms used in casual conversation or other grammar patterns.\nる-Verb (Past)\nる - Verbs (Past tense)\nN5 Grammar\nThe non-past (or dictionary) form expresses habitual or future actions, while the past form expresses completed actions or past states. For る-Verbs, change the final る to た to form the past tense. Both the non-past and past forms are casual, non-polite forms used in conversation or other grammar patterns.\nる-Verb (Negative)\nWill/Does/Do (not)\nN5 Grammar\nThe non-past (or dictionary) form is the base form of a verb, used to express habitual or future actions (e.g., 食べる = 'I eat' or 'I will eat'). To express the negative, use the negative form. For most える/いる-Verbs, change the final る to ない (e.g., 食べない = 'I do not eat' or 'I will not eat'). Both the non-past and negative forms are plain forms used in casual conversation or other grammar patterns.\nVerb + て\n～て (Conjunction)\nAnd, Then (Linking events)\nN5 Grammar\nThe non-past or dictionary form is the base form of a verb, used for habitual or future actions. Meanwhile, the Verb + て form connects verbs, indicating actions in sequence or as part of complex grammar patterns. Note that unlike the non-past form, the Verb + て form has no tense or politeness level on its own, which are instead determined by what follows.\nう-Verb (Dictionary)\nう + ます (Godan Verbs)\nN5 Grammar\nIn Japanese, the dictionary form and the non-past form of verbs are identical. For example, 買う is both the dictionary form and the non-past form of the verb 'to buy.' Unlike in English, this form can be used as is to express future actions ('I will buy'), habitual actions ('I regularly buy'), or general truths ('People buy a lot around Christmas').\nう-Verb (Past)\nう - Verbs (Past tense)\nN5 Grammar\nThe non-past (or dictionary) form of a verb expresses habitual or future actions, while the past form indicates completed actions or past states. For う-Verbs, the last kana changes before adding た (or だ) to form the past tense. Both forms are plain and used in casual conversation or other grammar patterns.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n鳥\n(\nとり\n)\nは\n飛\n(\nと\n)\nぶ\n。\n鳥\n(\nとり\n)\nは\n飛\n(\nと\n)\nびます\n。\nBirds\nfly\n. (\nthe verb is used as a general truth\n)\nロック\nを\n時々\n(\nときどき\n)\n聞\n(\nき\n)\nく\n。\nロック\nを\n時々\n(\nときどき\n)\n聞\n(\nき\n)\nきます\n。\nI\nlisten\nto rock sometimes. (\nhabit\n)\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\n九\n(\nく\n)\n時\n(\nじ\n)\nに\nバナナ\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべる\n。\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\n九\n(\nく\n)\n時\n(\nじ\n)\nに\nバナナ\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべます\n。\nI\nwill eat\na banana at 9 o'clock. (\nfuture event\n)\nヴァンパイア\nは\n血\n(\nち\n)\nを\n飲\n(\nの\n)\nむ\n。\nヴァンパイア\nは\n血\n(\nち\n)\nを\n飲\n(\nの\n)\nみます\n。\nVampires\ndrink\nblood. (\ngeneral truth\n)\nたなか\nさん\nは\n毎週\n(\nまいしゅう\n)\n車\n(\nくるま\n)\nを\n洗\n(\nあら\n)\nう\n。\nたなか\nさん\nは\n毎週\n(\nまいしゅう\n)\n車\n(\nくるま\n)\nを\n洗\n(\nあら\n)\nいます\n。\nTanaka-san\nwashes\nthe car every week. (\nhabit\n)\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nThere are no Online resources listed for 「Verbs (Non-past)」.\nYou can\nsubmit a resource for review\n. Resources are constantly updated, come back later to see new additions!\nOffline\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 87\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nVerbs (Non-past) – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (3 in total)\nFoguinho3007\nI didn’t understand one thing about this topic, that means tha those verbs doenst’t have past form? Or just doesn’t need it?\nBreadmanNin\nWhen a verb is referred to as non-past, it simply means that it is not in the past form. All verbs have a past form and a non-past form. The non-past form can be used for various purposes, most commonly for things that are happening now or things that will happen in the future.\nFor example:\nパンを食べる。This can mean “I eat bread” or “I will eat bread”.\n店へ行く。 This can mean “I go to the shop” or “I will go to the shop.”\nThis is called the non-past form.\nThese verbs (all verbs) also have a past form:\nパンを食べた。I ate bread.\n店へ行った。I went to the shop.\nThis is called the past form or the た-form.\nWhen doing reviews, sometimes you’ll see that Bunpro gives you a hint saying “non-past”. That’s a hint to tell you you should use the non-past form of the verb.\nFoguinho3007\nTy, it was much easier to understand with these examples\nGot questions about Verbs (Non-past)?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
