      "sourceText": "ている③ (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 10: 2/12\n～ている ③\nHas/have done, Is ~ing (Repetition)\nている is often shortened to てる. This applies to all tenses, including てる, てて, てた, and even てます\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nている③\nHas/have done, Is ~ing (Repetition)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nVerb［て］\n+\nいる\nVerb［て］\n+\nる\nVerb［\nて\n］ +\nと\nる\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nConjunctive Particle\nWord Type\nVerb\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout ている③\nThe 3rd most common meaning (in no specific order) that\nている\nis used for in Japanese, is the repetition of common activities. This is for things that are not 'continuing', but happen so often that they are considered to be continuous (like going to work, having a hobby, and similar things).\nDue to\nいる\nbeing one of the verbs for 'existing' in Japanese, the\nている\nform is closer to 'continuing to exist in a specific state', rather than (A)~ing in English. In this construction, it can be thought of as 'to be existing in the state of performing a regular activity'.\nCaution\nWith this meaning of\nている\n, to make it easier to understand, expressions of time are often used. This can be things like\n毎日\n(\nまいにち\n)\n,\n毎晩\n(\nまいばん\n)\n,\n毎週\n(\nまいしゅう\n)\n, and similar phrases.\nFun Fact\nRegularly in novels, manga, and specifically some parts of Japan,\nとる\nmay be used instead of\nている\nwith the same meaning.\nTopics\nHigh Tension\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn more phrases to do with experience and tense. Specifically, we will learn about the pattern ～たことがある, which is used to talk about things we 'have done', and about the final use of ている, which highlights habitual or repeated activities. With these, we'll have a complete knowledge of tense and aspect at the N5 level!\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nている①\n～ている ①\nIs, Am, Are (~ing)\nN5 Grammar\nThe ている form describes an ongoing action or state. In the ている① form, it expresses something you are currently doing, like 食べている ('I am eating'). In the ている③ form, it refers to a habitual or repeated action, even if you're not currently doing said action, often paired with a time word. For example, 毎朝３０分走っている means 'I run every morning for 30 minutes.'\nRelated\nExpand All\nVerb + て\n～て (Conjunction)\nAnd, Then (Linking events)\nN5 Grammar\nThe Verb + て form connects verbs or parts of a sentence. One common use of this form is to connect it to the existence verb いる to express continuing or repeated actions, like going to work or going to sleep at the same time every day. Note that the specific nuance of ている can depend on the verb and context (see ている① and ている②).\nVerb［た・ている］+ Noun\n～た + (Noun)\nVerb modified noun, Relative clause\nN5 Grammar\nThe ている① form expresses completed actions or past states. Unlike English, verbs can directly modify nouns, similar to い-Adjectives. For example, 行っている学校 means 'the school I am going to.' However, when describing a noun's state, the past form is often preferred over ている. For example, 死んだネズミ ('a dead mouse') is more common than 死んでいるネズミ.\nている②\n～ている ②\nState of being, Has (have) done\nN5 Grammar\nている expresses an ongoing action or state. The ている② form is used with verbs indicating a single change in state, like 死んでいる ('is dead'), not an action. The ている③ form describes repeated or habitual actions, like 会社で働いている could mean ('I'm working for [that/this] company).\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n俺\n(\nおれ\n)\nは\n毎週\n(\nまいしゅう\n)\n金曜日\n(\nきんようび\n)\nの\n夜\n(\nよる\n)\nに\nスーパーに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nっている\nのだ\n。\nI go\nto the supermarket every Friday night. (I\nam going\n...)\nふうたろうさん\nは\n毎日\n(\nまいにち\n)\nみくさん\nと\n一緒\n(\nいっしょ\n)\nに\n歴史\n(\nれきし\n)\nを\n勉強\n(\nべんきょう\n)\nしている\n。\nFuutarou-san\nstudies\nhistory every day with Miku. (Fuutarou-san\nis studying\n...)\n魔王\n(\nまおう\n)\nさま\nは\nバーガークイーン\nで\n働\n(\nはたら\n)\nいています\n。\nThe Demon Lord\nworks\nat Burger Queen. (The Demon Lord\nis working\n...)\nこの\n公園\n(\nこうえん\n)\nで\nは\n毎週末\n(\nまいしゅうまつ\n)\n多\n(\nおお\n)\nくの\n人\n(\nひと\n)\nが\n走\n(\nはし\n)\nっています\n。\nMany people\nrun\nin this park every weekend. (Many people\nare running\n...)\nどうしてスティーブン\nは\nいつも\n黒\n(\nくろ\n)\nい\nタートルネック\nを\n着\n(\nき\n)\nている\nのです\nか\n。\nWhy does Steven always\nwear\na black turtleneck? (\nis wearing\n)\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nEnduring state-of-being vs enduring state of action\nTae Kim\nHow to use ている\nMaggie Sensei\nている uses explained\nBriefJapanese\nOffline\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 117\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nている③ – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (11 in total)\nsemanticman\nI’m having trouble understanding the difference between when the “repetition of common activities” use of ている is different than the “habitual” sense of the basic non-past form.\nIn this lesson, we see the example:\n毎晩\nまいばん\n９\n時\nじ\nに\n寝\nね\nている。\nI sleep at 9 every night. (This is a habit of the speaker)\nWhile in the lesson for non-past, we see:\n嫁\nよめ\nは\n毎晩\nまいばん\n映画\nえいが\nを\n見\nみ\nます\n。\nMy wife watches movies every night.\nAnd many other examples seem equivalent.\nている\n#3\nlesson example:\nばあさんは\n毎晩\nまいばん\n、\n薬\nくすり\nを\n飲\nの\nんど\nる\n。\nGrandmother takes medicin...\ndiddy_dante\nFor:\nひとみさんのお母さんは教師をしている。\nHitomi’s mother is a teacher. (Hitomi’s mother is doing…)\nwould just ひとみさんのお母さんは教師です not also be fine? Is the usage of している significantly more common?\nAnd also (I’m genuinely serious), regarding\n“をやっている can also be used in this case, but it sounds more casual (and might sound a bit rude)”\nConsidering やる is also slang for to have sex (according to dictionary (JMdict)), how would you differentiate that Hitomi’s mother is a teacher vs Hitomi’s mother is\ndoing\na teacher?\nsemanticman\nThis is still unanswered\nGot questions about ている③?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
