      "sourceText": "な (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 6: 17/17\nな (Prohibitive)\nDo not, Don't (Prohibitive)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nな\nDo not, Don't (Prohibitive)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb +\nな\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nSentence Ending Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout な\nな\nis classified as a\nsentence ending particle\nin Japanese. When attached to the base (dictionary) form of a verb, it has the nuance of strongly demanding that someone 'not' do something.\nTopics\nの, Don't!\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn how to strongly forbid people from doing things using な, and we'll also learn how to shorten our sentences which use the possessive の.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nないでください\n～ないでください\nPlease don't (Polite request)\nN5 Grammar\nThe ないでください form is used to make polite negative requests, similar to the English 'Please don't do [this]'. In comparison, the dictionary/non-past form of a verb + な expresses an extremely blunt or brusque command not to do something. The two forms have similar meanings (telling someone not to do something) but vary greatly in politeness level.\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nてください\n～てください\nPlease do (Polite request)\nN5 Grammar\nThe てください form is used to make polite requests, similar to the English 'Please do [this]'. In contrast, the dictionary/non-past form of a verb + な expresses an extremely blunt or brusque command not to do something. The two forms have opposite meanings and are completely different in politeness levels (i.e. 'please do' vs. 'don't do!').\nたまえ\nたまえ\n(Please) do!, Ask to, Beg to, (Order somebody to do something)\nN2 Grammar\nThe dictionary/non-past form of a verb + な expresses a blunt command not to do something. In contrast, たまえ expresses a strong, authoritative command to do something. For example, 食べるな！ means 'Don't eat it!' while 食べたまえ means 'I command you: eat it!' な is more casual than the formal たまえ, but both are used toward someone of lower status whom you have power over.\nてごらん\nてごらん\n(Please) try to, (Please) look\nN3 Grammar\nThe dictionary/non-past form of a verb + な expresses a blunt command not to do something. In contrast, てごらん is a gentle encouragement to try doing something. For example, 食べるな！ means 'Don't eat it!' while 食べてごらん means 'Try eating it' Both are used toward someone of lower status, such as an adult speaking to a child.\nなさい\nCommand to do\nN4 Grammar\nThe dictionary/non-past form of a verb + な expresses a blunt command not to do something. In contrast, a verb stem + なさい expresses a strong but respectful command. For example, 食べるな means 'Don't eat it!' while 食べなさい means 'Eat it!' Despite being derived from an honorific verb, なさい is still a command and is used with lower-status people or children, not superiors.\nRelated\nExpand All\nないで\nWithout doing, Not ~ and ~\nN4 Grammar\nThe dictionary/non-past form of a verb + な expresses a blunt command not to do something. Similarly, ないで attached to the negative stem of a verb is a more casual and gentle request. For example, するな is a strong way of saying 'Don't do it!' while しないで is a softer, more casual way of saying '[Please] don't do it.'\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n行\n(\nい\n)\nくな\n。\nDon't go\n.\nここ\nで\nサッカー\nを\nするな\n。\nDon't play\nsoccer here.\n泣\n(\nな\n)\nくな\n。\nDon't cry\n.\n一人\n(\nひとり\n)\nで\nするな\n。\nDon't do\nit alone.\n「\nこれ\nは\n不味\n(\nまず\n)\nい\n！」\nって\n言\n(\nい\n)\nうな\n。\nDon't say\n, 'This tastes bad!'\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nCommands\nWasabi\nNegative Command\nTae Kim\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 46 & 266\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 183\nみんなの日本語 II\nPage 54 [CH 33]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nな – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (10 in total)\nznic1998\nHey! I’m back with more questions. Based on the example sentence it seems that this can only be used with verbs in their dictionary form. I am correct with making that assumption. I’m assuming there will grammar later to cover more imperatives but I just want to be sure.\nFredKore\nznic1998:\nthis can only be used with verbs in their dictionary form\nThat’s correct. The negative imperative is (plain verb)-な.\nThe positive imperative is either (う-verb, え-ending) or (る-verb stem + ろ).\nまつな！\nDon’t wait!\nまて！\nWait!\nたべるな！\nDon’t eat!\nたべろ！\nEat!\n(I can’t seem to find the Bunpro page for positive imperative though.)\nznic1998\nThanks, @FredKore I will ever use it since apparently it is quite rude, but good to know\nGot questions about な?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
