      "sourceText": "だろう (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 3: 6/14\nだろう\nRight?, Probably, Isn't it?\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nだろう\nRight?, Probably, Isn't it?\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb +\nだろう\nAdjective +\nだろう\nNoun +\nだろう\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nAuxiliary Verb\nWord Type\nDependent Word\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout だろう\nだろう\nis a conjugated form of\nだ\n, and can also be seen in the contracted form\nだろ\n(more casual).\nだろう\nexpresses that the speaker thinks that something is true (assertion/expectation), and also that the listener would probably agree with them. This comes across like 'right?', or 'probably' in English.\nだろう\ncarries exactly the same meaning as\nでしょう\n(the polite equivalent). However, due to だ being more casual than\nです\n,\nだろう\nis also considered to be more casual/masculine than\nでしょう\n.\nCaution\nWhile\nだろう\nis usually used when the speaker has some sort of information that would support their assertion, it can also be used simply when the speaker 'feels' that something is true.\nIn this kind of sentence that expresses a 'feeling' or 'hunch',\nだろう\ncomes across as quite low confidence. The speaker's tone of voice will often reveal how confident\nだろう\nis.\nTopics\nProbably That's Right, Right?\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn how to change the tone of our sentences with でしょう and だろう. Both can be used to mean something similar to 'probably' or 'right?', although with more varied nuances.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nでしょう\nRight?, Probably, Isn't it?\nN5 Grammar\nBoth でしょう and だろう express conjecture (e.g., 'It's probably…') or seek confirmation when used with rising intonation in a question (e.g., 面白いでしょう？ or 面白いだろう？ = 'It's interesting, right?'). でしょう is more polite and used with strangers or those of equal or higher rank, while だろう is casual and blunt, used with close friends, family, and in informal settings.\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nまい\nWon't, Intend not to, Probably not\nN2 Grammar\nまい and だろう are both used to express speculation, but they contrast in polarity. だろう indicates positive speculation or likelihood, as in 雨が降るだろう (It will probably rain), while まい expresses strong negative speculation or denial, as in 雨は降るまい (It probably won’t rain).\nRelated\nExpand All\nね\nIsn't it?, Right? (Sentence ending particle)\nN5 Grammar\nだろう is a form of だ used when the speaker feels uncertain. It's often translated as 'probably,' 'I guess,' or '...right?' when asking a question. In contrast, ね is used to seek confirmation or agreement, often translated as '...isn't it?' These two can combine to express both uncertainty and confirmation, as in 面白いだろうね ('It's probably interesting, isn't it?').\nのではないだろうか\nのではないでしょうか\nI think ... isn't it?, It might be that..., Don't you agree?\nN2 Grammar\nだろう is a form of だ used to express mild uncertainty (like 'It's probably…') or to seek confirmation from the listener ('It's this, right?'). のではないだろうか is similar but expresses much less certainty, with a nuance of wondering or seeking confirmation or approval ('I wonder if it's…?').\nかもしれない\nMight, Maybe, Probably\nN4 Grammar\nかもしれない (polite form かもしれません) and だろう (polite form でしょう) are both casual phrases used to express uncertainty or possibility, but with different degrees of certainty. かもしれない is less certain, similar to 'maybe' or 'might,' while だろう expresses a higher degree of certainty, like 'probably' or 'I guess.'\nじゃないか\nIsn't it?, Is it not?\nN4 Grammar\nだろう, in addition to expressing conjecture ('It's probably…'), can seek confirmation or agreement from the listener (e.g., 面白いだろう = 'It's interesting, right?'). じゃないか is more forceful and assertive, suggesting the speaker strongly believes something and expects agreement (e.g., 面白いじゃないか = 'It's interesting, isn't it!'). Both are casual forms and should be avoided in polite conversation.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nここ\nが\nいい\nだろう\n？\nThis place here is good (\nright?\n)\nあなた\nだろう\n。\nIt's you (\nright?\n)\nこれ\nは\nペン\nだろう\n。\nThis is a pen. (\nright?\n)\n今夜\n(\nこんや\n)\nは\n、\n忙\n(\nいそが\n)\nし\nい\nだろう\n。\nTonight is busy (\nright?\n)\nこれ\nは\n、\n水\n(\nみず\n)\nだろう\n。\nThis is water,\nright?\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nDifference between そうです、ようです、だろう and らしいです\nBriefJapanese\nUsing 「でしょう」 and 「だろう」 to express strong amount of certainty (casual)\nTae Kim\nでしょう / だろう in Japanese\nSelfTaughtJapanese\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 100\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 241\nTobira\nPage 45\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nだろう – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (9 in total)\nMZa\nSince だろう is just a form of だ as said in the new explanation, I guess there is no more reason to use a particle when using the former than when using the later.\nsteffuld\nHi! If だろう is a conjugated form of だ, and だ is not used with verbs, why can だろう be used with verbs? Is there a reasonable explanation?\nMaybe it was のだろう initially but then の was omitted, or maybe it goes back to である times or something like that?\nMastanese1\nWhy is “のだろう” (as opposed to  だろう\") considered wrong, but if adding the question marker, considered correct (i.e. ~のだろうか\")?\nGot questions about だろう?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
