      "sourceText": "って (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 6: 12/17\nって\nCasual quotation\nCan replace と\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nって\nCasual quotation\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nQuote +\nって\n+ Verb\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nCase Marking Particle\nRegister\nCasual\nAbout って\nって\nis a casual variation of the\nと\nparticle that is used for quoting something. This use is called\n引用\n(\nいんよう\n)\nin Japanese, which basically translates to 'reference', or 'citation'.\nって\nis almost exclusively used after direct quotations, and will be followed by a verb indicating what type of communication was used.\n言\n(\nい\n)\nった,\n思\n(\nおも\n)\nった,\n聞\n(\nき\n)\nいた, for example.\nJust like with\nと\n, the verb that comes after\nって\nis frequently omitted. This is even more true for\nって\n, as many things are omitted in casual language.\nCaution\nAlthough often heard, due to\nって\nbeing very casual, it can be considered unnatural to use in sentences that also use the\nです\nor ます polite structures. However, if the conversation is formal but friendly (like between a senpai and a kouhai), most people would consider it normal.\nTopics\nYou Can Quote Me on That\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn how to quote someone or something in Japanese. To do this, we can use the particle と or, for more casual speech, we can use って. This will allow us to start talking about what people have said or are thinking (and more!).\n2m\nRelated\nExpand All\nと\nQuotation\nN5 Grammar\nと and って are quoting particles marking direct or indirect speech, thoughts, or feelings. For example, 行くと言っていた and 行くって言っていた both mean '[They] were saying [they] will go.' The difference is that って is used mainly in casual conversation, while と is used in speech or writing. って can also casually introduce a topic, like 日本っていいよね ('Japan is great, isn't it?'), which と cannot.\nんだって\nI heard that, He or She said that\nN3 Grammar\nって (a contracted form of と) is a casual quoting particle used to mark direct or indirect speech, thoughts, feelings, or to introduce a topic. んだって, which combines って with the explanatory ん・の, is used to express 'I heard' or 'He/she/they said.' While って can follow any word, んだって requires な before な-Adjectives or nouns.\nといってもいい\nといってもいい\nYou could say, You might say\nN4 Grammar\nって (a contracted form of と) is a casual quoting particle used to mark direct or indirect speech, thoughts, feelings, or to introduce a topic casually. For example, 行くって言っていた means '[They] were saying [they] will go.' といってもいい combines と with 言う and いい, meaning 'You/one could say' (literally: 'It's fine even if one says...').\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n「\n乾杯\n(\nかんぱい\n)\n！」\nって\n言\n(\nい\n)\nい\nたい\n。\nI want to\nsay\n, 'Kanpai!'\n先生\n(\nせんせい\n)\nは\n「おはよう！」\nって\n言\n(\nい\n)\nった\n。\nThe teacher\nsaid\n, 'Good morning!'\n「\n明日\n(\nあした\n)\nは\n雨\n(\nあめ\n)\nが\n降\n(\nふ\n)\nります\nか\n」\nって\n聞\n(\nき\n)\nい\nた\n。\nI\nasked\n, 'Is it going to rain tomorrow?'\n彼\n(\nかれ\n)\nは\n「\n晩\n(\nばん\n)\nごはん\nを\n捨\n(\nす\n)\nて\nた\n」\nって\n言\n(\nい\n)\nった\n。\nHe\nsaid\n, 'I threw away my dinner'.\n「\n何時\n(\nなんじ\n)\nから\nです\nか\n」\nって\n聞\n(\nき\n)\nいた\n。\nI\nasked\n, 'What time is it from?'\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nDirect & Indirect speech ＆って\nMaggieSensei\nSaid in Japanese (と言った vs って言ってた)\nJapanese Ammo\nUsing 「って」 as a casual version of 「と」\nTae Kim\nOffline\n[AIAIJ] An Integrated Approach to Intermediate Japanese\nPage 113\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 510\nGenki II 1st Edition\nPage 97\nGenki II 2nd Edition\nPage 123\nGenki II 3rd Edition\nPage 119\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 156\nTobira\nPage 21\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nって – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (8 in total)\nmrnoone\nHey\nBasically, って can be considered to be the contraction of both, と particle by itself or whole と言う phrase (among others).\nSo it all depends on how much speaker wants to shorten the phrase, basically preference.\nYou can say お母かあさんは「危あぶない！」って, and it will be also OK.\nI hope it makes things a bit more clear,\nCheers!\njomteon\nAh, okay, that’s what I thought! Thanks for clearing it up\nPep95\nI’m currently reading a book in which the protagonist says, 『Stuff blah blah』て言う本. I interpret this as “A book called ‘Stuff blah blah’”, but am not sure what the grammar point would be, apart from a kind of colloquial version of this (って) colloquial version of 〜と.\nIt’s a children’s book though, so I’m kind of surprised there are even any colloquialisms in there.\nAm I correct in my assumption here?\nGot questions about って?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
