      "sourceText": "が・の (Subject) (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 6: 15/17\nが・の (Subject)\nParticle Swapping\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nが・の (Subject)\nParticle Swapping\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nNoun +\nの\n+ Verb\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nCase Marking Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout が・の (Subject)\nIn some cases, the particle の can be used in place of が to mark a subject. This happens in subordinate clauses that describe or modify a noun, where the subject marked by の is not the main subject of the sentence.\nThere are two main reasons why someone may choose to use の instead of が.\nFirst, if there is already another が, marking the main subject of the sentence, then it normally sounds more natural to not repeat が twice in the same sentence.\nSecond, が can put emphasis on the thing that it marks, delineating it specifically as new or noteworthy. Using の instead of が sounds less strong, so something else in the sentence can be emphasized.\nCaution\nが is not swapped for の if the clause contains an object marked by を. In such cases, の will be interpreted as the possessive particle, thus changing the meaning entirely.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nこれは\n僕\n(\nぼく\n)\nの\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべた\n苺\n(\nいちご\n)\nです。\nThis is the strawberry I ate. (\nRelative clause subject\n)\n妹\n(\nいもうと\n)\nの\n落\n(\nお\n)\nとしたコップは\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nのです。\nThe cup my younger sister dropped is mine. (\nRelative clause subject\n)\nあれは\n看護師\n(\nかんごし\n)\nの\n履\n(\nは\n)\nく\n靴\n(\nくつ\n)\nだ。\nThose are the shoes nurses wear. (\nRelative clause subject\n)\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nの\n飲\n(\nの\n)\nんだ\n水\n(\nみず\n)\nはこれじゃなかった。\nThe water I drank wasn't this one. (\nRelative clause subject\n)\nお\n母\n(\nかあ\n)\nさん\nの\n持\n(\nも\n)\nっている\n帽子\n(\nぼうし\n)\nは\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\nの？\nWhose hat is the one mom is holding? (\nRelative clause subject\n)\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nThere are no Online resources listed for 「が・の (Subject)」.\nYou can\nsubmit a resource for review\n. Resources are constantly updated, come back later to see new additions!\nOffline\nThere are no Offline resources listed for 「が・の (Subject)」.\nYou can\nsubmit a resource for review\n. Resources are constantly updated, come back later to see new additions!\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nが・の (Subject) – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (3 in total)\ndarzington\nIn this quiz sentence:\n友達：「その服良いね！」僕：「僕____着ている服はお兄ちゃんのなんだ。」\nCould someone please explain how this use of の is different from the possessive の?\nka5\nWith possessive の, the meaning of the resulting sentence would be different.\nの subject marker inside a clause marks the subject doing the action:\n僕\nの\n着ている服は…\nthe clothes that\nI am wearing\nare…\nの possessive would mark possession:\n○○が着ている僕\nの\n服は…\nmy clothes\nthat ○○ is wearing are…\ndarzington\nMakes sense, thanks!\nGot questions about が・の (Subject)?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
