      "sourceText": "が (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 5: 6/12\nが\nBut, However\nが can sometimes mean something closer to 'and', or a comma that connects clauses\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nが\nBut, However\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nVerb +\nが\n［い］Adjective\n+\nが\n［な］Adjective\n+\nだ\n+\nが\nNoun +\nだ\n+\nが\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nConjunctive Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout が\nAs a particle,\nが\nhas several different uses in Japanese. However, each of these different uses share a common theme. That theme expresses 'highlighting (A) as important, (B)'. In this use,\nが\ncomes across as 'but', or 'however' in English.\nThis nuance of\nが\nis used directly after any form of verb or\nい-Adjective\n, but requires one of the conjugations of\nだ\nor\nです\nbefore it when used with a noun or\nな-Adjective\n.\nFrom these examples, we can see that\nが\nis simply highlighting that 'while (A) is important, there is extra information'.\nが\nwith the meaning of 'but' is considered to be very formal, this is due to it sounding quite direct in asserting that there is extra information.\nCaution\nSometimes a statement will end with\nが\n. In these cases it just means that more information exists, but it is obvious, so it does not need to be said.\nTopics\nYeah, But...\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn two ways to say 'but' by using the conjunctive particles けど and が. With these words, we will be able to expand our range of expression and make more nuanced statements.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nものの\nAlthough, But, Even Though\nN2 Grammar\nものの expresses contrast, similar to 'although' in English, showing that while one thing is true, another contradicts it. が also expresses contrast, but its nuance is weaker, and in some cases, it merely introduces a statement without implying contrast. が is more general, while ものの is more specific. Both are formal and more common in writing than in casual speech.\nけれども\nBut, Although\nN5 Grammar\nBoth が and けれども express contrast, similar to 'but' in English, or sometimes simply indicate a transition to added information (like 'and'). Both are somewhat formal, used in writing or formal speech, compared to the casual けど. Note that while が and けれども follow immediately after verbs or い-Adjectives, だ or です is required after a な-Adjective or noun.\nけど・だけど\nけど\nBut, However\nN5 Grammar\nBoth が (or だが/ですが after a noun or な-adjective) and けど・だけど express contrast or transition, similar to 'but' in English (e.g., '見た目は美味しそうだけど、味がない' = 'It looks tasty, but has no flavor'). The difference is that が is more formal or written-style, while けど is more colloquial. Both can also be used at the end of a sentence to suggest an implied contrast, even if not explicitly stated.\nだが・ですが\nだが\nBut, Still, However, Nevertheless\nN4 Grammar\nThe particle が in both patterns expresses contrast, similar to 'but' in English, or sometimes indicates a transition to additional information (like 'and'). The difference is that が follows directly after an い-Adjective or verb, while it requires だ or です after a な-Adjective or noun. が is somewhat formal and often used in writing or formal speech.\nRelated\nExpand All\nんだけど・んですが\nんだけど\nBut..., And...\nN4 Grammar\nBoth express contrast, but が is more formal and used in writing, like 面白いが難しい ('It's interesting, but difficult'). When combined with ～んだ・～んです, as in 行きたいんですが, it softens the sentence and invites a response or action from the listener.\nくせに\nくせに\nEven though, And yet, Despite\nN3 Grammar\nくせに expresses disapproval, similar to 'in spite of' or 'although' in English. In contrast, が is a neutral connecting particle, often expressing contrast (like 'but') or adding information (like 'and'). Unlike くせに, which carries a negative nuance, が is neutral. After な-Adjectives and nouns, くせに requires な or の, while が requires だ or です.\nところが\nEven so, However, Even though, But\nN3 Grammar\nBoth ところが and が express contrast or contradiction. ところが is used at the beginning of a sentence to emphasize a contrast between expectation and reality. In contrast, が is used after a phrase or at the end of a sentence to express simple contrast, or sometimes to add information or context without implying surprise or expectation.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n早\n(\nはや\n)\nいです\nが\n、\n起\n(\nお\n)\nきます\n。\nIt is early,\nbut\nI will wake up.\n難\n(\nむずか\n)\nし\nい\nです\nが\n、\n頑張\n(\nがんば\n)\nります\n。\nIt is difficult,\nbut\nI will do my best.\n寒\n(\nさむ\n)\nい\nです\nが\n、\n外\n(\nそと\n)\nへ\n行\n(\nい\n)\nきます\n。\nIt's cold,\nbut\nI will go outside.\nロイさん\nの\n携帯\n(\nけいたい\n)\nは\n古\n(\nふる\n)\nい\nです\nが\n、\n速\n(\nはや\n)\nい\nです\n。\nRoy's cellphone is old,\nbut\nit is fast.\n綺麗\n(\nきれい\n)\nです\nが\n、\n危\n(\nあぶ\n)\nな\nい\nです\n。\nIt is beautiful\nbut\ndangerous.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nExpressing contradiction using 「が」 and 「けど」\nTae Kim\nThe Conjunctive Particles が & けれど\nImabi\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 120\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 110\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 56 [CH 8]\nみんなの日本語 II\nPage 126 [CH 45]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nが – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (10 in total)\ncjulian\nRegarding the following example sentence, for some reason, my brain wants to say 面白いですが instead of 面白いが.  Is this just a bad habit I’ve developed or would it be fine to do so here?\n日本語\nにほんご\nは\n面白\nおもしろ\nい\nが\n、\n難\nむずか\nしいです。\nnekoyama\nI think it’s even better that way. Politeness should be the same on both sides of が.\ncjulian\nThank you for that!  That makes plenty of sense to me.\nGot questions about が?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
