      "sourceText": "くらい ① (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 8: 4/13\nくらい ①\nAbout, Approximately\nくらい and ぐらい may be used 100% interchangeably, but ぐらい is far more common in speech\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nくらい ①\nAbout, Approximately\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nNumber/counter +\nくらい\nor\nぐらい\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nAdverbial Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout くらい ①\nくらい\nor\nぐらい\n(more common in spoken language) has several different uses in Japanese, but is often translated as 'about', or 'approximately'. It comes from the kanji\n位\n(\nくらい\n)\n, which literally means a 'rank', or 'grade' of something. This use of\nくらい\ncomes after numbers, or counter words.\nAs can be seen in the last example,\nくらい\nmay also be used after question words such as\nどの\n, when asking about the extent (grade) of something.\nDue to the original meaning of\nくらい\nbeing 'rank', or 'extent', it may not be used when referring to broad periods of time.\nころ\nwill be used in these situations. However,\nくらい\nand\nころ\nmay be used interchangeably when referring to a specific time (due to a specific time being able to be thought of as an extent).\nCaution\nPlease see the\nころ\ngrammar point if you would like to compare these two structures further.\nTopics\nRoughly Before That\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about the two words which allow us to talk about relative value or position. くらい can be used as an adverbial particle or noun to express something is roughly 'about' a certain value.\n前\n(\nまえ\n)\nis a noun which can be used adverbially to mean 'before'.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nほど\nほど\nThe more, About, To the extent that, So much… that\nN3 Grammar\nくらい (often ぐらい in speech) expresses an approximate degree or extent. It's often used with number + counter expressions (the くらい① form), as in 10分くらいかかった = 'It took about ten minutes.' ほど can be used similarly, but is more formal and carries the nuance of an upper limit—e.g., 10分ほど implies 'about, but no more than ten minutes.'\nごろ\nころ\nAround, About\nN4 Grammar\nくらい (often ぐらい in speech) expresses an approximate degree or extent. A common use is with number + counter expressions, as in 10分くらいかかった ('It took about 10 minutes'). Unlike くらい, which can be used with durations or points in time (e.g. 10分くらい, 10時くらい), ごろ can only be used with points in time (e.g. 10時ごろ = 'around 10 o'clock').\nRelated\nExpand All\nくらい ②\nTo the extent that, So…that, About the only thing that...\nN3 Grammar\nくらい (often ぐらい in speech) expresses an approximate degree or extent. It's commonly used with number + counter expressions (the くらい① form), as in 100円くらい = 'about 100 yen.' It can also be used like ほど to express extent or degree, as in 足が動けなくなるくらい走った = 'I ran so much my legs couldn't move anymore.'\nは～くらいです\nAbout the only, To the extent that\nN3 Grammar\nくらい (or ぐらい in speech) expresses an approximate degree or amount, like the English 'about.' It's commonly used with numbers and counters (the くらい① form), e.g. 100円くらい = 'about 100 yen.' A related pattern, は～くらいです, means 'just about the only X is Y.' For example, 食べ物で嫌いなのはピーマンくらいです = 'The only food I dislike is peppers.'\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n何時\n(\nなんじ\n)\nぐらい\nに\n来\n(\nき\n)\nます\nか\n。\nApproximately\nwhen will you come?\nあと\n３０\n(\nさんじゅっ\n)\n分\n(\nぷん\n)\nぐらい\nで\n行\n(\nい\n)\nきます\n。\nI will go/leave in\napproximately\n30 minutes.\n毎週\n(\nまいしゅう\n)\nコーヒー\nは\nどの\nくらい\n飲\n(\nの\n)\nむ\n？\nApproximately\nhow much coffee do you drink per week?\n彼\n(\nかれ\n)\nは\n１０\n(\nじゅっ\n)\n歳\n(\nさい\n)\nくらい\nだろう\n。\nHe's\nabout\n10 years old.\n一週間\n(\nいっしゅうかん\n)\nに、\n五\n(\nご\n)\n時間\n(\nじかん\n)\nぐらい\nスポーツ\nを\nする\n。\nI play sports for\nabout\nfive hours in one week.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nThe Particles ほど & くらい\nImabi\n位 = くらい・ぐらい\nMaggie Sensei\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 212\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 112\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 74 [CH 11]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nくらい ① – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (16 in total)\nDasutin\nTo follow up on the question asked by alexpwns, why is で used after くらい in these examples?\n後３分くらいで着く。\nどのくらいで着きますか?\nあと３０分ぐらいで行きます。\nAshitaka1\nI also would like to know.\nPablunpro\nHi!\nIf you look at the sentences, they all deal with the amount of time someone needs to perform an action, arriving or going in these cases. Now, if you go to this で grammar point, you can see that\n‘at its core, で is always used to highlight something that is ‘required’ to perform some sort of action’\n.\nIn these cases, that\nsomething\nis\ntime\n. Thus,\nで\nis used here to indicate that someone will need about a certain amount of time to either arrive or (before they) go.\nHTH!\nGot questions about くらい ①?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
