      "sourceText": "なくてはならない (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 9: 9/13\nなくてはならない\nMust do, Have to do\nAlso used to express duty or necessity.\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nなくてはならない\nMust do, Have to do\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nVerb［\nない\n］\n+\nなくて\nは + ならない\nVerb［\nない\n］\n+\nなくちゃ\n+ ならない\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nExpression\nWord Type\nAuxiliary Verb\nRegister\nFormal\nAbout なくてはならない\nMuch like\nてはいけない\n'must not do',\nなくてはならない\nexpresses an obligation or duty. However, this is where the similarities end.\nなくてはならない\nis an example of a double negative in Japanese, and actually has the meaning '(A) must be done', or 'must do (A)'.\nWhile this grammar point is usually translated as 'must do', the literal translation is 'must not, not do (A)'. This double 'not' is where many learners have difficulties.\nならない\nsimply means 'cannot become', and comes from the negative form of\nなる\n.\nTo use this structure, simply create the negative form of a verb, and then convert ない to なく (the conjunctive form of an\nい-Adjective\n), followed by\nて\n. After this,\nは\nis added, and then finally\nならない\n.\nならない\nis considered to be the most formal structure out of\nならない\n,\nいけない\n, and だめ. It is regularly used in formal writing, or semi-formal/formal speech.\nいけない\n(the most common variation) and だめ (the most casual variation) are also acceptable in many situations.\nCaution\nThe ては in なくては is often shortened to ちゃ, as this is easier to say.\nなくちゃ\nmay then be followed by\nならない\n. However, due to\nならない\nbeing quite formal,\nなくちゃ\n(a casual structure) will not be used with\nならない\non a regular basis. It is more common to see\nなくちゃ\npaired with\nいけない\n, or だめ.\nThese casual structures are very similar to words like 'gotta' in English.\nTopics\nNeeds Must\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about a variety of ways to say that someone must or must not do something. We'll learn how all these phrases come down to the same core idea, and how they vary based on formality.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nなくてはいけない\nMust do, Have to do\nN5 Grammar\nなくてはいけない and なくてはならない express obligation or necessity, meaning something 'must' or 'needs to' be done. They are equivalent in meaning, but ならない is slightly more formal and more common in written language, such as rules or regulations. In casual speech, both forms are often shortened to なくちゃ, with the いけない or ならない part left implied.\nねばならない\nねばなら + ぬ\nMust, Have to, Should\nN2 Grammar\nねばならない and なくてはならない both express obligation or necessity, meaning something 'must' or 'needs' to be done. They are equivalent in meaning, but ねばならない is a highly formal expression used primarily in the written language and in historical speech. Note that in colloquial everyday conversation, なくてはならない is often shortened to なくちゃ.\nなければならない\nMust do, Have to do\nN4 Grammar\nなくてはならない and なければならない both express obligation or necessity, meaning something 'must' or 'needs' to be done. They are equivalent in meaning, but なければならない is slightly more formal and common in written rules or official language. In casual speech, these forms are often shortened to なくちゃ and なきゃ, respectively.\nなければいけない\nMust do, Have to do\nN4 Grammar\nなくてはならない and なければいけない express obligation, meaning something 'must' or 'needs' to be done. The two forms are equivalent. The level of formality is similar, with なければ being slightly more formal than なくては and ならない being slightly more formal than いけない. The parts are interchangeable, and in casual speech, these forms are often shortened to なくちゃ and なきゃ, respectively.\nなくちゃ・なきゃ\nMust do, Have to do\nN5 Grammar\nなくちゃ, なきゃ, and なくてはならない express obligation, meaning something 'must' or 'needs' to be done. なくてはならない is more formal, while なくちゃ・なきゃ (shortened forms of なくては and the similar なければ) are casual alternatives. なくてはならない is often seen in writing or official statements, while なくちゃ・なきゃ would be used colloquially with friends and family.\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nてはいけない\n～てはいけない\nMust not, May not\nN5 Grammar\nてはいけない expresses prohibition, meaning something 'must not' or 'cannot' be done. In contrast, なくてはならない expresses obligation or something that 'must' be done. These forms can be tricky for learners, as the negative form なくてはならない (literally, 'it's not good if you don't') means 'must,' while the affirmative form てはいけない ('it's not good if you do') means 'must not.'\nRelated\nExpand All\nずにはおかない\nずにはおかない\nWill definitely, Will certainly, Bound to, Not fail to\nN1 Grammar\nBoth ずにはおかない and なくてはならない express inevitability, but they differ in source and nuance. なくてはならない states an objective or logical necessity such as rules, duties, as in 法律を守らなくてはならないパスポートがなくてはならない (‘One must obey the law.'). By contrast, ずにはおかない expresses an inevitable result caused by someone’s actions or by a situation’s inherent force, often implying emotional impact or consequence rather than obligation, as in 彼の言葉は人を感動させずにはおかない (‘His words are bound to move people.’).\nずにはすまない\nWon't get away without, Won't get off without, Have to, Have no choice but to, Can’t not\nN1 Grammar\nBoth express necessity, but the source of that necessity is different. ずにはすまない means ‘cannot avoid doing’ and implies moral pressure, social responsibility, or consequences that make inaction unacceptable, often based on feelings or circumstances rather than rules, as in 迷惑をかけた以上、謝らずにはすまない ('Having caused trouble, I can’t avoid apologizing.'). なくてはならない expresses objective or logical necessity such as rules, requirements, or unavoidable conditions, without emotional pressure, as in 10時までに行かなくてはならない ('I must go by 10 o'clock.').\nないと\nMust, Have to\nN4 Grammar\nBoth ないと and なくてはならない both express obligation or necessity, meaning something 'must' or 'needs' to be done.  なくてはならない is more formal, while ないと (negative form with a condition と) is a more casual alternative. While ないと can be used alone or followed by either いけない (more formal) or だめ (more casual), it's generally not used with the more formal ならない.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n毎日\n(\nまいにち\n)\n勉強\n(\nべんきょう\n)\nを\nしなくてはならない\n。\nYou\nmust do\nyour studies every day.\n３\n(\nさん\n)\n時\n(\nじ\n)\n半\n(\nはん\n)\nまでに\n、\n銀行\n(\nぎんこう\n)\nに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nかなくてはならない\n。\nBy 3:30,\nI have to go to\nthe bank.\nもう\n二時\n(\nにじ\n)\nです\nか\n！\nまだ\nお\n弁当\n(\nべんとう\n)\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべてない\nです\n。\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべなくてはならない\n。\nIt's already two?! I haven't eaten my bento yet. I\nhave to eat\n.\n京都\n(\nきょうと\n)\nで\n電車\n(\nでんしゃ\n)\nを\n降\n(\nお\n)\nりなくてはならない\n。\nI\nmust get off\nthe train in Kyoto.\n君\n(\nきみ\n)\nが\n決\n(\nき\n)\nめなくてはならない\nよ\n。\nYou\nhave to decide\n.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\n\"HAVE TO\", \"NEED TO\" \"SHOULD HAVE\", \"I WISH...HADN'T\" in Japanese\nJapanese Ammo\nExpressing things that must be done\nTae Kim\nHow to say “must do something”\nNihonOnTheGo\nOffline\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 142\nTobira\nPage 43\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nなくてはならない – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (38 in total)\nJamesBunpro\nWe organise grammar points based on JLPT level. The JLPT itself broadly follows frequency and importance in terms of ordering. It doesn’t necessarily follow how difficult something is to learn, with some difficult grammar being introduced early on due to being common and important.\nAt N2 there are a couple of other points which are close to てはならない in appearance, which can cause confusion. Namely, てならない (‘can’t help but…’) and (ねば)ならぬ (‘must do’).\nAt N2 you are more likely to be asked to judge what grammar is appropriate based on context, as context sensitivity becomes more important as you learn more grammar points which seem to mean the same thing. I can imagine てはならない being used in a multiple choice question in that specific way. There is not much imaginable benefit in trying to test someone at N5 on the contextual nuance of this point. It is also important to note that the JLPT does not restrict grammar points to certain levels, so you may see grammar points at diff...\ngpressutto5\nIn the first sentence, てはならない is linking to ～てはいけない.\nMuch like てはならない,\nI assume the link is correct, but the text is wrong? Otherwise it is also a bit weird to start the text with “Much like [this grammar you still don’t know and will learn much later on]”.\nRKstudios\nGot questions about なくてはならない?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
