      "sourceText": "なくてはいけない (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 9: 8/13\nなくてはいけない\nMust do, Have to do\nAlso used to express duty or necessity.\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nなくてはいけない\nMust do, Have to do\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nVerb［\nない\n］\n+\nなくて\nは + いけない\nVerb［\nない\n］\n+\nなくちゃ\n+ いけない\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nExpression\nWord Type\nAuxiliary Verb\nRegister\nFormal\nAbout なくてはいけない\nMuch like\nてはいけない\n,\nなくてはいけない\nstates that something 'must not' be done. However, this is where the similarities end.\nなくてはいけない\nis an example of a double negative in Japanese, and actually has the meaning '(A) must be done', or 'must do (A)'.\nWhile this grammar point is usually translated as 'must do', the literal translation is 'must not, not do (A)'. This double 'not' is where many learners have difficulties.\nいけない\nsimply means 'cannot go', and comes from the negative\npotential form\nof\n行\n(\nい\n)\nく.\nTo use this structure, simply create the negative form of a verb, and then convert ない to なく (the conjunctive form of an\nい-Adjective\n), followed by\nて\n. After this,\nは\nis added, and then finally\nいけない\n.\nいけない\nis considered to be the most common structure used with the 'must', and 'must not' grammar points, and is natural in both speech, and written Japanese. Alternatively,\nならない\nmay be used in formal speech, but is much better suited to writing. だめ is also acceptable, but much more casual than either\nいけない\nor\nならない\n.\nCaution\nThe ては in なくては is often converted to ちゃ, as this is easier to say.\nなくちゃ\nmay then be followed by\nいけない\n, or\nいけない\nmay be omitted, without changing the meaning.\nThese casual structures are very similar to words like 'gotta' in English.\nTopics\nNeeds Must\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about a variety of ways to say that someone must or must not do something. We'll learn how all these phrases come down to the same core idea, and how they vary based on formality.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nなくてはならない\nMust do, Have to do\nN5 Grammar\nなくてはいけない and なくてはならない express obligation or necessity, meaning something 'must' or 'needs to' be done. They are equivalent in meaning, but ならない is slightly more formal and more common in written language, such as rules or regulations. In casual speech, both forms are often shortened to なくちゃ, with the いけない or ならない part left implied.\nねばならない\nねばなら + ぬ\nMust, Have to, Should\nN2 Grammar\nねばならない and なくてはいけない express obligation or necessity, saying something 'must' or 'needs to' be done. They are equivalent in meaning, but  ねばならない is a highly formal expression used primarily in the written language and in historical speech. Note that in colloquial, everyday conversation, なくてはいけない is often shortened to なくちゃ.\nなければいけない\nMust do, Have to do\nN4 Grammar\nなくてはいけない and なければいけない express obligation or necessity, saying something 'must' or 'needs to' be done. They are equivalent in meaning, with the only difference being that the なければ version is slightly more formal, though the difference is not highly significant. Note that in casual speech, these forms are often shortened to なくちゃ and なきゃ, respectively.\nなくちゃ・なきゃ\nMust do, Have to do\nN5 Grammar\nなくちゃ・なきゃ and なくてはいけない express obligation or necessity, saying that something 'must' or 'needs to' be done. なくてはいけない is more formal and often appears in writing or official contexts. In contrast, なくちゃ・なきゃ are casual contractions (of なくては and なければ) used in everyday speech with friends and family.\nなければならない\nMust do, Have to do\nN4 Grammar\nなくてはいけない and なければいけない express obligation or necessity, saying something 'must' or 'needs to' be done. They are equivalent in meaning, but the なければならない version is slightly more formal, and thus more likely to be found in written rules, etc. Note that in casual speech, these forms are often shortened to なくちゃ and なきゃ, respectively.\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nてはいけない\n～てはいけない\nMust not, May not\nN5 Grammar\nなくてはいけない expresses necessity or obligation, something that 'must' be done. In contrast, てはいけない means something 'must not' or 'cannot' be done. These forms can be tricky for learners: なくてはいけない looks negative but means 'must' (literally, 'it's not good if you don't'), while てはいけない looks affirmative but means 'must not' (literally, 'it's not good if you do').\nなくてもいい\nDon't have to\nN4 Grammar\nなくてはいけない expresses obligation or necessity, saying something 'must' or 'needs to' be done. なくてもいい has the opposite meaning, saying that one doesn't have to do something (literally, 'it's okay not to do'). For example, 食べなくてはいけない means '(You) must eat it', while 食べなくてもいい means '(You) don't have to eat it.'\nRelated\nExpand All\nないと\nMust, Have to\nN4 Grammar\nないと, なくちゃ, and なくてはいけない express obligation, meaning something 'must' or 'needs' to be done. なくてはいけない is more formal, while ないと (the negative form plus conditional と) is a casual alternative. While いけない can be replaced with ならない, ないと is usually used on it's own or followed by いけない or だめ, the more casual option.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nテスト\nの\n前\n(\nまえ\n)\nに\n、よく\n寝\n(\nね\n)\nなくてはいけない\n。\nBefore a test, you\nmust sleep\nwell.\n１２\n(\nじゅうに\n)\n月\n(\nがつ\n)\nになっ\nてから\n、\n暖房\n(\nだんぼう\n)\nを\nつけなくてはいけない\n。\nOnce December comes,\nyou have to turn on\nthe heating.\n来週\n(\nらいしゅう\n)\nは\n試合\n(\nしあい\n)\nがある\nので\n、\nたくさん\n練習\n(\nれんしゅう\n)\nしなくてはいけない\n。\nBecause I have a game next week, I\nmust train\na lot.\n今日\n(\nきょう\n)\nは\nいい\n天気\n(\nてんき\n)\nだ\nから\n、\n外\n(\nそと\n)\nで\n遊\n(\nあそ\n)\nばなくてはいけない\n。\nBecause the weather is nice today,\nI have to\nplay outside.\nまだ\nやり\nたい\nことがある\nけど\n、\nもう\n寝\n(\nね\n)\nなくてはいけない\n。\nThere are still things that I want to do, but I\nhave to sleep\nalready.\nGet more example sentences!\n