      "sourceText": "Adjective + て + B (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 10: 4/12\n～て (Qualities and States)\nAnd..., Both and (Conjunctive)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nAdjective + て + B\nAnd..., Both and (Conjunctive)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\n［い］Adjective［\nい\n］\n+\nく\n+\nて\n［な］Adjective\n+\nで\n+ Phrase\nNoun +\nで\n+ Phrase\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nConjunctive Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout Adjective + て + B\nThe\nて\nform of an\nい-Adjective\n, or\nで\nform of a noun (or\nな-Adjective\n) is exactly the same as the\nて\nform of a verb, in that it carries the meaning of 'and', and is used for linking.\nBuilding on from the\nadjective +て, noun + で\ngrammar point, instead of linking adjectives,\nadjective + て + (B)\nis used for linking an adjective to an entire phrase. With this construction, the phrase is simply added after\nて\n(with\nい-Adjectives\n), or\nで\n(with nouns and\nな-Adjectives\n), without any extra consideration for the phrase following it (this means that the (B) phrase will behave as if it was its own sentence).\nAs with\nadjective +て, noun + で\n, it should be noted that\nて\nand\nで\nare different structures here.\nて\nis the same conjunction particle that is used with verbs (meaning that\nい-Adjectives\nare similar to verbs), while\nで\nis actually a form of\nだ\nthat is used for conjugation. It is the same\nで\nas the one that is used in the formal version of\nだ\n,\nである\n. Which we will learn later.\nCaution\nLike other\nて\nforms (\nている 1\n,\nている 2\n, and\nている 3\n), the\nて\n(or\nで\n) form of other words can have several possible meanings. These meanings are as follows.\nLinking - Like 'and' in English.\nReasons - Like 'with (A)', or 'because of (A)' in English.\nManner - Like 'to do (B) in an (A) way' in English.\nCaution\nAs with all other conjugations of\nいい\n, remember that it will be よくて, and not いくて.\nTopics\nTe-rrific て-Form\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about how the て-form is used with adjectives and nouns. It is much the same as with verbs, being used to connect two things together. In the details section, we will also recap the logic and use of the て-form in general.\n2m\nRelated\nExpand All\nAdjective + て・Noun + で\n～て (Adjectives and Nouns)\nAnd... (Conjunctive)\nN5 Grammar\nBoth of these patterns involve connecting an adjective or noun phrase to the rest of the sentence using the て form. The meaning of the connection depends on context. Sometimes it simply links words, as in 大きくてきれい ('big and pretty'), while in other cases, the first part may provide a reason for the second, as in 甘くておいしい ('sweet and tasty').\nし～し\nし (Reasons)\nAnd, Giving reasons\nN4 Grammar\nし～し lists multiple reasons, similar to 'because of A, B, and so on' in English. In contrast, Adjective + て + B just connects adjectives. While it can imply the first quality supports the second (e.g. 甘くておいしい = 'sweet and delicious'), it doesn't suggest a broader conclusion. し often implies one, as in 甘いし、おいしいし, meaning 'It's sweet and delicious… (so I like it).'\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nりさ\nは\n背\n(\nせ\n)\nが\n高\n(\nたか\n)\nくて\nかわい\nい\nです\n。\nRisa is\ntall and\ncute.\n静\n(\nしず\n)\nかで\n平和\n(\nへいわ\n)\nな\nところ\nに\n住\n(\nす\n)\nみ\nたい\n。\nI want to live in a\nquiet and\npeaceful place.\nほろさん\nは\n親切\n(\nしんせつ\n)\nで\n優\n(\nやさ\n)\nし\nい\n。\nHolo is\nkind and\ngentle.\n「ルフィさん\nは\n明\n(\nあか\n)\nるくて\nいい\n人\n(\nひと\n)\nです\nよ\nね\n。」\n「そう\nです\nよ\n。」\n'Luffy is a\nbright and\ngood-natured person, isn't he?'\n'That's right.'\n新田\n(\nにった\n)\nさん\nは\n悲\n(\nかな\n)\nしくて\n寂\n(\nさび\n)\nし\nかった\nんです\n。\nNitta was (used to be)\nsad and\nlonely.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nExpressing a sequence of states\nTae Kim\nOffline\nGenki 1 3rd Edition\nPage 173\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 103\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nAdjective + て + B – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (11 in total)\nDaru\nTerilian:\nIn my understanding the first sentence could also be seen as “This phone is fast because its new” (eg. if the context was that someone is surprised your phone is so much faster than his/hers) instead of “fast\nand\nnew”\nYou answered your own question: it depends in the context!\nRemember that while it is 3 separate uses, the basic meaning stays the same. So really, its as if the 3 are happening at the same time, yet context define which of the three is happening. So even the same exact sentence can come off differently depending on the context.\ndollicia\nHi everyone\nThe111\nI realize that at some level, many usages of て that Bunpro has marked as “separate lessons” actually all condense to nearly the same semantics. In spite of that, this lesson claims to teach one specific thing (linking qualities of a single subject). Many of the examples fit this pattern perfectly, e.g.\nGot questions about Adjective + て + B?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
