      "sourceText": "くれる (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 10: 9/12\nくれる\nTo give (me), To be given\nFor both くれる and あげる, に marks the recipient\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nくれる\nTo give (me), To be given\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nGiver\n(*)\nは\n(1)\n+ Recipient\n(*)\nに\n+ Object\n(*)\nを\nくれる\nRecipient\n(*)\nに\n+ Giver\n(*)\nは\n(1)\n+ Object\n(*)\nを\nくれる\nGiver\n(*)\nは\n(1)\n+ Object\n(*)\nを\n+ Recipient\n(*)\nに\nくれる\n(*)\nGiver, Recipient and Object are Nouns\n(1)\nが\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nVerb\nWord Type\nIndependent Word\nRegister\nStandard\nRare Kanji\n呉れる\nAbout くれる\nくれる\nis generally taught as part of a set of 3 verbs that are regularly used for giving and receiving in Japanese. These verbs are\nくれる\n,\nあげる\n, and\nもらう\n.\nくれる\nmeans 'to give' (to you, or someone that is part of your 'in' group).\nWith\nくれる\nand\nあげる\n, they both function in the regular way you would expect (regarding particles). This means that\nが\nwill always mark the giver (the person performing the action), and\nに\nwill mark the recipient (the person that is the 'destination' of the thing that is given). This means that\nを\nwill be used to mark the 'object' that is given, as is usually the case.\nThe giver/recipient are often omitted from sentences with\nくれる\n, as one/both are usually obvious from context, or the sentence may be focusing on the object.\nAs can be seen here, the same nuance is kept, even when things are omitted.\nFun Fact\nWhat someone considers their 'in' group will be different for each person, but usually consists of members of your family, clubs that you are in, or something similar that has a 'group' atmosphere (that you are a part of).\nThe giver will always be someone that is more distant than the people that are in the 'group'.\nCaution\nWhen we want to say that we (or someone else) gave something to someone that is not in our 'in' group, we will need to use\nあげる\ninstead.\nTopics\nGive and Take\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about three very important verbs which are all to do with giving and receiving. With these words, the direction and relationship is the important thing. These concepts are the backbone of polite speech and are essential for understanding more advanced Japanese.\n4m\nRelated\nExpand All\nもらう\nもらう\nTo receive, To get, To obtain\nN5 Grammar\nくれる means 'to give' and is used when someone from the speaker's out-group gives something to the speaker or their in-group. The subject, marked with が, is the giver. In contrast, もらう means 'to receive' and describes the action from the receiver's perspective. With もらう, the subject marked with が is the one receiving.\nてくれる\n～てくれる\nTo do something for someone (usually you)\nN4 Grammar\nくれる means 'to give' and is used when someone outside the speaker's group gives something to the speaker or their in-group. For example, プレゼントをくれた means '(someone) gave me a present.' As てくれる, it shows someone did something for the speaker's benefit, as in 日本語を教えてくれた ('(someone) taught me Japanese').\nあげる\nTo give (away), To present, To provide\nN5 Grammar\nあげる and くれる are basic verbs for giving. あげる is used when the speaker or their in-group gives something to someone outside the group, or when one out-group member gives to another. くれる is used when someone outside the speaker's group gives something to the speaker or their in-group. In both cases, the subject marked by が is the giver.\nてくれてありがとう\n～てくれてありがとう\nThank you for ~ ing\nN4 Grammar\nくれる is a basic verb meaning 'to give,' usually used when someone from outside the speaker's group gives something to the speaker or their in-group. For example, プレゼントをくれた means '(someone) gave me a present.' It also appears in the pattern てくれてありがとう to thank someone for a favor. For example, 日本語を教えてくれてありがとう means 'Thank you for teaching me Japanese.'\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n母\n(\nはは\n)\nは\n僕\n(\nぼく\n)\nに\nかっこいいペン\nを\nくれた\n。\nMy mom\ngave me\na cool pen. (\nI was given\n)\n兄\n(\nあに\n)\nは\n(\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nに\n)\n便利\n(\nべんり\n)\nな\n箱\n(\nはこ\n)\nを\nくれた\n。\nMy brother\ngave me\na convenient box. (\nI was given\n)\nおじいちゃん\nは\n(\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nに\n)\n古\n(\nふる\n)\nい\n自転車\n(\nじてんしゃ\n)\nを\nくれた\n。\nMy grandpa\ngave me\nan old bicycle. (\nI was given\n)\n彼\n(\nかれ\n)\nが\n(\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nに\n)かわい\nい\n靴\n(\nくつ\n)\nを\nくれて\n、\n嬉\n(\nうれ\n)\nし\nい\nです\n。\nHe\ngave me\nsome cute shoes, so I am happy. (\nI was given\n)\n友達\n(\nともだち\n)\nは\n毎週\n(\nまいしゅう\n)\n土曜日\n(\nどようび\n)\nに\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nの\n母\n(\nはは\n)\nに\nクッキー\nを\nくれる\n。\nMy friend\ngives my mom\ncookies every Saturday. (\nMy mom is given\n)\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nInner/outer circles and which verb to use\nKanji Link [video]\nWhen to use くれる\nTae Kim\nあげる, くれる, and もらう as Verbs\nWasabi\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 213\nGenki II 2nd Edition\nPage 56\nGenki II 3rd Edition\nPage 52\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 171\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 152 [CH 24]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nくれる – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (16 in total)\nBazza\n近所\nきんじょ\nの\n人\nひと\nが\n旅行\nりょこう\nから\n帰\nかえ\nって\n来\nき\nて、\n父\nちち\nにお\n土産\nみやげ\nをくれた。\nWhen the neighbors return home from traveling, they\ngive\nmy father souvenirs.\nThat is one of the example sentences and its translation. I thought it was describing a one time event, because of the final くれた. The translation however says ‘give’ rather than  ‘gave’, suggesting it is a habit.\nCould you confirm please?\nThanks in advance!\nFuga\nHey @Bazza !\nThis seemed like an input error on our end so we have just fixed it!\nBazza\nBazza:\ne\nmy father souvenirs.\nThat is one of the example sentences and its translation. I thought it was describing a one time event, because of the final くれた. The translation however says ‘give’ rather than ‘gave’, suggesting it is a habit.\nAwesome - thanks for the quick work on that!\nGot questions about くれる?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
