      "sourceText": "もらう (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 10: 10/12\n貰\n(\nもら\n)\nう\nTo receive, To get, To obtain\nUnlike あげる and くれる, に (or から) marks the giver, not the recipient\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nもらう\nTo receive, To get, To obtain\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nGiver\n(*)\nに\n+ Recipient\n(*)\nは\n(1)\n+ Object\n(*)\nを\nもらう\nRecipient\n(*)\nは\n(1)\n+ Giver\n(*)\nに\n+ Object\n(*)\nを\nもらう\nGiver\n(*)\nに\n+ Object\n(*)\nを\n+ Recipient\n(*)\nは\n(1)\nもらう\n(*)\nGiver, Recipient and Object are Nouns\n(1)\nが\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nVerb\nWord Type\nIndependent Word\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout もらう\nもらう\nis the 3rd verb that is regularly taught together with\nくれる\n, and\nあげる\n. It is used to highlight that (A) is 'received' by someone. However, unlike\nくれる\nand\nあげる\n(which are used to highlight that something is 'given'),\nもらう\nrequires the\nに\nparticle (or\nから\n) to mark the giver, not the recipient.\nが\nor\nは\nmay be used to mark the recipient, as they are the one performing the action of receiving.\nを\nwill mark the object that is being given.\nIn the second sentence we can see that the recipient has been omitted, while in the third example, the giver was omitted. This is a regular occurrence when using\nもらう\n, as the receiver is usually obvious, or the 'giver' may not be important to the overall meaning of the sentence.\nIn this example, the thing that was given is the focus, while the speaker does not consider the 'giver' something that needs to be mentioned. Sometimes, for very simple sentences with clear context, neither the 'giver', nor the 'receiver' needs to be mentioned.\nTopics\nGive and Take\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about three very important verbs which are all to do with giving and receiving. With these words, the direction and relationship is the important thing. These concepts are the backbone of polite speech and are essential for understanding more advanced Japanese.\n4m\nRelated\nExpand All\nくれる\nTo give (me), To be given\nN5 Grammar\nくれる means 'to give' and is used when someone from the speaker's out-group gives something to the speaker or their in-group. The subject, marked with が, is the giver. In contrast, もらう means 'to receive' and describes the action from the receiver's perspective. With もらう, the subject marked with が is the one receiving.\nあげる\nTo give (away), To present, To provide\nN5 Grammar\nあげる means 'to give' and is used when the speaker or someone in their in-group gives something to someone outside the group. The subject, marked by が, is the giver. In contrast, もらう means 'to receive' and describes the act from the receiver's perspective. With もらう, the subject marked by が is the one receiving.\nてもらう\n～てもらう\nTo get someone to do, To have someone do\nN4 Grammar\nもらう is a basic verb meaning 'to receive,' often used when the speaker or someone in their in-group receives something from someone outside the group. For example, プレゼントをもらった means 'I received a present.' When used as てもらう after a verb in the て form, it shows that the speaker received the benefit of an action, as in 日本語を教えてもらった ('I had someone teach me Japanese').\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\nお\n祖母\n(\nばあ\n)\nさん\nから\nおはし\nと\nコップ\nを\nもらった\n。\nI\nreceived\nchopsticks and a glass from my grandmother.\n彼女\n(\nかのじょ\n)\nに\n(\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\n)\n青\n(\nあお\n)\nい\n花\n(\nはな\n)\nを\nもらいます\n。\n(I)\nreceive\nblue flowers from her (my girlfriend).\n(\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\n)\n友達\n(\nともだち\n)\nに\n暖\n(\nあたた\n)\nか\nい\nセーター\nを\nもらった\n。\n(I)\nreceived\na warm sweater from (my) friend.\n父\n(\nちち\n)\nから\n(\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\n)お\n金\n(\nかね\n)\nを\nもらった\n。\n(I)\nreceived\nmoney from my father.\n(\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\n)\n明後日\n(\nあさって\n)\n給料\n(\nきゅうりょう\n)\nを\nもらいます\n。\n(I)\nreceive\n(my) salary the day after next.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nInner/outer circles and which verb to use\nKanji Link [video]\nWhen to use もらう\nTae Kim\nあげる, くれる, and もらう as Verbs\nWasabi\nOffline\nGenki II 2nd Edition\nPage 57\nGenki II 3rd Edition\nPage 52\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 174\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 50 [CH 7]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nもらう – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (9 in total)\nIcyIceBear\nReceived. Something to be said while showing someone an item received. もらう is used when something is received as the grammar point says\nWelcome to the community\nka5\nWell, we don’t have context for this sentence, but もらった！ can also mean “I believe I will succeed in doing this”. This is because of もらう additional definition of “to have in one’s pocket (a fight, match)”. You can hear anime characters use it from time to time.\nfor example\nk-n\nthank you both\nGot questions about もらう?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
