      "sourceText": "から (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 5: 3/12\nから\nBecause, Since\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nから\nBecause, Since\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb +\nから\n［い］Adjective\n+\nから\n［な］Adjective\n+\nだ\nから\nNoun +\nだ\n+\nから\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nCase Marking Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout から\nから\ncan have several different meanings in Japanese, depending on which part of the sentence it is in, and what comes before/after it. It is often translated as 'because', or 'since'. In these cases, it just means 'Because of (A), (B)'.\nから\nmay be used with almost any type of word, but when the nuance is 'because', it will require\nだ\nbefore it when following a noun or\nな-Adjective\n.\nDespite having several different nuances, it may be easier to think of\nから\nas meaning 'from' in all situations, as that is the base nuance.\nFrom (A as a reason), (B) = Because/Since.\nFrom (A as a location), (B) = From.\nFun Fact\nから\nis a special type of particle in Japanese called a\ncase marking particle\n. These are particles that describe the relationship that (A) and (B) exist in. We will learn more about these soon!\nTopics\nJust Because\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn how to use the conjunctive particles ので and から to mean 'because'. We'll also learn how から can be used as a case-marking particle to show origin, meaning something like 'from'. With these, we will be able to start to string together our sentences in far more natural ways by giving reasons and justifications.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nものだから\nBecause, The reason is\nN2 Grammar\nから expresses a reason or cause, similar to 'because' or 'so' in English, with the structure (A) から (B). In contrast, ものだから is more emphatic or emotional, often used to explain or justify actions. Both can be used in subjective statements, where the speaker presents their perspective or judgment.\nものですから・もので\nものですから\nBecause, My excuse is, My explanation is\nN2 Grammar\nBoth から and ものですから・もので express reasons or explanations. から is more straightforward and commonly used, similar to '(A), so (B)' in English. ものですから・もので places more emphasis on the reason, often used to explain one's own actions. Note that ものですから is more polite, while から is used in more casual speech.\nので\nBecause, So, Since, The reason being\nN5 Grammar\nから and ので both express a cause or reason, similar to the English 'A, so B.' The reason comes first, followed by から or ので, and then the result. から is more colloquial, used in everyday speech, and often for personal or emotional statements. ので is slightly more formal and tends to be used for more objective statements.\nもの・もん\nBecause, Cause\nN3 Grammar\nから expresses a cause or reason, similar to 'A, so B' in English. もの or もん (with もん being more casual) can also express an emphatic or emotional reason, similar to 'because' in English. The main difference is that から can be used mid-sentence, while もの or もん typically appears at the end of a sentence and conveys a more personal or emotional tone.\nRelated\nExpand All\nしたがって\nしたがって\nTherefore, Thus, As a result\nN2 Grammar\nBoth したがって and から express a cause or reason. The difference is that から is common in casual, everyday speech, while したがって is more formal, used in writing or structured speech. Additionally, から appears in the middle of a sentence, while したがって comes at the start. To use から at the start, you would use ですから or だから.\nし～し\nし (Reasons)\nAnd, Giving reasons\nN4 Grammar\nBoth し～し and から express reasons, similar to 'so' or 'because' in English. The reason (A) for outcome (B) is always followed by し or から, e.g. (A) し/から (B). The key difference is that から focuses on a single reason, while し～し lists multiple reasons. Even with one reason, し implies other unstated or implied reasons.\nなぜなら〜から\nなぜなら + から\nBecause, The reason is, That is because\nN3 Grammar\nBoth から and なぜなら〜から express reasons or causes. から is straightforward and casual, used in both casual conversations and formal speech, similar to 'A, so B.' In contrast, なぜなら〜から emphasizes the reason more strongly, often used in writing or formal speech with the nuance of 'As for why, it's because...' and typically when explaining a point in detail.\nからこそ\nEmphasized because, Precisely because\nN3 Grammar\nから expresses a reason or cause, similar to 'because of A, B' or 'A, so B' in English. The reason comes before から, with the result after. からこそ is the same から followed by the emphatic こそ, highlighting that the given reason is precisely or solely responsible for what follows. It's used for strong arguments or emphatic points.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\n犬\n(\nいぬ\n)\nが\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\nですから\n。\nBecause\nI like dogs.\nお\n菓子\n(\nかし\n)\nだから\n、\n甘\n(\nあま\n)\nい\n。\nBecause\nit is candy, it is sweet.\n辛\n(\nから\n)\nい\nから\n、カレー\nが\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\n。\nBecause\nit's spicy, I like curry.\n楽\n(\nたの\n)\nし\nい\nから\n、スポーツ\nを\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nる\n。\nBecause\nit is fun, I watch sports.\n学生\n(\nがくせい\n)\nがいる\nから\n、\n学校\n(\nがっこう\n)\nがある\nだろう\n。\nBecause\nthere are students, there is a school, right?\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nBECAUSE (から vs ので, からです vs ですから)\nJapanese Ammo\nExpressing reason or causation using 「から」 and 「ので」\nTae Kim\nUsing から (because) with nouns, adjectives and verbs\nStackExchange\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 179\nGenki I\nPage 121\nGenki I\nPage 177\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 153\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 215\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 217\nMarugoto Elementary 1 (A2) Rikai\nPage 43\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 106\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 62 [CH 9]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nから – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (18 in total)\nFuga\nHey @MikkaT !\nThe  だ used here and in けど is this. The reason that だ needs to be added after nouns and adjectives are the same reason you need to add ‘is’ in English.\nFor example, 大きい会社だから、人がたくさんいる (natural) VS 大きい会社から人がたくさんいる (unnatural) is the same as comparing ‘Because it is a big company, there are a lot of workers’  VS ‘Because big company, there are a lot of workers’.\nAlthough they may look the same, だから, in the vocabulary section is a conjunction (接続詞), and だ+から is a case marking particle (格助詞). This means that だから is used to connect the first sentence and the second sentence like in the image you have attached. This is kind of similar to how grammar points like しかし is used!\nI hope that answers your question!\nMikkaT\nHi, yes that makes it a bit clearer. Thank you.\nzekken\nWhat is the difference between the following quiz answers?\n私は犬が好き\nですから\n私は犬が好き\nだからです\n私は犬が好き\nだから\nI assume だから is less polite than the others, but then what’s the difference between ですから and だからです? Are they basically equivalent? Are there situations where I should prefer one over the other?\nGot questions about から?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
