      "sourceText": "けど・だけど (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 5: 5/12\nけど\nBut, However\nMore casual than が\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nけど・だけど\nBut, However\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb +\nけど\n［い］Adjective\n+\nけど\n［な］Adjective\n+\nだ\n+\nけど\nNoun +\nだ\n+\nけど\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nConjunctive Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout けど・だけど\nけど\nis one of the common conjunction particles in Japanese. These are particles that function by connecting the (A) and (B) parts of a sentence.\nけど\nworks very similarly to\nが\n, in that it highlights 'while thinking about (A), (B)'. This means that\nけど\nwill often come across as 'but' in English.\nAs can be seen from these examples, just like many other grammatical constructions,\nだ\nwill be required when using\nけど\nafter a\nな-Adjective\n, or a noun.\nけど\nis just a modern (casual) version of\nけれども\n. Please see below for a list of the most casual, to the most formal variations.\nCasual -\nけど\n-\nけども\n-\nけれど\n-\nけれども\n-\nが\n- Formal.\nCaution\nThe phrase after\nけど\nwill often be omitted if it is obvious, or the speaker does not want to say it.\nTopics\nYeah, But...\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn two ways to say 'but' by using the conjunctive particles けど and が. With these words, we will be able to expand our range of expression and make more nuanced statements.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nが\nBut, However\nN5 Grammar\nBoth が (or だが/ですが after a noun or な-adjective) and けど・だけど express contrast or transition, similar to 'but' in English (e.g., '見た目は美味しそうだけど、味がない' = 'It looks tasty, but has no flavor'). The difference is that が is more formal or written-style, while けど is more colloquial. Both can also be used at the end of a sentence to suggest an implied contrast, even if not explicitly stated.\nRelated\nExpand All\nけれども\nBut, Although\nN5 Grammar\nけど and けれども are essentially the same expression, with けど being a shortened form of けれども. Both express contrast, like 'but' in English, or soften a statement. The difference is in their formality: けど is casual and colloquial, while けれども is more formal and is most often used in writing or formal speech.\nだが・ですが\nだが\nBut, Still, However, Nevertheless\nN4 Grammar\nBoth けど・だけど (and the polite ですけど), as well as だが・ですが, express contrast, similar to 'but,' or sometimes just indicate a transition (like 'and'). The key difference is that だが is more formal and used in written contexts, while けど is more casual. Both can be used at the end of a sentence to imply something is left unsaid.\nところが\nEven so, However, Even though, But\nN3 Grammar\nBoth ところが and けど・だけど express contrast or contradiction. The key difference is that ところが is used at the beginning of a sentence to emphasize a contrast between the expected result and reality, and is more formal. In contrast, けど・だけど are used after a phrase or at the end of a sentence to express simple contrast or contradiction.\nんだけど・んですが\nんだけど\nBut..., And...\nN4 Grammar\nBoth introduce contrast, like the English 'but'. けど・だけど are common in speech, as in 難しいけど面白い ('It's difficult, but interesting.'). Adding ～ん to this pattern adds an explanatory nuance, often used to imply a request or hesitation, e.g. 難しいんですけど 'It's difficult, but...(do you want me to do it anyway?, etc.)'.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n便利\n(\nべんり\n)\nだけど\n、\n高\n(\nたか\n)\nい\n。\nIt is convenient,\nbut\nexpensive.\nその\n赤\n(\nあか\n)\nい\nＴ\n(\nティー\n)\nシャツ\nが\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\nだけど\n、\n青\n(\nあお\n)\nい\nＴ\n(\nティー\n)\nシャツ\nを\n買\n(\nか\n)\nう\n。\nI like that red T-shirt,\nbut\nI will buy the blue one.\nお\n金\n(\nかね\n)\nが\nある\nけど\n、\n買\n(\nか\n)\nい\nません\n。\nI have money,\nbut\nI won't buy (it).\n豚肉\n(\nぶたにく\n)\nは\n嫌\n(\nきら\n)\nい\nだけど\n、\n鶏肉\n(\nとりにく\n)\nは\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nます\n。\nI don't like pork,\nbut\nI eat chicken.\nその\n店\n(\nみせ\n)\nは\n高\n(\nたか\n)\nい\nけど\n、\n美味\n(\nおい\n)\nし\nい\nでしょう\n。\nThat restaurant is expensive,\nbut\nit is delicious, isn't it.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nExpressing contradiction using 「が」 and 「けど」\nTae Kim\nHow to use けど and だけど\nMaggie Sensei\nけど vs. けれども\nJapanese Stack Exchange\nだけど\nJapanese For Beginners\nOffline\nGenki II 3rd Edition\nPage 78\nMarugoto Elementary 1 (A2) Rikai\nPage 58\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 111\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 128 [CH 20]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nけど・だけど – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (29 in total)\nFabi02\nSo the dialogue in question comes from the beginning of the Manga “聲の形”, where 2 people (a boy and a girl) meet each other. When the girl recognizes the boy, she runs away, the boy follows her and screams “小6ン時の石田\nだけど\n”.\nSpoiler\nIt is later revealed that the boy used to bully the girl heavily 6 years prior\nMarcus\nI’ve just bumped into this very same problem!\nDo you need to add a だ before けど after all て-form verbs, or just 初めて?\nOr is only 初めて possible here?\nAre all て-form verbs considered to be nouns?\nIs this worth mentioning in the grammar explanation?\nSorry, so many questions! Hope someone can help! Thank you!\nCowsAreBeautiful\nI also encountered some instances in which けど with the meaning of “but” doesn’t quite suit.\nIn this video the other uses of けど made much more sense. けど Is Not Always But\nQuick Summary:\nけど as a particle marks the situation (A) and transferring to the main point of the sentence (B).\nExample:\n皆さん、明日田中さんの誕生日会があるんです(A)\nけど\n、行きますか(B)？\n→ The situation is that tomorrow is Tanakas birthday (Situation)[けど],  do you want to go there (Main Point).\nEffect: Before make a decision, here is the situation we are talking about.\nAnother Example:\nさっき田中さんにメロンもらったんだけど、食べる？\n→ Tanaka gave me a melon just now (A → Situation)[けど], do you want to eat it? (B → Main Point)\nI think that also this concept applies pretty well to the translation of “but” and helped me a lot understanding these occurrences in the examples above or if someone picks up the pho...\nGot questions about けど・だけど?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
