      "sourceText": "は (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 1: 3/12\nは\nAs for... (Highlights sentence topic)\nは can be also used to contrast several things\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nは\nAs for... (Highlights sentence topic)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nSentence topic +\nは\n, Pronounced \"わ\"\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nLinking Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout は\nは\n(pronounced わ) is often considered to be one of the most difficult particles to master in Japanese, alongside\nが\n.\nは\nmarks the topic of the sentence, and describes the whole sentence broadly. However,\nが\nprimarily marks single actions or statements. We will explore this distinction in more detail when covering\nが\n.\nIn many sentences (especially short ones), either\nは\nor\nが\nwould be considered perfectly natural. This depends on whether someone is simply stating something (\nは\n), or highlighting it especially (\nが\n).\nIn the first sentence,\nは\nbroadly describes what the sentence is about (curry). In the second sentence,\nは\nagain describes the whole sentence being about 'me', but\nが\nhighlights one specific piece of information (wine being the thing that is hated).\nCaution\nは\ncannot be used to describe things that you are seeing or experiencing 'in the moment' (right now). This is due to 'in the moment' events being considered part of a larger experience (your whole day for example).\nCaution\nは\nhas 2 main functions. The first marks the topic of the sentence, the second is used for contrast or making comparisons. Although there are no 'strict' rules for this, when\nは\nis used toward (or after) the middle of a sentence, it will usually have the nuance of contrast.\nIn the second sentence,\nは\nwill give the listener the impression that there is a comparison being made.\nTopics\nWhat Are You Talking About?\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about two 'particles'. は (pronounced わ ’wa’) and も.\n2m\nRelated\nExpand All\nの\nのは・のが\nVerb nominalizer, The one who..., That which...\nN5 Grammar\nThe particle の often shows possession, like the English ''s' (e.g., ケンジの本 means 'Kenji's book'). It can also connect two nouns, as in 日本人の学生 ('a student who is Japanese'). In contrast, は marks the topic of the sentence. Unlike の, it doesn't show possession — it introduces what the rest of the sentence will describe or explain.\nといえば\nSpeaking of, If it were the case that\nN3 Grammar\nBoth といえば and は can introduce the topic of a sentence. といえば is used when referencing something previously mentioned or known, often implying that more information will follow ('speaking of…'). は is broader and can also suggest contrast, as in 'バナナは食べたけど…' ('I ate the banana, but [not the apple]'). といえば doesn't create contrast in this way.\nが\nSubject marking particle, Identification particle\nN5 Grammar\nThe particle は marks the topic of a sentence, or what the sentence is about. が usually marks the grammatical subject, especially when introducing new information or expressing desire, ability, or necessity. は can also show contrast, while が emphasizes what it marks.\nって\nAs for, Speaking of, Casual は (Sentence topic marker)\nN3 Grammar\nって is used in casual conversation to introduce a topic informally. It comes from the quotation particle と and can carry the nuance of 'speaking of…'. は is a more standard and flexible topic marker, used in both casual and formal situations, including writing. Unlike って, は can also carry a contrastive nuance.\nNumber/Amount + は\n(Amount) + は\nAt least, Sort of, Or so\nN4 Grammar\nWhen は follows a number or amount, it often adds a contrastive nuance like 'at least' or 'no more than.' For example, １回は行ったけど means 'I went once, but no more than that.' This is similar to contrastive uses of は, like in 田中さんは行ったけど ('Tanaka went, but no one else did'). If there's no contrast, the number is usually used without a particle, as in 三つ買った ('I bought three').\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\nトム\nです\n。\nI (\nas the topic of this sentence\n) am Tom.\nあなた\nは\nジム\nです\n。\nYou (\nas the topic of this sentence\n) are Jim.\nトム\nは\n先生\n(\nせんせい\n)\nです\n。\nTom\n[as the topic of this sentence]\nis a teacher.\nカバン\nは\n汚\n(\nきたな\n)\nい\n。\nThe bag (\nas the topic of this sentence\n) is dirty.\nカレンダー\nは\n必要\n(\nひつよう\n)\nです\n。\nA calendar (\nas the topic of this sentence\n) is necessary.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nHow to use は, は vs が\nBunpro\nThe 「は」 topic particle\nTae Kim\nThe Particle Wa は I: The Topic/Contrast Marker\nImabi\nThe Ultimate Guide To: は vs が (The ONLY lesson you need!)\nJapanese Ammo [video]\nVery thorough breakdown of は and が - Page 299\n80/20 Japanese\nWhy は is pronounced as わ\nBriefJapanese\nは and です\nJapanese Ammo\nは as a \"non-logical\" particle\nCure Dolly [video]\nは vs. が quick breakdown and comparison\nJapanese StackExchange\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 21, 516 & 521\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 42 & 93\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 91\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 33\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 14 [CH 1]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nは – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (18 in total)\nLi_Shang_Yin\n(This thread is from a long time ago, so I apologize!) Can I mentally translate は as “as for” all the time?\nFredKore\nI think you can translate it as “as for” all the time, but 1) sometimes that translation sounds unnatural in English because “as for” can have a more formal tone, and 2) in higher JLPT levels, you’ll find other grammar points that can also be translated as “as for” but carry different nuances.\nSo, it’s probably okay to do that now, but just be aware that it may not always work in the future.\nAlso don’t worry about reviving threads! These are wiki pages of gathered knowledge!\naimlessmee\nIn the first example it says ha is used when there is no comparison and ga is used when comparing to other things. Under the caution about the two main functions of ha it says that ha is used to compare two things and ga is used to focus on the topic. I’m confused if ha or ga shows contrast/comparison.\nGot questions about は?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
