      "sourceText": "それ (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 1: 6/12\nそれ\nThat\nAlthough uncommon, its kanji can be seen as 其れ\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nそれ\nThat\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nDemonstrative noun\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nNoun\nWord Type\nPronoun\nRegister\nStandard\nRare Kanji\n其れ\nAbout それ\nJust like\nこれ\n,\nそれ\nis a\n'pronoun-like'\nword in Japanese (\nsubstitute noun\n).\nそれ\ntranslates roughly to 'that' and highlights something that is closer to the listener (physically or emotionally) than the speaker.\nIn these sentences, we can see\nそれ\nhighlighting things that the speaker is identifying as being closer to the listener, or about the listener.\nそれ\nis also used to point back to things that have been mentioned previously in the conversation. This can be something that was said earlier within the same sentence, or something that someone else said.\nTopics\nThis, That, and the Other\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn three essential words for pointing things out. これ, それ, and あれ. They are similar to 'this' and ‘that' in English, but Japanese has a useful distinction that English doesn't. It has two different words for 'that'!\n2m\nRelated\nExpand All\nあの\nThat (over there)\nN5 Grammar\nそれ refers to something closer to the listener than to the speaker, or something just mentioned, similar to 'that' in English. あの points to something far from both the speaker and listener, or something both people are aware of. あの must be used with a noun, like あの本 ('that book'), while それ can stand alone.\nその\nThat\nN5 Grammar\nその and それ both refer to something closer to the listener than the speaker, like 'that' in English. それ is used by itself to mean 'that' or 'that thing.' その must be followed by a noun, as in その本 ('that book'). In other words, それ stands alone, while その comes before a noun.\nどれ\nWhich (of three or more)\nN5 Grammar\nどれ is a question word used to ask 'which' or 'which one' when choosing from three or more options. それ, on the other hand, refers to something near the listener or something already mentioned, similar to 'that' in English. Unlike どれ, それ is not a question word.\nあれ\nThat (over there)\nN5 Grammar\nそれ and あれ are both similar to the English word 'that' and are used to refer to objects or ideas away from the speaker. それ refers to something near the listener or just mentioned in conversation. あれ points to something far from both the speaker and listener, or something both people are aware of. For physical objects, think of それ as 'that near you' and あれ as 'that over there.'\nそこ\nThere, That place\nN5 Grammar\nそこ and それ both refer to something previously mentioned or something closer to the listener than the speaker. そこ refers to a place and is similar to 'there' in English. それ refers to a thing or idea, like 'that.' Both are used when the speaker and listener share an understanding of what is being referred to, so the name doesn't need to be repeated.\nこれ\nThis\nN5 Grammar\nこれ and それ are both used to refer to things, but the difference lies in distance. これ means 'this' and refers to something near the speaker. それ means 'that' and refers to something closer to the listener than the speaker. Both can refer to physical objects or more abstract things.\nここ\nHere, This place\nN5 Grammar\nここ refers to a location where the speaker is, or is pointing to (like on a map), and is similar to 'here' in English. それ refers to something near the listener or something just mentioned, like 'that.' The key difference is that ここ refers to a place, while それ refers to a thing.\nこの\nThis\nN5 Grammar\nこの points to something near the speaker, like 'this' in English, and must be followed by a noun (e.g., この本 = 'this book'). それ, on the other hand, refers to something near the listener or recently mentioned, like 'that.' Unlike この, それ can be used on its own to mean 'that' or 'that thing.'\nあそこ\nOver there\nN5 Grammar\nあそこ refers to a place far from both the speaker and listener, or a known location not close to either. It's similar to 'over there' or 'that place' in English. それ, on the other hand, refers to an object near the listener or something just mentioned, like 'that.' The difference is whether you're referring to a place or a thing, and from whose perspective.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nそれ\nです\n。\nIt's\nthat\n.\nそれ\nも\nエアコン\nです\n。\nThat\nis also an air conditioner.\nそれ\nは\n本\n(\nほん\n)\nです\n。\nThat\nis a book.\nそれ\nも\n本\n(\nほん\n)\nです\n。\nThat\nis also a book.\nそれ\nは\n、\n水\n(\nみず\n)\nです\n。\nThat\nis water.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nExplanations and example sentences\nいいね Nihongo\nThis / That + adjectives これ / それ / あれ\nJapanese Ammo\nこれ、それ、あれ、and どれ\nWasabi\nOffline\nGenki I\nPage 34\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 62\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 60\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 20 [CH 2]\nみんなの日本語 II\nPage 30 [CH 29]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nそれ – Grammar Discussion\nThere's currently no discussion for それ\nAsk questions and learn together with other Bunpro users!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.