      "sourceText": "そこ (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 2: 12/13\nそこ\nThere, That place\nAlthough not common, 其処 kanji use can be seen\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nそこ\nThere, That place\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nDemonstrative\nPlace\nnear the listener, Previously mentioned\nplace\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nNoun\nWord Type\nPronoun\nRegister\nStandard\nRare Kanji\n其処\nAbout そこ\nそこ\nis one of the forms of the\nそれ\nfamily of words used to identify things close to the listener. In this case it demonstrates a place. Just like\nそれ\nrepresents 'that',\nそこ\nrepresents 'that place', or simply 'there'.\nIn the same way that\nそれ\ncan be used to point back to something that was mentioned previously,\nそこ\ncan be used to point back to a place that was mentioned previously.\nCaution\nそこ\nshould not be confused with そっち, a similar word which is also sometimes translated as 'there'. そっち focuses more on the direction that one is/is going, while\nそこ\nfocuses purely on 'that place'.\nTopics\nHere, There, and Everywhere\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about another set of こそあど words.\n3m\nRelated\nExpand All\nあそこ\nOver there\nN5 Grammar\nあそこ and そこ both indicate locations, differing only by perspective. あそこ refers to a place far from both the speaker and listener, similar to 'over there' (away from both of us). In contrast, そこ refers to a location closer to the listener than to the speaker, similar to 'there' (near you).\nそれ\nThat\nN5 Grammar\nそこ and それ both refer to something previously mentioned or something closer to the listener than the speaker. そこ refers to a place and is similar to 'there' in English. それ refers to a thing or idea, like 'that.' Both are used when the speaker and listener share an understanding of what is being referred to, so the name doesn't need to be repeated.\nあの\nThat (over there)\nN5 Grammar\nそこ refers to a place near the listener, similar to 'there' in English. あの, however, points to something distant from both speaker and listener or something familiar to both, like 'that.' Unlike そこ, あの always refers to objects or concepts and must directly precede a noun (e.g., あの本 = 'that book').\nこの\nThis\nN5 Grammar\nこの refers to something physically or figuratively close to the speaker, similar to 'this' in English, and must directly precede a noun (e.g., この本 = 'this book'). In contrast, そこ refers specifically to a location closer to the listener than to the speaker, similar to 'there' in English. Unlike この, そこ can stand alone without a noun.\nその\nThat\nN5 Grammar\nその must always precede a noun and refers to something physically or figuratively closer to the listener (e.g., その本 = 'that book'). In contrast, そこ stands alone and refers specifically to a place near the listener, similar to 'there' or 'that place' in English. Thus, both その and そこ point to things near the listener but differ in usage (noun vs. place).\nここ\nHere, This place\nN5 Grammar\nそこ and ここ are both used to indicate locations, with the difference being in the speaker's perspective. そこ refers to a location closer to the listener than to the speaker, similar to 'there' or 'that place' in English. On the other hand, ここ refers to a location closer to the speaker, similar to 'here' or 'this place.'\nどこ\nWhere\nN5 Grammar\nどこ is a question word used to ask about locations, similar to 'where' in English. In contrast, そこ refers specifically to a place closer to the listener, physically or figuratively, equivalent to 'there.' For instance, トイレはどこですか？ means 'Where is the bathroom?' and could be answered with そこです, meaning 'It's there (near you, or a place I'm pointing to).'\nあれ\nThat (over there)\nN5 Grammar\nあれ refers to something far from both the speaker and listener, or something both people know but isn't nearby, similar to 'that (over there)' in English. そこ refers to a place near the listener, like 'there.' Both relate to distance, but あれ is used for objects or ideas, while そこ is used for locations.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nそこ\nが\nいい\n。\nThat place\nis good.\nそこ\nの\nパソコン。\nThat PC\nthere\n.\nたぶん、\nそこ\n。\nMaybe,\nthere\n.\nそこ\nの\nレストラン\nは\n美味\n(\nおい\n)\nし\nい\nです\n。\nThe restaurant\nthere\nis delicious. (Near the listener)\nここ\nと\nそこ\nも\n明\n(\nあか\n)\nる\nい\n。\nIt is bright here and\nthere\ntoo.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nBonus examples\nDeviantart\nExamples\nJapanese with Anime\nKosoado II: Here and There\nImabi\nReview of ここ、そこ、どこ ＆ あそこ\nいいね Nihongo\nOffline\nGenki I\nPage 36\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 64\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 62\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 26 [CH 3]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nそこ – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (2 in total)\ndeltacat3\nVery pedantic question, but is there any change in nuance when using そこ instead of その in this example sentence?\n～\nその\nそこの\nレストランは美味しいです。\nThat restaurant is delicious.\nPushindawood\n@deltacat3 そこ + の emphasizes the “place.” Think “\nthat’s the place\nthat is good.” rather than just “that restaurant is good.” That being said, even though Bunpro calls for the そこの answer, “そのレストラン” is more commonly heard in daily conversations. Cheers!\nGot questions about そこ?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\n