      "sourceText": "あそこ (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 2: 13/13\nあそこ\nOver there\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nあそこ\nOver there\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nあそこ\nPlace\nseparated from\nboth\nthe speaker and the listener\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nNoun\nWord Type\nPronoun\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout あそこ\nLike\nここ\nand\nそこ\n,\nあそこ\nis a word that is used to identify a place in Japanese. The place that\nあそこ\nwill be used to identify is somewhere that is separated from both the speaker and the listener. This translates roughly to 'that place over there' in English.\nあそこ\ncan be used to identify both physical locations, and emotional ones.\nIn the same way that\nあれ\ncan be used to point back to something that was mentioned previously,\nあそこ\ncan be used to point back to a place that was mentioned previously. However, unlike\nそれ\nor\nそこ\n,\nあれ\nand\nあそこ\nwill only be used this way when both the speaker and listener have intimate knowledge of the topic.\nCaution\nあそこ\nshould not be confused with あっち, a similar word which is also sometimes translated as 'over there'. あっち focuses more on the direction that one is/is going, while\nあそこ\nfocuses purely on 'that place'.\nTopics\nHere, There, and Everywhere\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about another set of こそあど words.\n3m\nRelated\nExpand All\nそこ\nThere, That place\nN5 Grammar\nあそこ and そこ both indicate locations, differing only by perspective. あそこ refers to a place far from both the speaker and listener, similar to 'over there' (away from both of us). In contrast, そこ refers to a location closer to the listener than to the speaker, similar to 'there' (near you).\nその\nThat\nN5 Grammar\nその refers to something physically or figuratively close to the listener, similar to 'that' in English, and must directly precede a noun (e.g., その本 = 'that book'). In contrast, あそこ refers specifically to a location distant from both the speaker and listener, equivalent to 'over there' in English. Unlike その, あそこ can stand alone without needing a noun.\nこの\nThis\nN5 Grammar\nこの refers to something close to the speaker, either physically or figuratively, similar to 'this' in English, and must be followed by a noun (e.g., この本 = 'this book'). In contrast, あそこ refers to a location far from both the speaker and listener, similar to 'over there' in English. Unlike この, あそこ can stand alone without a noun.\nあれ\nThat (over there)\nN5 Grammar\nあそこ is used to refer to a location that is far from both the speaker and listener. あれ refers to objects similarly far away, or to ideas known to both people, similar to the English 'that' or 'that thing (over there).' In short, あそこ refers to places, while あれ refers to objects, situations, or ideas.\nそれ\nThat\nN5 Grammar\nあそこ refers to a place far from both the speaker and listener, or a known location not close to either. It's similar to 'over there' or 'that place' in English. それ, on the other hand, refers to an object near the listener or something just mentioned, like 'that.' The difference is whether you're referring to a place or a thing, and from whose perspective.\nここ\nHere, This place\nN5 Grammar\nあそこ and ここ both indicate locations but differ in perspective. あそこ refers to a place far from both the speaker and the listener, similar to 'over there' in English. On the other hand, ここ refers to a place near the speaker, similar to 'here' or 'this place.'\nあの\nThat (over there)\nN5 Grammar\nあそこ refers directly to a place far from both the speaker and listener, physically or figuratively, similar to the English 'over there.' あの, however, must be followed by a noun and indicates something distant from both parties (e.g., あの本 = 'that book [over there]'). In other words, both あそこ and あの point to things far away, but differ structurally in their usage.\nどこ\nWhere\nN5 Grammar\nどこ is a question word used to inquire about a place, similar to 'where' in English. あそこ, in contrast, refers specifically to a place far from both the speaker and listener, physically or figuratively, similar to 'over there.' For example, トイレはどこですか？ ('Where's the bathroom?') could be answered with あそこです ('It's over there,' indicating a place distant from both speaker and listener).\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nあそこ\nが\nいい\n。\nThat place over there\nis good.\nあそこ\nも\n楽\n(\nたの\n)\nし\nい\n。\nThat place over there\nis also fun.\nそこ\nと\nあそこ\n。\nThat place and\nthat place over there\n.\nあそこ\nが\n広\n(\nひろ\n)\nい\n。\nThat place over there\nis spacious.\nあそこ\nの\n店\n(\nみせ\n)\n。\nThe store\nover there\n.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nBonus examples\nDeviantart\nExamples\nJapanese with Anime\nKosoado II: Here and There\nImabi\nReview of ここ、そこ、どこ ＆ あそこ\nいいね Nihongo\nOffline\nGenki I\nPage 36\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 64\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 62\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 26 [CH 3]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nあそこ – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (4 in total)\ns1212z\nWe were having a similar discussion the other day on the この if you want to read:\nこの - Grammar Discussion\nGrammar Points\nI think it’s the English translation phrasing feels a bit awkward and I would use the orange box as a rephrasing guideline. Part of it is Japanese giving redunda...\nsharkey\ncuriosity, if you were referring to a map and calling something out about a place that you were indicating, would you use あそこ because the place is away from both of you, or would you use この since it is directly what you are touching/pointing at on the map?\nFuga\nWhen pointing at a location on a map, people will use この・ここ!\nGot questions about あそこ?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
