      "sourceText": "よ (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 2: 2/13\nよ\nEmphasis (Sentence ending particle)\nUsed when conveying new information to the listener.\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nよ\nEmphasis (Sentence ending particle)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nSentence +\nよ\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nSentence Ending Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout よ\nよ\nis classified as a\nsentence ending particle\nin Japanese. In most cases, it is used to convey new information to the listener, or just to add a bit of emphasis. Like most sentence ending particles, it is simply added to the end of almost any sentence.\nDespite the standard use of\nよ\nbeing to convey new information, it is also very regularly paired with\nね\n, to convey the meaning of 'right?!'. This more literally means something closer to 'I think (A), and I bet you think (A) too!'.\nCaution\nDid you notice that there is a\nだ\nafter the noun, but not the\nい-Adjective\n? This is not a unique rule for\nよ\n, but just one of the regular conjugation rules introduced in the lessons for\nい-Adjectives\nand\nな-Adjectives\n. It is also acceptable to use\nです\nよ\nafter either type of adjective (or noun).\nTopics\nPut Some Emotion Into It\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about three 'sentence-ending particles'. よ, ね and な. We'll also see how よ and ね can work together as よね.\n3m\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nか\nQuestion marking particle\nN5 Grammar\nよ is a common sentence-ending particle used for emphasis, often indicating that the speaker is providing new information (e.g., 面白いですよ = 'It's interesting, I'm telling you!'). In contrast, か is the standard question particle, which has the opposite function, asking for information (e.g., 面白いですか？ = 'Is it interesting?').\nRelated\nExpand All\nぜ\nStrong emphasis (friendly or familiar), !\nNon-JLPT Grammar\nよ is a sentence-ending particle used to add emphasis and indicate the speaker is sharing new information (e.g., 面白いですよ = 'It's interesting, I'm telling you!'). ぜ is a casual, masculine particle that also adds strong emphasis. Unlike ぞ, it can sound a bit more friendly, but it's still informal and should be avoided in polite situations or with people you don't know well.\nだ\nTo be, Is\nN5 Grammar\nよ is a common sentence-ending particle used to add emphasis, often indicating the speaker is sharing new information (e.g., 面白いよ = 'It's interesting, I'm telling you!'). だ is a grammatical form expressing assertion, similar to 'is' or 'am.' These two are often combined, especially in casual or masculine speech (元気だよ = 'I'm feeling good!'). In softer or more feminine speech, the だ is frequently omitted (e.g., 元気よ).\nぞ\nStrong emphasis (toward anyone), !\nNon-JLPT Grammar\nよ is a sentence-ending particle used to add emphasis and indicate the speaker is sharing something new (e.g., 面白いですよ = 'It's interesting, I'm telling you!'). ぞ is a rougher, more masculine sentence-ending particle that adds strong emphasis. It has a blunt tone and should be avoided in polite conversation or with strangers, as it lacks the softness or friendliness of よ.\nね\nIsn't it?, Right? (Sentence ending particle)\nN5 Grammar\nよ and ね are common sentence-ending particles that add feeling or nuance. よ adds emphasis and is used when giving new information or asserting something (e.g., 面白いですよ = 'It's interesting, I'm telling you!'). ね is used to seek agreement or confirmation (e.g., 面白いですね = 'It's interesting, isn't it?'). When combined as よね, they express both emphasis and a request for agreement.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nトム\nは\n足\n(\nあし\n)\nが\n速\n(\nはや\n)\nい\nよ\n。\nTom has fast feet. (Tom is a fast runner)\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\n背\n(\nせ\n)\nが\n高\n(\nたか\n)\nい\nよ\n。\nI am tall.\n野球\n(\nやきゅう\n)\nは\n楽\n(\nたの\n)\nし\nい\nよ\n。\nBaseball is fun.\nこれ\nは\nケーキ\nだ\nよ\n。\nThis is a cake.\nそれ\nは\nいい\nよ\n。\nThat is good.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\n「よ」 sentence ending\nTae Kim\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 46 & 543\nGenki I\nPage 38\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 66\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 64\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 80\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 38 [CH 5]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nよ – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (6 in total)\nxuzab\nIn response to someone who asked, 今日、何曜日ですか, wouldn’t it be kind of rude to say, 今日は水曜日ですよ？  I know that it’s “new information,” but for some reason have the impression that in this case, it’d be sort of presumptuous to present it as such.  More generally, when should one refrain from using よ？\nnekoyama\nI think this situation is one of the least rude ways to use よ, assuming the question is sincere and the person asking really doesn’t know the answer. よ expresses that the speaker has more knowledge or authority. It’s easy to interpret that as presumptuous, but another way to look at it is that in this sentence the よ emphasises that the speaker actually knows what they’re talking about. When answering a request for information, that’s a good thing.\nI find that when よ is rude, it’s often not directly because of the “new information” nuance. Of course that’s possible too, but but it’s much easier to be rude with よ in other ways. For example, let’s say we get used to agreeing to propositions from friends by saying something like いいよ. Then, one day, our boss asks us to do something and we reply いいですよ - acting as if we actually had a say in which requests from our boss we accept. (The correct answer would be something like わかりました. We do not answer requests from the boss from a poin...\nhoma\nThe least used particle in my conversations I think.\n相手をマウントするからあんまり使わないよね\nOkey, I just found it by writing the message above, we can just add ね to make it softer like “you know btw”. People use this all the time instead of just よ\nGot questions about よ?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
