      "sourceText": "か (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 3: 3/14\nか\nOr\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nか\nOr\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb (A) +\nか\n+ Verb (B) +\nか\n［い］Adjective\n(A) +\nか\n+\n［い］Adjective\n(B) +\nか\n［な］Adjective\n(A) +\nか\n+\n［な］Adjective\n(B) +\nか\nNoun (A) +\nか\n+ Noun (B) +\nか\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nAdverbial Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout か\nOne of the common uses of the particle\nか\nis often translated to 'or' in English. While this translation is accurate, it is not actually any different from\nか\n's regular usage as a question marker.\nFrom these examples, we can see that, in Japanese,\nか\nis performing the same role that a question mark would, or that a change in tone of voice would in spoken language.\n'Would you like tea? coffee?' (said with a rising tone).\nThis nuance of\nか\ncan be used after the dictionary form of almost any word in Japanese. However, using\nです\nor ます will change the nuance from 'presenting possibilities', to 'asking a formal question', so should be avoided.\nTopics\nThis and/or That\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn a couple of ways to list and group things in Japanese. Also, we’ll deepen our understanding of particles.\n2m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nそれとも\nOr, Or else, Rather\nN2 Grammar\nBoth か and それとも present alternatives. か directly follows nouns to mean 'or' (e.g., 今日か明日休みます = 'I'll take off today or tomorrow'). In contrast, それとも means 'or' as well, but is used at the start of sentences or questions, not directly after nouns (e.g., 今日休む？それとも明日にする？ = 'Will you take off today? Or [will you do it] tomorrow?').\nあるいは\nあるいは\nOr, Possibly, Maybe, Perhaps, Or possibly\nN3 Grammar\nBoth か and あるいは present alternatives. か is casual and follows nouns to mean 'or' (e.g., 今日か明日休みます = 'I'll take off today or tomorrow'). あるいは is more formal, mainly used in writing or formal speech (e.g., 新品で買うか、あるいは中古で買うか、まだ決めていません = 'I haven't decided yet whether I'll buy it new or perhaps second-hand').\nRelated\nExpand All\n～なり～なり\nOr, Either or\nN1 Grammar\n～なり～なり is a somewhat formal or literary expression used to provide examples, typically implying additional options or suggesting that the speaker doesn't mind which option is chosen. In contrast, the question particle か is used after nouns to present alternatives or offer a choice between two or more items (e.g., 今日か明日休みます = 'I'll take off today or tomorrow').\nでも\nOr something,  Any... (with question words), No matter (who)\nN4 Grammar\nThe particle か can follow nouns to mean 'or,' clearly specifying alternatives (e.g., 今日か明日休みます = 'I'll take off today or tomorrow'). On the other hand, でも, which also follows nouns, means 'or something,' suggesting an open-ended alternative (e.g., お茶でも飲みましょうか = 'Shall we drink tea or something?').\nQuestion-phrase + か\n(Question-Phrase) + か\nIncluding questions within sentences\nN4 Grammar\nBoth uses of か are extensions of its basic function as a question particle. When following a Wh-question word (like who, what, where, why, how), it creates a phrase meaning 'some-(word)' (e.g., 誰か = 'someone,' どこか = 'somewhere,' いつか = 'sometime'). When following regular nouns, it means 'or' (e.g., 今日か明日休みます = 'I'll take off today or tomorrow').\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nここ\nか\nそこ\n。\nHere\nor\nthere?\n赤\n(\nあか\n)\nか\n青\n(\nあお\n)\n。\nRed\nor\nblue.\n寝\n(\nね\n)\nる\nか\n遊\n(\nあそ\n)\nぶ\n。\nTo sleep\nor\nto play.\n今日\n(\nきょう\n)\nか\n明日\n(\nあした\n)\n。\nToday\nor\ntomorrow.\nこれ\nは\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nの\nペン\nか\nあなた\nの\nペン\nです\nか\n。\nIs this my pen\nor\nyour pen?\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nAll about 「か」: See #5\nJapaneseAmmo\nParallel Markers\nWasabi\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 164\nTobira\nPage 45\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 20 [CH 2]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nか – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (11 in total)\nKirumi\nThe\nか\nreview question\n寝る …か… 遊ぶ。\nasks for “Non-exhaustive, Particle”, while a\nと\nreview question asked for an “Exhaustive” particle. What exactly does exhaustive and non-exhaustive mean?\nPablunpro\nHi!\n‘Exhaustive’\nmeans that you are stating all of the items whilst\n‘Non-exhaustive’\nmeans that you are just stating some of the items while implying that there are more left unsaid.\nHTH!\nrichardf89\nThis grammar point seems particularly poorly worded. The structure suggests usage should be: verb/adjective/noun + か + verb/adjective/noun + か. But pretty much every example sentence omits the second か.\nDoes this mean the second か is optional? Where is the nuance here? Does this come up again in the future but in a bit more detail?\nGot questions about か?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
