      "sourceText": "と (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 3: 4/14\nと\nWith\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nと\nWith\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nNoun (A) + は + Noun (B) +\nと\n+ Verb\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nCase Marking Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout と\nIn our second lesson about\nと\n, we will look at the common translation of 'with' that is associated with this particle.\nと\nshows that there is a relationship between (A) and (B), regardless of if that relationship is translated as '\nand\n', or '\nwith\n'.\nIn either case, the nouns that are marked with\nと\nare usually all performing some mutual action (that mutual action may simply be existing).\nWhen\nと\nis used as 'with', it means that both/all people or objects are equally involved in the action. However,\nに\nhighlights that one person is the target (destination) of an action, so will result in something sounding a lot more one sided.\nHere we can see that both people are talking when\nと\nis used. It is a mutual conversation. When\nに\nis used, however, the person marked by\nに\nis being talked 'to', and is usually not actively participating in the conversation.\nTopics\nLocation, Location, Location\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we'll learn about how the particles で and に are used with locations, and also see the particle と being used to mean something like 'with'.\n5m\nRelated\nExpand All\nと共に\nとともに\nTogether with, At the same time,  As well as\nN3 Grammar\nBoth と and と共に express mutual action or relation, similar to the English 'with.' と is more common and used in everyday speech and writing, but it only follows nouns (e.g., 友だちと遊ぶ = 'play with a friend'). In contrast, と共に is more formal, appearing in written Japanese or structured speech, and can follow verbs, adjectives, and nouns.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nトム\nと\n話\n(\nはな\n)\nす\n。\nI talk\nwith\nTom.\n僕\n(\nぼく\n)\nは\n友達\n(\nともだち\n)\nと\nごはん\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nる\n。\nI will eat food\nwith\nmy friend.\nゆうこさん\nは\nみおさん\nと\nコーヒー\nを\n飲\n(\nの\n)\nむ\n。\nYuko-san drinks coffee\nwith\nMio-san.\nタキ\nは\nミツハ\nと\n走\n(\nはし\n)\nる\n。\nTaki runs\nwith\nMitsuha.\n田中\n(\nたなか\n)\nさん\nは\n山田\n(\nやまだ\n)\nさん\nと\n話\n(\nはな\n)\nす\n。\nTanaka-san talks\nwith\nYamada-san.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nThe Inclusive 「と」 particle\nTae Kim\nOffline\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 113 (Chapter 4)\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 113\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nと – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (9 in total)\nCrossbot\nQuestion about example sentence: コーヒーを妻と飲む。\nHere “wife” is after the particle wo. I was confused by this as when とwas covered in Duolingo (I’m aware perhaps not the best) I walked away with the understanding that a sentence like this would have appeared as 妻とコーヒーをのむ. Is it then possible to place the person with whom you are doing the object to the “right” of the object marking particle? An example sentence written both ways could be\n友達とじゅうどうをします or じゅうどうを友達とします\nWould love some clarification thanks y’all!\nDaru\nCrossbot:\nHere “wife” is after the particle wo\nThe key here is to see each part of the sentence as a component of it, with their order switched around.\n[コーヒーを][妻と飲む]\nI think a good way to think about this is that the particle next to the word establishes that word’s role in the sentence, and not quite the order in which the words appear.\nCrossbot:\n<...\nEnkiEnki\nSo, in English, word order matters a lot more in regards to indicating the role a word plays in a clause. The English word order will most of the time be SVO (Subject-Verb-Object) “She[subject] reads[Verb] books[Object]”. In Japanese, because we have particles that allow us to explicitly mark the role of a word, word order matters a lot less. So you can have things ordered pretty much however you want and retain close to the same meaning. The emphasis of things may just change a bit. The closer to the front of the clause something is, the more it’s emphasized. Which is why the topic is generally placed at the front, but it doesn’t have to be. The only rule is that if a clause contains a verb, it must always come at the end of the clause. A clause can also end in an い-adjective or a copula/sentence ending particle though too if there is no verb. The only thing a clause can’t end with is a noun or な-adjective (which are technically nouns themselves.) Nouns have to have a copula attach...\nGot questions about と?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
