      "sourceText": "でしょう (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 3: 5/14\nでしょう\nRight?, Probably, Isn't it?\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nでしょう\nRight?, Probably, Isn't it?\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nTentative\nNoun +\nでしょう\nVerb +\nでしょう\nAdjective +\nでしょう\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nAuxiliary Verb\nWord Type\nDependent Word\nRegister\nPolite\nAbout でしょう\nでしょう\nis a conjugated form of\nです\n, and can also be seen in the contracted form\nでしょ\n(more casual).\nでしょう\nis regularly translated to 'right?', or 'probably' in English. In a broader sense it just expresses an assertion about something (like\nです\n), that the speaker assumes the listener would probably agree with.\nIn the above, we can see that, in both examples, the speaker is looking for confirmation that/affirming that the listener agrees with them.\nAs\nでしょう\nis a conjugation of\nです\n(the polite form of だ), it usually follows the plain form of words, rather than words using ～ます. However, in very formal situations, or older literature ～ます\nでしょう\nmay be seen.\nCaution\nDespite sounding relatively low confidence, using\nでしょう\nimplies that the speaker has some form of information that makes them believe that the (A) statement is true.\nTopics\nProbably That's Right, Right?\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn how to change the tone of our sentences with でしょう and だろう. Both can be used to mean something similar to 'probably' or 'right?', although with more varied nuances.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nだろう\nRight?, Probably, Isn't it?\nN5 Grammar\nBoth でしょう and だろう express conjecture (e.g., 'It's probably…') or seek confirmation when used with rising intonation in a question (e.g., 面白いでしょう？ or 面白いだろう？ = 'It's interesting, right?'). でしょう is more polite and used with strangers or those of equal or higher rank, while だろう is casual and blunt, used with close friends, family, and in informal settings.\nRelated\nExpand All\nね\nIsn't it?, Right? (Sentence ending particle)\nN5 Grammar\nでしょう is a form of です used when the speaker is uncertain, often translated as 'probably,' 'I guess,' or '...right?' when asking a question. In contrast, ね is a sentence-ending particle used to seek confirmation or agreement, similar to '...isn't it?' When combined (e.g., 面白いでしょうね), the sentence expresses both uncertainty and a request for agreement: 'It's probably interesting, isn't it?'\nのではないだろうか\nのではないでしょうか\nI think ... isn't it?, It might be that..., Don't you agree?\nN2 Grammar\nでしょう is a form of です used to express mild uncertainty (e.g., 'It's probably...') or to soften a statement. When used with a question intonation, it seeks the listener's agreement (e.g., 'It's this, right?'). In contrast, のではないだろうか expresses more doubt and seeks confirmation or approval (e.g., 'I wonder if it's…?'). In polite speech, だろう is replaced with でしょう, as in のではないでしょうか.\nかもしれない\nMight, Maybe, Probably\nN4 Grammar\nかもしれない (polite form かもしれません) and でしょう both express uncertainty or possibility, softening a statement. The main difference is the degree of certainty: かもしれない expresses less certainty, similar to 'maybe' or 'might,' while でしょう (casual だろう) expresses a higher degree of certainty, similar to 'probably' or 'I guess.'\nじゃないか\nIsn't it?, Is it not?\nN4 Grammar\nでしょう, in addition to expressing conjecture, can also seek confirmation or agreement from the listener (e.g., 面白いでしょう = 'Interesting, right?'). じゃないか is more forceful and casual, implying the speaker strongly believes something and expects the listener to agree (e.g., 面白いじゃないか = 'Interesting, isn't it!'). Note that でしょう is polite, while じゃないか is very casual.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nこれ\nは\nあなた\nの\n本\n(\nほん\n)\nでしょう\n。\nThis is your book,\nright?\nいい\nでしょう\n。\nIt is\nprobably\ngood. (\nright?\n)\n明日\n(\nあした\n)\nは\n寒\n(\nさむ\n)\nい\nでしょう\n。\nIt will\nprobably\nbe cold tomorrow.\nあなた\nは\nトム\nでしょう\n？\nYou are Tom,\nright\n?\nこれ\nは\n、\nいい\n車\n(\nくるま\n)\nでしょう\n。\nThis is a good car,\nright?\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nDifference between そうです、ようです、だろう and らしいです\nBriefJapanese\nUsing 「でしょう」 to express a fair amount of certainty (polite)\nTae Kim\nWhat's the difference between でしょうか and ですか at the end of a question?\nJStackExchange\nOffline\nGenki I\nPage 234\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 274\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 280\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 207\nTobira\nPage 45\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 134 [CH 21]\nみんなの日本語 II\nPage 48 [CH 32]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nでしょう – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (16 in total)\nka5\nSentence-ending particle よ specifically implies the speaker is telling the listener new information.\nこれはあなたの本でしょう。\nI think this is probably your book.\nこれはあなたの本ですよ。\nI know for sure that this is your book, and it looks to me like you don’t think so/don’t recognize it/have forgotten that.\nThese two are probably distinguishable from translation without hints. But maybe there could be a better hint keyword for でしょう, since it’s not a very assertive assertion.\nmrhollow\nああ、なるほど、ありがとうございます。\nXelbas\nIs this explanation wrong then?\nDespite sounding relatively low confidence, using でしょう implies that the speaker has some form of information that makes them believe that the (A) statement is true.\nSource: でしょう (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nGot questions about でしょう?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
