      "sourceText": "がある (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 3: 7/14\nが\n有\n(\nあ\n)\nる\nTo be, There is\n有る kanji use can be seen\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nがある\nTo be, There is\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nNoun +\nが\n+ ある\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nExpression\nWord Type\nVerb\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout がある\nがある\nis a common construction that is simply a combination of the\nが\nparticle, and the\nう-Verb\n,\nある\n. This expression means 'to exist', or 'there is', and is used in relation to non-living/inanimate things.\nBecause the things being described in these examples are not considered to be 'alive',\nある\nis the verb that will be used.\nいる\nis used for living things.\nThe polite form of\nある\nconjugates as\nあります\n. Due to the る changing to り, we can determine that ある is a\nう-Verb (Godan verb)\n. However,\nある\nis irregular in that the plain negative form is\nない\n, not あらない.\nThe kanji form of\n有\n(\nあ\n)\nる\nis quite common, and should be learned as early as possible, to save any confusion while reading. There is no difference in nuance between this expression being used with, or without kanji, and is up to the preference of the writer.\nCaution\nThe\nが\nin\nがある\nis often omitted in casual speech, or writing that behaves like casual speech (manga, instant messaging, etc).\nTopics\nExistential Crisis\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to take a closer look at the verbs ある and いる, which are used to say that someone or something exists. We'll dive into the full range of uses and nuances in the details section.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nがいる\nがいる\nTo be, There is,  To remain (in a state)\nN5 Grammar\nBoth がある and がいる combine the particle が with a verb to express existence or possession. The difference is that ある refers to inanimate objects or intangible concepts (e.g., 車がある = 'I have a car' or 'There is a car,' アイデアがある = 'I have an idea'), while いる is used for living or animate things (e.g., 猫がいる = 'I have a cat' or 'There is a cat').\nRelated\nExpand All\nです\nTo be, Is\nN5 Grammar\nThe phrase ～がある is used to show existence or possession. For example, 本がある means 'There is a book' or 'I have a book.' On the other hand, です is used to identify something, like 本です ('It is a book'). がある is more casual and often used with close friends or in certain grammar patterns, while です is polite.\nだ\nTo be, Is\nN5 Grammar\nがある indicates the existence or possession of something. For example, ペンがある means 'There is a pen (on the table, etc.)' or 'I have a pen.' On the other hand, だ expresses assertion or determination similar to 'is/are' in English. In contrast to the がある example, ペンだ would mean 'It (=the thing we are looking at) is a pen.'\nがある + Noun\nがある + (Noun)\nNoun (B) that has Noun (A), Noun (B) with Noun (A)\nN5 Grammar\nがある expresses the existence or possession of inanimate objects, similar to 'there is' or 'I have' in English (e.g., ピアノがある = 'There is a piano' or 'I have a piano'). When combined with a noun, it describes the noun, much like an adjective (e.g., ピアノがあるレストラン = 'A restaurant with a piano'). Remember, ある changes to いる when referring to animate objects.\nである\nFormal or literary だ, Authoritative, Copula\nN3 Grammar\nがある expresses the existence or possession of inanimate objects, similar to saying 'there is' or 'I have' in English. In contrast, である is comparable to (and is, in fact, an alternate form of) the casual だ or polite です, and is therefore equivalent to the English \"it is\". In other words, ピアノがある means 'There's a piano' or 'I have a piano', but ピアノである means 'It's a piano' in formal/written language (the spoken polite equivalent would be ピアノです).\nございます\nございます\nTo be, To exist (Polite)\nN4 Grammar\nある and ございます are both verbs of existence. ある is the plain form used in everyday conversation, while ございます is the polite form used in formal settings or speeches. For example, 本がある means 'I have / there is a book' in casual speech, while 本がございます is the same phrase in a more polite and formal context (note that が must be used in both cases).\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n本\n(\nほん\n)\nがある\n。\nThere is\na book.\nペン\nがない\n。\nI\ndon't have\na pen.\n時間\n(\nじかん\n)\nがあります\n。\nThere is\ntime.\n仕事\n(\nしごと\n)\nがない\n。\nI\ndon't have\nwork.\nエレベータ\nがあります\n。\nThere is\nan elevator.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\n#1 Common Mistakes じゃない (JA nai) vs がない (GA nai) / いる (iru) vs ある (aru)\nJapanese Ammo\nBreakdown and example sentences\nJLPT 先生\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 10 & 73\nGenki I\nPage 77\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 107\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 107\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 62 [CH 9]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nがある – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (14 in total)\nXReaper95\nI have a a problem… i think. I hide the english hints completely when doing reviews, just for an extra challenge, but some examples show\n[casual]\nas a placeholder, so there is no way to know when to write がある or だ. Some proof:\nThis shows for some examples of different questions:\nmatt_in_mito\nThe site doesn’t work too well without English translations. For your extra challenge I suggest that you switch to yellow text only, so you know the grammar point you’re aiming for, but don’t get the full sentence in English.\nXReaper95\nI see, well thats to bad, I was not expecting to run into a problem like that, as that is an option they have. I guess that using the smallest hint is the solution then. Thank you!\nGot questions about がある?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
