      "sourceText": "のがすき (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 4: 11/13\nのが\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\nLike doing, Love doing\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nのがすき\nLike doing, Love doing\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nVerb［る］\n+\nの\n+\nが\n+\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\n+\nだ\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nExpression\nWord Type\nAdjectival Noun\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout のがすき\n～のが\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\nis a common construction that is used in Japanese to express that you like/love doing something. This particular expression is used with the plain (\ndictionary\n) form of verbs only.\nIn these examples, we can see that there is no difference between\nIchidan verbs\nand\nGodan verbs\n. Both will be used in their plain forms. In this expression,\nのが\nis performing the same function as in the regular\nのは\nor\nのが\nconstruction. This function is nominalization (turning a phrase into a noun).\nCaution\nIf you want to express that you liked doing something (in the past), the verb will still remain in the plain form, but\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\nwill change to past tense.\nThis construction can also be used to ask questions about what other people like. This is done through the addition of\nか\n.\nTopics\nThe One Who Likes Studying Grammar\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn how の can be used like a noun, and what this actually means for a sentence. We'll also look more closely at two very common and important な-Adjectives,\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき 'like' and\n嫌\n(\nきら\n)\nい 'hate', which appear frequently with this grammar pattern.\n3m\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nのがへた\nのがへた\nTo be bad at, Poor at, Unskillful\nN5 Grammar\nThese phrases combine the nominalizer の with the な-Adjectives 好き and 下手. They are used to express liking something (好き) or not being good at something (下手). In Japanese culture, boasting is rare, so while 下手 can be used to humbly describe a lack of ability, 好き serves as a more modest alternative to the boastful-sounding 上手.\nRelated\nExpand All\nのがじょうず\nのがじょうず\nTo be good at, Proficient\nN5 Grammar\nThese phrases combine the nominalizer, or 'dummy noun,' の with the な-Adjectives 好き and 上手. They are used to express liking something (好き) or being good at something (上手) by attaching them to a verb. In Japanese culture, boasting about one's abilities is rare, so 絵を描くのが好き might be preferred over 上手 when talking about being good at drawing.\nこと\nConverting a verb into a noun\nN4 Grammar\nThis phrase combines the nominalizer の with the な-Adjective 好き to express liking an activity (e.g., 本を読むのが好き = 'I like to read books'). の can be replaced with こと, another nominalizer. The difference is subtle: の implies a concrete or tangible action, while こと is more abstract (e.g., 本を読むことが好き implies liking the 'act of reading').\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\nサッカー\nを\nする\nのが\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\n。\nI like\nplay\ning\nsoccer.\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\n本\n(\nほん\n)\nを\n読\n(\nよ\n)\nむ\nのが\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\n。\nI\nlike\nread\ning\nbooks.\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nは\nラーメン\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nる\nのが\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\nです\n。\nI like\neat\ning\nramen.\n犬\n(\nいぬ\n)\nと\n遊\n(\nあそ\n)\nぶ\nのが\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\n。\nI\nlike\nplay\ning\nwith dogs.\n旅行\n(\nりょこう\n)\nに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nく\nのが\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\nでしょう\n？\nYou\nlike\ngo\ning\non trips, don't you?\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\n「～のがすきです」\nE-Japanese\n「～のがすきです」\nWikiBooks\nSee examples under Comic 6\nTae Kim\nOffline\nGenki I\nPage 157\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 194\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 194\nみんなの日本語 II\nPage 12 [CH 26]\nみんなの日本語 II\nPage 84 [CH 38]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nのがすき – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (4 in total)\nmrnoone\nHey\n好き demands for the object of liking to be marked with が.\nSo it is always Someoneはsomethingが好き。\nSame applies to 嫌い。\nTHERE IS AN EXCEPTION THOUGH:\nは is also a particle that expresses contrast.\nSo if you want to express contrast it is OK to use のは。\nExample:\n（私は）テレビを見るのは好き。でもラジオを聞くのは好きではないｌ\nI like watching TV. But I don’t like listening to the radio.\nThere is contrast expressed.\nGregX999\nHow does this differ from ことが好き? This review question told me “Can you say it another way?” when I answered with ことがすき, (with no other hints) so are they interchangeable?\nmrnoone\nThey are\nの is used more often with 好き and 嫌い though.\nI'm having some troubles with verb nominalizers\nGot questions about のがすき?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
