      "sourceText": "がある + Noun (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 4: 12/13\nがある + (Noun)\nNoun (B) that has Noun (A), Noun (B) with Noun (A)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nがある + Noun\nNoun (B) that has Noun (A), Noun (B) with Noun (A)\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nNoun +\nがある\n(1)\n+ Noun\n(1)\nのある\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nExpression\nWord Type\nNoun\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout がある + Noun\nIn this expression,\nがある\nhas the role of describing the noun that follows it. The description that it is giving is '(A)\nがある\n(B)' = '(B) has/with (A)'. This type of phrase is called a relative clause (something that describes a noun), and behaves similarly to an adjective.\nIn English, a relative clause would require a\npronoun\nsuch as 'who'\n誰\n(\nだれ\n)\n, 'which' どれ, 'that' それ・あれ, 'where' どこ. However, these types of words are not required in Japanese, and the noun will follow\nがある\ndirectly.\nCaution\nIn a relative clause, because (A) is describing (B), (B) will be considered the 'main topic/subject'. This means that\nは\nwill generally not be used in place of\nが\n. However,\nの\ncan be used instead of\nが\n, as showing a relationship between (A) and (B) is one of the main functions of\nの\n.\nTopics\nIt's All Relative\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about relative clauses. With relative clauses we will be able to take verb phrases and use them to describe nouns, giving us even more ways to give detail about the things we're talking about.\n3m\nRelated\nExpand All\nの\nIndicates possession\nN5 Grammar\nがある + Noun expresses possession, using a phrase with a noun, the particle が, and the verb ある to describe another noun (e.g., ピアノがあるレストラン = 'a restaurant with a piano'). In these cases, が can be replaced with の without changing the meaning (e.g., ピアノのあるレストラン).\nがある\nがある\nTo be, There is\nN5 Grammar\nがある expresses the existence or possession of inanimate objects, similar to 'there is' or 'I have' in English (e.g., ピアノがある = 'There is a piano' or 'I have a piano'). When combined with a noun, it describes the noun, much like an adjective (e.g., ピアノがあるレストラン = 'A restaurant with a piano'). Remember, ある changes to いる when referring to animate objects.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nベッド\nがある\n部屋\n(\nへや\n)\n。\nA room\nthat has\na bed.\nたくさん\n休\n(\nやす\n)\nみ\nがある\n５\n(\nご\n)\n月\n(\nがつ\n)\nが\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\n。\nI like the month of May\nwith\nits many holidays.\n綺麗\n(\nきれい\n)\nな\n池\n(\nいけ\n)\nがある\n公園\n(\nこうえん\n)\n。\nA park\nwith\na beautiful pond.\n家\n(\nいえ\n)\nがある\n人\n(\nひと\n)\n。\nPeople\nwho have\na house.\n冷蔵庫\n(\nれいぞうこ\n)\nがある\n台所\n(\nだいどころ\n)\n。\nA kitchen\nwith\na refrigerator.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nGrammar: Qualifying/Modifying Nouns\nNihonOnTheGo\nTreating verbs and state-of-being like adjectives\nTae Kim\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 376 - Relative Clause\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 63\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 62 [CH 9]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nがある + Noun – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (22 in total)\nmachinaeZER0\nIs the がある here “the same” as がある/がいる (to be/there is)? And if so, is there ever an instance where がいる would be used in a similar manner? (Hopefully this question makes sense - I know がある is for non-living things and がいる is for living ones, but that may only be relevant when not used in this type of clause (and/or I haven’t gotten to the がいる version yet).\nThanks!\nka5\nYes, it’s the same がある, just used as a relative clause modifying a noun. And yes, you can also use がいる in a relative clause in the same way. Or pretty much any other verb.\nFor example, from 安達としまむら (https://learnnatively.com/book/d55ed32253/):\n遠ざかっていく\n日野たちのいる橋\nの方を一瞥してから、しまむらが笑った。\nShimamura glanced in the direction of the\nbridge where Hino and her friend\nwere riding away, and laughed.\nHere 日野たち are people, and so we see のいる＝がいる being used to describe the bridge in question.\nmachinaeZER0\nThank you, this is super helpful!\nGot questions about がある + Noun?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
