      "sourceText": "だけ (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 7: 1/13\nだけ\nOnly, Just\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nだけ\nOnly, Just\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb +\nだけ\n［い］Adjective\n+\nだけ\n［な］Adjective +\nな\n+\nだけ\nNoun +\nだけ\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nAdverbial Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout だけ\nだけ\nis used in Japanese in a similar way to 'only' or 'just' in English. The basic meaning of this word is that 'while there are/were several possibilities, only (A) is true/was chosen'.\nだけ\nis used by attaching it to the base (dictionary) form of any word, except for\nな-Adjectives\n, which require\nな\nwhen being used adjectivally.\nDue to\nな-Adjectives\nbeing able to be used as either a noun or an adjective, the meaning of a sentence will be different depending on whether\nだけ\nis used with the noun form, or the adjective form.\nBasically this difference just highlights whether something is being discussed in a measurable way (an adjective), or an immeasurable way (a noun).\nTopics\nOnly That Is My Limit\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about the adverbial particle だけ. だけ lets us put a limit on something, saying it 'only' or 'just'. We'll also explore the core meaning of だけ in the details section.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nしか～ない\nしか～ない\nThere's only, Nothing but\nN4 Grammar\nだけ conveys 'only' or 'just.' しか～ない combines the particle しか with a negative verb to express 'nothing but.' While the two have similar meanings, their structures differ. For example, the equivalent of バナナだけ食べた ('I ate only a banana') would be バナナしか食べなかった ('I ate nothing but a banana'). Compared to だけ, しか～ない emphasizes the limitation or lack of other options more strongly.\nだけしか\nだけしか～ない\nTo emphasize '~only', Nothing but\nN3 Grammar\nだけ conveys the meaning of 'only' or 'just.' だけしか is a more emphatic expression, comparable to the English 'nothing but.' In addition to the increased emphasis, だけしか has more limited usage. While だけ can be used after nouns, verbs, and adjectives, だけしか is typically used only after nouns and is almost always followed by a negative verb (similar to the base しか～ない pattern).\nAntonyms\nExpand All\nのみならず\nNot only...but, Not just...but, What is more\nN2 Grammar\nだけ conveys 'only' or 'just.' In contrast, のみならず is a formal expression similar to だけじゃなく, meaning 'not only A, but also B.' While だけ expresses limitation, のみならず emphasizes a lack of restriction. Note that while だけ is commonly used in both everyday speech and writing, のみならず is more formal and typically limited to written language or formal speech.\nだけでなく\nNot only… but also\nN4 Grammar\nだけ conveys the meaning of 'only' or 'just'. だけでなく (or variant forms like だけではなく, だけじゃなくて, etc.) is a negation of this, combining だけ with で (the conjuctive form of だ) and なく (the conjunctive form of ない) to express the meaning of ‘not only (A)...but also (B)’. Unlike だけ, だけでなく will always be followed by a continuation of the sentence providing additional information, e.g. バナナだけでなく、リンゴも食べました (‘I ate not only a banana, but also an apple.’).\nRelated\nExpand All\nきり\nOnly, Just, Since\nN3 Grammar\nだけ means 'only' or 'just,' while きり has a more specific nuance. After a past tense verb, きり suggests a permanent change after an event. For example, 日本に行ったきり、戻ってこなかった means 'Once (he) went to Japan, he never returned.' When used after a number + counter (e.g. 一人きり), it emphasizes 'nothing more, nothing less.'\nだけで\nJust by, Just with\nN4 Grammar\nだけ conveys the meaning of 'only' or 'just.' だけで is the combination of だけ with the particle で, conveying the idea of the manner or means by which something is done. だけで expresses 'just by' or 'merely with' (literally, 'with/using only A'). It typically follows either nouns or verbs, suggesting that the thing or action was the only thing needed to achieve a certain result.\nばかり\nJust, Only, Nothing but\nN3 Grammar\nBoth ばかり and だけ express limitation, similar to 'only,' but with different nuances. 漫画だけ読んだ means '(He) read only comic books,' while 漫画ばかり読んだ implies '(He) read nothing but comic books,' with a suggestion that other reading would be better. だけ connects to the dictionary form, while ばかり connects to the verb's て form.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n一人\n(\nひとり\n)\nだけ\nです\n。\nOnly\none person.\nそれ\nだけ\nです\n。\nOnly\nthat.\nサッカー\nだけ\nが\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき\n。\nI\nonly\nlike soccer.\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nだけ\n行\n(\nい\n)\nきます\n。\nOnly\nI am going.\nその\n映画\n(\nえいが\n)\nは\n長\n(\nなが\n)\nい\nだけ\n。\nThat movie is\njust\nlong.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nIndicating that's all there is using 「だけ」\nTae Kim\nThe Particle だけ\nImabi\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 93\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 261\nMarugoto Elementary 2 (A2) Rikai\nPage 91\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 244\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 74 [CH 11]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nだけ – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (22 in total)\nwlan8051\nIs the structure ~たいだけ a separate grammar point? See example:\nお父さんは出張で疲れているだろうから、\nねたいだけ\nねさせてあげよ。\nI understand that it translates to “… let him sleep as much as he wants”. Is this usage explained somewhere on Bunpro? I could not find it.\nJohn-Machida\nWelcome back to the forums!\nYou raise an interesting question that actually gets to the heart of what だけ really means in Japanese.\nIt’s typically translated/glossed as ‘only’ or ‘just’, but the fundamental idea of だけ is that it’s indicating an extent.\nFor example, if you take this sentence from the writeup on だけ:\n日本語\nを\n勉強\nするのは、\n月曜日\nだけです。\nI study Japanese\nonly\non Mondays.\nBasically, what it’s saying is ‘the extent of when I study Japanese is on Mondays’.\nIn these terms, the だけ of 寝たいだけ isn’t doing anything significantly different. 寝たいだけ寝る means ‘to sleep to the extent that one wants to sleep’, which would be equiavalent to ‘as much as one wants’.\nSo it’s less that ～たいだけ is functioning in a completely different way, and more that the ‘extent’ nuance of だけ shifts a bit depending on what’s around it.\nI do agree that this isn’t really covered at the moment in the grammar writeup for だけ. ...\nwlan8051\n@John-Machida your explanation makes a lot of sense, thank you for sharing.\nHowever, in my head if I try to understand だけ as “to the extent of …” its meaning will sometimes overlap with ほど、ぐらい or まで. Maybe in some use cases, they are interchangeable but I guess in most of them they are not. I will need to depend on the amazing explanations from the Bunpro staff to clarify those differences\nGood to hear that Bunpro is still evolving, your work is much appreciated.\nGot questions about だけ?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
