      "sha256": "7b5737fab74a994e82a18ca7d8e046d7b9004aa0c12f2320f85709ac57d21a6b",
      "sourceText": "を (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 2: 6/13\nを\nObject marking particle\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nを\nObject marking particle\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nObject +\nを\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nCase Marking Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout を\nIn Japanese,\nを\nis a particle that marks the object of an action. This means that the\nsubject\n(the person or thing performing an action) is performing that action with the\nobject\nbeing the goal/target of the action. In most cases, whatever\nを\nis attached to will not actually be 'doing' anything, but will instead have something being done to it.\nIn these sentences,\nを\nis marking the thing that is having an action performed 'on' it. This is how the Japanese language views the '\nobject\n'. However, unlike English, an\nobject\ncan also be a place in which an action is performed 'through', if the goal of that action is based in that location itself.\nIn these sentences,\nを\nis marking the place 'through' which the action is taking place. This use of\nを\nhighlights that the place itself is what is being interacted with (running/walking requires interaction with the ground, so the ground/place is the object).\nTopics\nGetting Into Action\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to get our first look at Japanese verbs, and learn about two main types. る-Verbs and う-Verbs. Knowing the difference is important, as it affects how they conjugate (change so other words can attach to them).\n4m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nに\nIn, At, To, For, On\nN5 Grammar\nを marks the direct object, the thing an action is done to (e.g., リンゴを食べた = '[I] ate an apple'). に has multiple functions, including marking the destination of movement or the target of an action. Be aware that grammar patterns can differ between English and Japanese: for example, 'I passed the test' uses 試験に合格する in Japanese, not 試験を合格する.\nRelated\nExpand All\nが\nSubject marking particle, Identification particle\nN5 Grammar\nが and を are basic Japanese particles. が marks the subject—the one doing the action, while を marks the direct object, the thing being acted upon. For example, ジョンがリンゴを食べた means 'John ate an apple.' が is also used in expressions of preference or ability, like コーヒーが好きです ('I like coffee') or 英語がわかります ('I understand English').\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n漢字\n(\nかんじ\n)\nを\n覚\n(\nおぼ\n)\nえ\nる\n。\nTo memorize kanji. (\nobject marker\n)\n何\n(\nなに\n)\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nます\nか\n。\nWhat will you have to eat? (\nobject marker\n)\nバター\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nる\n。\nTo eat butter. (\nobject marker\n)\nスポーツ\nを\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nる\n。\nTo watch sports. (\nobject marker\n)\n時計\n(\nとけい\n)\nを\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nる\n。\nTo look at the clock. (\nobject marker\n)\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nAll uses of particle を explained\nBriefJapanese\nThe direct object 「を」 particle\nTae Kim\nThe Particle を: The Direct Object\nImabi\nVerbs basics & を particle\nJapanese Ammo\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 347\nGenki I\nPage 61\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 90\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 88\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 54\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 44 [CH 6]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nを – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (16 in total)\ndarzington\nIs it a thing to use を multiple times in a sentence? I’d not noticed that before until I saw this sample sentence on WK:\n人を手を使って招くことを「手招きする」って言うんですよ。\nIt’s called “beckoning” to use your hands to invite people in.\nThe double-を at the beginning threw me off. Having read the English translation, I think I get how it breaks down but I’d like to confirm with someone who knows better! (And of course while writing this I noticed the third を before the quote…)\n人を (\n手を使って\n) 招くことを「手招きする」って言うんですよ。\nusing your\nhands\n(\n人を\n) 手を使って (\n招く\n) ことを「手招きする」って言うんですよ。\ninvite\npeople\nin\n(using your hands)\n…and honestly I’m not sure how the を before the quote fits in.\nFredKore\n@darzington  I’d break it up like this:\n手を使う = use (your) hands\n手を使って招く = use your hands and invite = beckon with your hands\n人を手を使って招く = beckon a person with your hands\n人を手を使って招くこと = the act of beckoning a person with your hands = [\nA\n]\n[\nA\n]を[\nB\n]って言うんですよ = (we/they) call [\nA\n] [\nB\n] = we/they call the [\nA]\nthing as [\nB\n]\nI added more words because just “[A] [B]” might be confusing.\nSo then,\n人を手を使って招くこと\nを「\n手招きする\n」って言うんですよ。\n(we/they) call the act of beckoning a person with your hands「手招きする」.\n–or–\nIt’s called “beckoning” to use your hands to invite people in.\nThis sentence is unusual because it has so many を’s near each other, but it makes sense when you see each one belongs to a different verb.\n(Also,...\ndarzington\nAwesome, thanks!\nGot questions about を?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
