      "sourceText": "の (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 4: 1/13\nのは・のが\nVerb nominalizer, The one who..., That which...\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nの\nVerb nominalizer, The one who..., That which...\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nVerb +\nの\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nParticle\nWord Type\nCase Marking Particle\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout の\nIn Japanese,\nのは\nand\nのが\nbehave in a very similar way to the words 'that' and 'which' in English. This means that they perform a task called\nnominalization\n.\nNominalization\nis when a phrase is treated in the same way as a single noun.\nRather than a single noun, we can see from these examples that the phrases\n沢山\n(\nたくさん\n)\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nる\nのは\nand バスに\n乗\n(\nの\n)\nる\nのが\nare behaving in the same way that a single noun would. In English, this can either mean 'that which', or 'the one who/that'.\nThis construction can be used after verbs in any tense, except for the polite ます or ません, which are only used at the end of a sentence, or certain clauses.\nCaution\nの\ncannot be used as a substitute for\nこと\nin set expressions like\nことができる\n, or\nことがある\n. It also may not be followed by\nだ\n,\nである\n, or\nです\n, as this would become the explanatory\n～んです\n, or\nのです\nin that case.\nTopics\nThe One Who Likes Studying Grammar\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn how の can be used like a noun, and what this actually means for a sentence. We'll also look more closely at two very common and important な-Adjectives,\n好\n(\nす\n)\nき 'like' and\n嫌\n(\nきら\n)\nい 'hate', which appear frequently with this grammar pattern.\n3m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nAdjective + の(は)\n(Adjective) + のは\nThe 'one' that... (Indefinite pronoun, Adjective nominalization)\nN5 Grammar\nBoth of these patterns use の to turn a phrase into a noun. For example, the verb 走る ('to run') becomes 走るのが好き ('I like running') because the subject marker が must follow a noun. The Adjective + の(は) pattern works similarly, turning adjectives into noun phrases (e.g., 赤い ('red') becomes 赤いのはきれいです ('The red one is pretty')).\nRelated\nExpand All\nは\nAs for... (Highlights sentence topic)\nN5 Grammar\nThe particle の often shows possession, like the English ''s' (e.g., ケンジの本 means 'Kenji's book'). It can also connect two nouns, as in 日本人の学生 ('a student who is Japanese'). In contrast, は marks the topic of the sentence. Unlike の, it doesn't show possession — it introduces what the rest of the sentence will describe or explain.\nの\nIndicates possession\nN5 Grammar\nの shows possession, like the English 's' (e.g., ケンジの本 = 'Kenji's book'). が marks the subject of a clause, often the doer of an action or the one with a certain trait. In modifying phrases, の and が are sometimes interchangeable without changing the meaning—for example, 先生が書いた本 and 先生の書いた本 both mean 'the book the teacher wrote.'\nの\nPossessive use (Noun omission)\nN5 Grammar\nThe particle の connects nouns to indicate possession or description. For example, 私の本 means 'my book.' When used with the pronoun の (meaning 'one'), the second の is dropped. For instance, to say 'It's mine,' you would say 私のです, not 私ののです.\nParticle + の\n(Particle) + の\nCombined particles\nN3 Grammar\nIn the phrase AのB, の indicates a relationship between A and B, either possession (e.g., ジョンの本 = 'John's book') or description (e.g., 日本人の学生 = 'A Japanese student'). Particle + の is a variation where a phrase with a particle describes another noun (e.g., 先生からの手紙 = 'A letter from the teacher'). Note that particles like に, も, は, が, and を are typically not used in this construction.\nこと\nConverting a verb into a noun\nN4 Grammar\nBoth こと and の turn verbs or adjectives into noun phrases. こと is used for intangible or conceptual ideas, while の is used for more tangible or physical concepts. For example, 走るのが好きです and 走ることが好きです both mean 'I like running,' with の feeling more physical and こと more conceptual. However, they aren't interchangeable in set phrases like ことができる or ことがある.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n読\n(\nよ\n)\nむ\nのは\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nです\n。\nI am\nthe one who\nreads.\n君\n(\nきみ\n)\nを\n愛\n(\nあい\n)\nする\nのは\n、\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nだ\n。\nI am\nthe one who\nloves you.\nこの\n仕事\n(\nしごと\n)\nを\nする\nのは\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nだ\n。\nI am\nthe one who\ndoes this job.\n寿司\n(\nすし\n)\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべ\nる\nのは\n、\n彼\n(\nかれ\n)\nだ\n。\nHe is\nthe one who\neats sushi.\n漢字\n(\nかんじ\n)\nを\n覚\n(\nおぼ\n)\nえ\nる\nのは\n、\n彼女\n(\nかのじょ\n)\nです\n。\nShe is\nthe one who\nmemorizes kanji.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\n*Verb*ing is *adj.*\nJapanese Ammo\nDifference between の and こと nominalizers\nJapanese Stack Exchange\nNominalizers\nJLPTBootcamp\nNominalizers 「こと」and「の」\nWasabi\nThe Nominalizers の and こと\nimabi\nThe の particle\nTae Kim\nの、こと differences and history\nBriefJapanese\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 7, 318 & 337\nMarugoto Elementary 1 (A2) Rikai\nPage 42\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 70\nみんなの日本語 II\nPage 84 [CH 38]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nの – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (22 in total)\nstupidguy\nI’ve read the discussion here.\nIs のが being reserved as a quiz answer for some other lesson’s nuance?\nThis grammar point is labeled のは・のが. I expected either answers or alternate answers using のが.\nのが is talked about and has a couple examples given, but it seems like it’s going to be absent in the quizzes. I tested のが as an answer during the first two quizzes and with two manually created ghosts, and Bunpro nudged me away to のは without explaining why each time. It didn’t mark のが wrong.\nOn Bunpro specifically, should I only use Verb + のが for things like emotive statements and 下手／上手?\nAside from the adjective nominalization form that allows のが usage,  (Adjective) + のは, I only see these のが grammar points waiting: のがすき...\nZackAkai\nI think the phrase “the fact that” would be a good analogy.  Consider the sentence “I couldn’t come to the party last night, because of ‘the fact that’ I was studying for my final.”  (I realize this is still kinda clunky phrasing in English, but hopefully the function/meaning is more clear).  I like to think of it as “the fact that” sort of packages up the clause “I was studying for my final” into it’s own little self-contained noun.\nsteffuld\nI saw this one in a novel today. Usually this translates to ‘the one who’ or ‘that which’, this is what the lesson teaches.\nSo, for me, naturally, to say something like ‘what we were waiting for was…’ you’d have to first turn it into ‘that which was waited for by us was…’ to get\n俺たちに待たれていたのは、…\nBut in reality it’s just\n俺たちが待っていたのは、…\nThis isn’t mentioned anywhere in the lesson, and the examples either use intransitive verbs or have an object marked with を! The Topic mentions it briefly as one example in the end.\nSo far, my best way to parse it is to treat everything before の as a relative clause, as if の can be expanded as もの (or maybe こと in other cases). I don’t know if this is correct though\nGot questions about の?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
