      "sha256": "352f7242979fe01e3f327a52f526f2e5ce8e937d7205b77b8405d5f2f650f93e",
      "sourceText": "たことがある (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 10: 1/12\n～たことがある\nHave done before\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nたことがある\nHave done before\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nVerb［た］\n+\nこと +\nが + ある\nVerb［た］\n+\nこと +\nが + ない\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nExpression\nWord Type\nVerb\nRegister\nStandard\nAbout たことがある\nたことがある\nis a set expression in Japanese that is used for saying that 'there has been a time that I have done (A)'.\nThe\nauxiliary verb\nた\nwill be added to the verb indicating what you have done, followed by\nこと\n-\nが\n-\nある\n. Like\nの\n,\nこと\nis regularly used for nominalization (creating a noun-like phrase). However,\nの\ncannot replace\nこと\nin this particular grammar pattern.\nFrom these examples, we can see that\nない\n(the negative equivalent of\nある\n) may also be used, when you want to express that you have 'never' done something.\nCaution\nJust like English, many set phrases cannot be adjusted in Japanese (in the same way that\nこと\ncannot be swapped with\nの\nin this expression).\nFun Fact\nIn expressions where\nこと\nis followed by a verb phrase,\nの\nmay not be used. When used for nominalization,\nの\nis almost always followed by a noun or adjective phrase. Here are some examples.\nTopics\nHigh Tension\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn more phrases to do with experience and tense. Specifically, we will learn about the pattern ～たことがある, which is used to talk about things we 'have done', and about the final use of ている, which highlights habitual or repeated activities. With these, we'll have a complete knowledge of tense and aspect at the N5 level!\n3m\nRelated\nExpand All\nたものだ\n～たものだ\nUsed to\nN3 Grammar\nBoth expressions describe past experiences but with different nuances. たことがある states that something has happened before, as in 日本に行ったことがある ('I've been to Japan'). たものだ adds a nostalgic tone and refers to things done regularly in the past, as in 子どもの頃、よくこの公園で遊んだものだ ('I often played at this park when I was a child').\nことがある\nことがある\nSomething is possible, Sometimes, Occasionally, There are times when\nN3 Grammar\nWith a non-past verb, ことがある expresses that something happens occasionally or from time to time, similar to saying 'There are times when…' In contrast, たことがある uses the past tense to describe a completed experience. For example, 日本に行ったことがある means 'I've been to Japan.' It refers to something that has happened at least once, without implying it occurred repeatedly.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\nここ\nで\nサッカー\nを\nしたことがあります\nか\n。\nHave you ever played\nsoccer here\nbefore\n?\n日本\n(\nにほん\n)\nに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nったことがあります\nか\n。\nHave you ever\nbeen to Japan\nbefore\n?\nこの\n本\n(\nほん\n)\nを\n読\n(\nよ\n)\nんだことがある\n。\nI\nhave read\nthis book\nbefore\n.\n私\n(\nわたし\n)\nの\n家\n(\nいえ\n)\nを\n見\n(\nみ\n)\nたことがあります\nか\n。\nHave you ever seen\nmy house\nbefore\n?\n６\n(\nろく\n)\n時\n(\nじ\n)\n前\n(\nまえ\n)\nに\n起\n(\nお\n)\nきたことがない\n。\nI\nhave never woken up\nbefore six o'clock.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nExperiences\nTae Kim\nExpressions with こと\nImabi\nHave been / done たことがある\nJapanese Ammo\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 196\nGenki I\nPage 216\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 256\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 260\nTae Kim's Japanese Grammar Guide\nPage 236\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 122 [CH 19]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nたことがある – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (22 in total)\nSFJerry\nIn the example sentence provided by Bunpro, would the polite form of 私はお菓子を買ったことが無い be to simply change 無い to ありません?\nBreadmanNin\nThat’s exactly right. ことがありません is indeed the correct way to use this expression in polite speech.\nSFJerry\nThanks!  私はこのフレーズを以前に使ったことがありません。   But now I know!\nGot questions about たことがある?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
