      "sha256": "b8bf01e587c7ed581fea8e8a1d63c016424424f3b1a44573ea78e90e2f2a8582",
      "sourceText": "ませんか (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 9: 3/13\nませんか\nWon't you, Would you not, Why don't we\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nませんか\nWon't you, Would you not, Why don't we\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nVerb［ない］ +\nか\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nExpression\nWord Type\nSentence Ending Particle\nRegister\nPolite\nAbout ませんか\nませんか\n(or\nないか\nmore casually) is an expression that is used after verbs as an invitation. It basically translates to 'won't you do (A) with me?'.\nThis expression is a little bit different to\nましょうか\n, in that it sounds directly like an invitation to do (A).\nましょうか\ntends to sound more like a suggestion that (A) should be done.\nIt is recommended to use the\nませんか\nversion, as\nないか\nwould be a bit too casual to use with someone that you are not very close with (and is also technically not correct Japanese).\nTo make it clearer that the speaker wants to do (A) 'with' someone, expressions like\n一緒\n(\nいっしょ\n)\nに\n'together' are often added, for clarification.\nTo examine the difference between\nましょうか\nand\nませんか\n, let's have a look at how the nuance changes in the same type of sentence.\nHere,\nませんか\nwill come across as an invitation due to the fact that you are asking the person if they 'wouldn't mind doing (A)' with you. This is the main reason that it is considered a more polite option when inviting someone to do something, than\nましょうか\n.\nTopics\nShall We?\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we're going to learn about how to make invitations and suggestions using different forms of the auxiliary verb ます. Specifically, we'll see the patterns ましょう and ましょうか, used for suggestions, and ませんか, used for invitations. With these, we'll be able to make social plans and soften our statements.\n2m\nRelated\nExpand All\nましょう\nLet's, Shall we (Polite volitional)\nN5 Grammar\nましょう and ませんか are both polite expressions based on the ます form that can be used to make suggestions or invitations. ましょう is more assertive and usually implies the speaker and listener will do something together (e.g., 食べましょう = 'Let's eat'). In contrast, ませんか is more of an invitation directed at the listener (e.g., 食べませんか = 'Won't you eat [some]?').\n～ましょうか\nましょうか\nShall we, Shall I\nN5 Grammar\nましょう and ませんか are both polite forms based on the ます form and used to make suggestions or invitations. ましょう typically suggests doing something together (e.g. 食べましょう = 'Let's eat'), while ませんか invites the listener to join (e.g. 食べませんか = 'Won't you eat [some]?') ましょう is more assertive, while ませんか is softer and more inviting.\nたい\nWant to do\nN5 Grammar\nませんか is a polite way to extend an invitation, similar to 'Wouldn't you like to...?' in English. For example, 何か食べませんか？ ('Won't you eat something?') might be used when politely offering food to a guest. Note that unlike English, the たい form ('Do you want to…?') isn't typically used to invite someone in Japanese—何か食べたいですか？ simply asks if the person wants to eat something.\nたらどう\nWhy don't you...?, What if you did...?\nN4 Grammar\nませんか is a polite way to extend an invitation, similar to 'Won't you…?' or 'Wouldn't you like to...?' in English. In contrast, たらどう has a stronger tone and is used to suggest or advise a course of action. For example, 何か食べませんか？ is a polite offer (e.g. to a guest), while 何か食べたらどう？ implies the speaker thinks the listener should eat something.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n寿司\n(\nすし\n)\nを\n食\n(\nた\n)\nべに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nきませんか\n。\nWon't you go eat\nsushi (\nwith me\n)\n?\nサッカー\nを\nしませんか\n。\nWon't you play\nsoccer (\nwith me\n)\n?\n一緒\n(\nいっしょ\n)\nに\n文法\n(\nぶんぽう\n)\nを\n勉強\n(\nべんきょう\n)\nしませんか\n。\nWon't you study\ngrammar\nwith me?\nお\n風呂\n(\nふろ\n)\nに\n一緒\n(\nいっしょ\n)\nに\n入\n(\nはい\n)\nりませんか\n。\nWon't you take a bath with me?\n金曜日\n(\nきんようび\n)\nに\n、\n買\n(\nか\n)\nい\n物\n(\nもの\n)\nに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nきませんか\n。\nWon't you\ngo shopping (\nwith me\n) on Friday\n?\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\n～ませんか\nいいね Nihongo\nませんか\nTransparent Language\nOffline\nGenki I\nPage 62\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 92\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 90\nMarugoto Elementary 1 (A2) Rikai\nPage 90\nみんなの日本語 I\nPage 44 [CH 6]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nませんか – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (11 in total)\ngovknar\nIn some of the examples/questions, it seems I need to put し before ませんか, for しませんか… But I can’t seem to find or understand the exact reasoning, unless it has something to do with し as a particle…?\nIcyIceBear\nJake:\nVerb[stem] +\nません・か\nし is a stem form of the verb する, so thats why its added in those sentences.\nwelcome to the community\ngovknar\nAh, naruhodo, thanks for the clarification! Seems like a great community\nGot questions about ませんか?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us\nTestimonials\nAcknowledgements\nFor Teachers\nJapanese\nOfficial Forums\nContent Search\nReading Practice\nGrammar Library\nHiragana & Katakana\nSupport\nContact Us\nSupport & FAQ\n© 2026, Bunpro SRS.\nRead our\nTerms of Service\n,\nPrivacy Policy\nand\nCompany Overview\n.\nMade with\n❤️\nfrom Osaka.\nEnglish",
