      "sourceText": "Invitations: How to say “let’s…” in Japanese?\nSkip to main content\nMANGO\nMANGO\nLanguages\n\nAll Languages\nEnglish (ESL) Courses\nEndangered and Indigenous Languages\nK12 Language Courses\nHow It Works\nOrganizations\n\nLibraries\nK12\nHigher Ed\nBusiness\nGovernment\nHomeschool\nPricing\nResources\n\nArticles & Lessons\nK12 Teacher Resources\nResearch & Efficacy\nCustomer Success Stories\nLog In\nSign Up\n\nLanguages\nAll Languages\nEnglish (ESL) Courses\nEndangered and Indigenous Languages\nK12 Language Courses\nHow It Works\nOrganizations\nLibraries\nK12\nHigher Ed\nBusiness\nGovernment\nHomeschool\nPricing\nResources\nArticles & Lessons\nK12 Teacher Resources\nResearch & Efficacy\nCustomer Success Stories\nLog In\nSign Up\n\nAll\nJapanese\narticles\nInvitations: How to say “let’s…” in Japanese?\nBy:\nMayumi Ito\nUpdated\nSeptember 11, 2026\nOn this page\nWhat are invitation sentences in Japanese?\n\nTo invite the listener to join the speaker in doing a certain action, we can use invitation sentences called\n勧誘文\n(\ntranscribed\nkan’yūbun\n)\nin Japanese. You can form a sentence like this using one of three possible structures:\nInvitation Structure\nEndings\nthe volitional form\n〜ましょう\n(\ntranscribed\nmashō\n)\n〜(よ)う\n(\ntranscribed\nyō\n)\nthe volitional form +\nか\n(\ntranscribed\nka\n)\n〜ましょうか\n(\ntranscribed\nmashō ka\n)\n〜(よ)うか\n(\ntranscribed\nyō ka\n)\na negative question\n〜ませんか\n(\ntranscribed\nmasen ka\n)\n〜ないか\n(\ntranscribed\nnai ka\n)\nWhile all three can be used to invite something to do something, they are not always interchangeable. In this post, we will find out what invitation sentences are and how and when we can use each of the above expressions. Let’s get started or\n始めましょう\n(\ntranscribed\nhajimemashō\n)\n!\nWhat are invitation sentences in Japanese?\nInvitation sentences, which are called\n勧誘文\n(\ntranscribed\nkanyūbun\n)\nin Japanese, are expressions that you can use to\ninvite someone to do something with you\n. There are two types of invitation sentences:\n“Let’s” type:\nIn this type,\nthe speaker and the listener are starting something as a team\n. The speaker encourages the listener to start the action together.\nExample:\nEveryone in the room is dancing except John and Yoko. John invites Yoko to dance together: “Let’s dance,” “Shall we dance?” or “Why don’t we dance?”\n“Join-me” type:\nIn this type,\nthe speaker is already doing something\n, and encourages the listener to join them. Only the listener will be changing their behavior.\nExample:\nJohn has been dancing with his friends, while Yoko is sitting at the table. John approaches Yoko and invites her to join him in dancing: “Won’t you dance too?” or “Why don’t you dance with me?”\nAll three of the structures we mentioned in the introduction can be used to form both kinds of invitations, but it’s important to keep this distinction in mind, because it can impact how you’ll use them.\nLet’s have a look!\nInvitations with 〜(よ)う [yō] or 〜ましょう [mashō]\nOne way to form invitations in Japanese is to use the endings\n〜(よ)う\n(\ntranscribed\nyō\n)\nor\n〜ましょう\n(\ntranscribed\nmashō\n)\n. This is most often used to form “let’s” invitations in a situation where the invitation is expected.\nIn casual contexts\n, you’ll use\n〜う\n(\ntranscribed\nō\n)\nor\n〜よう\n(\ntranscribed\nyō\n)\n. These endings are used to create the volitional form of the verb, a form which is often used to express that you have decided or resolved to do something.\n行く → 行こ\nう\nTranscription:\niku → ik\nō\nTranslation:\ngo → let’s go\n見る → 見\nよう\nTranscription:\nmiru → mi\nyō\nTranslation:\nsee → let’s see\nThough volitional forms will all end with\n〜(よ)う\n(\ntranscribed\nyō\n)\n, you may want to check out our post on the\nvolitional in Japanese\nto learn the full rules for creating this form!\nIn polite contexts\n, you’ll use the ending\n〜ましょう\n(\ntranscribed\nmashō\n)\n. This is the volitional form of the ending\n〜ます\n(\ntranscribed\n-masu\n)\n, so you’ll just start with the polite form and use\n〜ましょう\n(\ntranscribed\nmashō\n)\nto replace the ending.\n行き\nます\n→ 行き\nましょう\nTranscription:\niki\nmasu\n→ iki\nmashō\nTranslation:\ngo → let’s go\n見\nます\n→ 見\nましょう\nTranscription:\nmi\nmasu\n→ mi\nmashō\nTranslation:\nsee → let’s see\n\nTip\nThe volitional form has several other uses as well. For example, it can be used to express that the speaker has a strong will or resolution to do something or it can be used to offer a service. To determine whether it’s used to express volition or invitation, look at who the “doer” is:\nIf the doer of the action is\nthe speaker (“I”)\n, it's a\nvolitional\nsentence:\nその荷物、\n私が\n運びましょう。\nTranscription:\nSono nimotsu,\nwatashi ga\nhakobimashō.\nTranslation:\nI’ll\ncarry that baggage for you.\nIf the doers of the action are\nthe speaker and the listener (“we”)\n, it’s an invitation sentence:\nその荷物、\nいっしょに\n運びましょう。\nTranscription:\nSono nimotsu,\nissho ni\nhakobimashō.\nTranslation:\nLet’s\ncarry that baggage together.\nPlease note that the doer of the action in invitation sentences is often omitted and only implied. In those cases, we need to pay attention to the contextual cues, such as\nいっしょに\n(\ntranscribed\nissho ni\n,\nmeaning\n“\ntogether\n”\n)\nin the second example above.\nWhen to use 〜ましょう [-mashō] or 〜 (よ)う [-yō] for invitations?\nAs we mentioned above,\n〜ましょう\n(\ntranscribed\n-mashō\n)\nand\n〜 (よ)う\n(\ntranscribed\n-yō\n)\nare most typically used to form “let’s” type invitations: to invite someone else to start an action at the same time you do. Specifically, you’ll use these endings in a situation where you’re\npretty sure the other person will accept\n, because you’ve made a prior agreement, because there’s a general expectation, or because you and your listener habitually do the action.\nLet’s look at some examples!\nField trip chaperone:\nみんな、いるね？じゃあ、行こ\nう\n。\nTranscription:\nMin’na iru ne? Jā, ik\nō\n.\nTranslation:\nEveryone is here, right? Well then, let’s go.\nThere is an expectation or agreement that the kids will accept the invitation\nStore Manager:\nでは、今日も笑顔でがんばり\nましょう\n。\nTranscription:\nDewa, kyō mo egao de ganbari\nmashō\n.\nTranslation:\nWell then, as usual, let’s do our best today with smiles on our faces.\nThe employees habitually do this, so it’s expected that they’ll accept the invitation.\nYou\ncan\nuse these two endings to form an invitation when there is no clear expectation of acceptance, but in this case it can sound a little pushy, like you’re not really giving the listener an opportunity to say “no.”\nFor example, imagine it’s the last night before the end of summer break, and the kids had expected to sleep in…\nParent:\n明日は早く起きて、ハイキングに行こ\nう\n！\nTranscription:\nAshita wa hayaku okite, haikingu ni ik\nō\n!\nTranslation:\nLet’s get up early tomorrow and go hiking!\nDoesn’t really give the kids the chance to say “no.”\nChildren:\nえっ〜！\nTranscription:\nĒ!\nTranslation:\nWhaaat?!\n\nTip\nYou can sometimes use these endings to form\n“join-me” type\ninvitations. These, though, can sound quite pushy, so it’s usually only done among close friends and family members.\nExample:\nKenta is playing baseball with his friends in a neighborhood park. He sees his neighbor’s son Akira walking by. Kenta invites Akira to join the game.\nKenta:\nあきらくんも野球し\nよう\n！\nTranscription:\nAkira kun mo yakyū shi\nyō\n!\nTranslation:\nLet’s play baseball, Akira!\nAkira:\nやる、やる！\nTranscription:\nYaru, yaru!\nTranslation:\nWill do!\nHow to soften invitations with 〜ましょう[-mashō] or 〜 (よ)う [-yō]?\nIf you don’t want to sound quite so pushy, you can soften the tone of an invitation with\n〜ましょう\n(\ntranscribed\n-mashō\n)\nand\n〜 (よ)う\n(\ntranscribed\n-yō\n)\nby adding particles to the end of the sentence.\nAdd the\nparticle\nよ\n(\ntranscribed\nyo\n)\nat the end of the invitation to solicit agreement. For example, let’s imagine coworkers Yoko and Hikaru are deciding on where to have lunch:\nYoko:\n今日はこの店で食べ\nましょう\nよ\n。\nTranscription:\nKyō wa kono mise de tabe\nmashō\nyo\n.\nTranslation:\nLet’s eat at this place today,\nshall we\n?\nAdding\nよ\n(\ntranscribed\nyo\n)\ngives Yoko a chance to agree or not.\nTo respond positively to an invitation like this, you can use a generic phrase\nそうしましょう\n(\ntranscribed\nsō shimashō\n)\n, which literally means “Let’s do so.”\nHikaru:\nいいですね。\nそうしましょう\n。\nTranscription:\nIi desu ne.\nSō shimashō\n.\nTranslation:\nIt sounds good to me.\nLet’s do so\n.\nAdding\nよ\n(\ntranscribed\nyo\n)\ngives Yoko a chance to agree or not.\nAdd the\nparticle\nね\n(\ntranscribed\nne\n)\nat the end of an invitation sentence to give a gentle nudge, like when you end an English sentence with “OK?” In this case, the intonation goes up at\nね\n(\ntranscribed\nne\n)\n.\nThis is usually\nnot used between peers or to a superior\n!\nThe ending combinations\n〜(よ)うね\n(\ntranscribed\n-yō ne\n)\nor\nましょうね\n(\ntranscribed\n-mashō ne\n)\n, are often used by parents or other nurturing figures, to give gentler, more roundabout commands or to persuade reluctant children:\nParent:\n暗くなってきたから、今日はもう帰ろ\nう\nね\n。\nTranscription:\nKurakunatte kita kara, kyō wa mō kaer\nō\nne\n.\nTranslation:\nIt’s getting dark, so let’s go home now for today,\nOK\n?\nInvitations with 〜ましょうか [mashō ka] or 〜(よ)うか [yō ka]\nAnother way to form an invitation is to phrase it as a question, by adding the\nquestion particle\nか\n(\ntranscribed\nka\n)\nafter the volitional forms above, creating the endings\n〜ましょうか\n(\ntranscribed\nmashō ka\n)\nand\n〜 (よ)うか\n(\ntranscribed\nyō ka\n)\n.\n〜ましょう\n/\n〜 (よ)う\nTranscription:\n-mashō / yō\n\nか\n(\ntranscribed\nka\n)\nquestion particle\nYou’ll use these endings to form “Let’s…” type commands that give the listener more opportunity to decline. English equivalents might be\n“Shall we…?”\nor\n“Do you want to…?”\n, because they are technically asking the listener what they want to do. As a result, invitations phrased as questions sound gentler and more polite.\nHowever,\n〜ましょうか\n(\ntranscribed\nmashō ka\n)\nand\n〜(よ)うか\n(\ntranscribed\nyō ka\n)\nstill sound most natural in situations where the speaker and the listener\nalready generally agree\nthat they’re going to do something, but might disagree on the\ndetails\n.\nExample:\nCoworkers Tanaka and Suzuki are told by their boss to go to Osaka this afternoon to meet their client there. They are quickly making a travel plan.\nTanaka:\n大阪へは２時の新幹線で行き\nましょう\nか\n。\nTranscription:\nŌsaka e wa ni ji no shinkansen de iki\nmashō\nka\n.\nTranslation:\nShall we go to Osaka by a 2:00-pm shinkansen?\nSuzuki:\nええ、そうしましょう。\nTranscription:\nĒ, sō shimashō.\nTranslation:\nYes, let’s do so.\nYou can also use\nquestion words\nto ask your listener a question about a shared plan.\nquestion word\nTanaka:\n今夜は\nどこ\nに泊まり\nましょう\nか\n。\nTranscription:\nKon’ya wa\ndoko\nni tomari\nmashō\nka\n.\nTranslation:\nWhere\nshall we stay tonight?\nSuzuki:\nマンゴーホテルに泊まり\nましょう\n。安くて便利ですから。\nTranscription:\nMangō hoteru ni tomari\nmashō\n. Yasukute benri desu kara.\nTranslation:\nLet’s stay at the Mango Hotel. It’s inexpensive and convenient.\nYou can also use\n〜(よ)うか\n(\ntranscribed\nyō ka\n)\nand\n〜ましょうか\n(\ntranscribed\n-mashō ka\n)\nto remind the listener to do something they were planning to do already.\nExample:\nTanaka and Suzuki are getting ready to leave the office to catch the 2:00 shinkansen.\nSuzuki:\nもう１時ですね。そろそろ出かけ\nましょう\nか\n。\nTranscription:\nMō ichi ji desu ne. Sorosoro dekake\nmashō\nka\n.\nTranslation:\nIt’s already 1:00, isn’t it? Shall we leave soon?\nTanaka:\nええ、そうしましょう。\nTranscription:\nĒ, sō shimashō.\nTranslation:\nYes, let’s do so.\nIn literal questions, the question marker\nか\n(\ntranscribed\nka\n)\nis often dropped, to soften the tone, especially when the speaker is female. However, you should\nnever omit\nか\n(\ntranscribed\nka\n)\nfrom a casual invitation, regardless of your gender or the level of formality.\n✅\nのど、かわいたね。なんか飲も\nう\nか\n。\nTranscription:\nNodo, kawaita ne. Nanka nom\nō\nka\n.\nTranslation:\nWe are thirsty, aren’t we? Shall we get something to drink?\n❌\nのど、かわいたね。なんか飲もう\nか\n。\nTranscription:\nNodo, kawaita ne. Nanka nom\nō\n?\n\nImportant\nPlease note that we\ncannot\nuse these to form\n“Join-me” type\ninvitations. See the example below:\nExample:\nYoko has been swimming while John is sun-bathing on the poolside. Yoko approaches John and invites him to join her in the water.\n✅\nジョンも泳ご\nう\n。\nTranscription:\nJon mo oyog\nō\n.\nTranslation:\nLet’s swim, John.\n❌\nジョンも泳ご\nう\nか\n。\nTranscription:\nJon mo oyog\nō\nka\n.\nInvitations with 〜ませんか [masen ka] and 〜ないか [nai ka]\nFinally, it’s possible to form an invitation in Japanese using a negative question. The English equivalents would be\n“Won’t you…?”\nor\n“Why don’t we…?”\nNegative verb\nplain or polite form\n\nか\n(\ntranscribed\nka\n)\nquestion particle\nBecause the\nplain negative form\ngenerally ends in\n〜ない\n(\ntranscribed\n-nai\n)\nwhile\npolite negative verbs\nend in\n〜ません\n(\ntranscribed\n-masen\n)\n, invitations following this patter generally end in\n〜ないか\n(\ntranscribed\n-nai ka\n)\nor\n〜ませんか\n(\ntranscribed\n-masen ka\n)\n.\nCasual:\nplain negative verb\n行か\nない\nか\nTranscription:\nika\nnai\nka\nTranslation:\nWhy don’t we go?\nPolite:\npolite negative verb\n行き\nません\nか\nTranscription:\niki\nmasen\nka\nTranslation:\nWhy don’t we go?\nWhen an invitation is given as a negative question, the question marker\nか\n(\ntranscribed\nka\n)\ncan\nbe omitted in casual contexts to soften the tone, as long as your intonation goes up at the end of the sentence.\n疲れたね。ちょっと休憩し\nない\n？\nTranscription:\nTsukareta ne. Chotto kyūkei shi\nnai\n?\nTranslation:\nTired, aren’t we? Why don’t we take five?\n\nTip\nWhen you use a negative question to form an invitation, you’re literally asking someone what their intentions are. So when you hear a question like this, need to use context to tell whether someone is inviting you to do something or just asking you a question. Compare:\nQuestion:\nThe speaker picks up the cup beside the listener and sees there is a small amount of stale coffee in it.\nこれ、飲み\nませんか\n。\nTranscription:\nKore, nomi\nmasen ka\n.\nTranslation:\nAre you drinking this?\nLiterally:\nlit.\nWill you not drink this?\nInvitation:\nThe speaker brings a fresh cup of coffee to the listener.\nこれ、飲み\nませんか\n。\nTranscription:\nKore, nomi\nmasen ka\n.\nTranslation:\nWould you like to drink this?\nLiterally:\nlit.\nWill you not drink this?\nWhen to use 〜ませんか [-masen ka] and 〜ないか [-nai ka] for invitations?\nYou can use\n〜ませんか\n(\ntranscribed\n-masen ka\n)\nor\n〜ないか\n(\ntranscribed\n-nai ka\n)\nin both\n“Let’s” type\nand\n“Join-me” type\ninvitations. They are considered more polite than either of the earlier two patterns we discussed. Here are, then, some of the cases where you’re more likely to hear negative questions used to issue invitations:\nWhen issuing a\n“join-me” type\ninvitation, a negative question is your best option. This is especially true in more polite contexts or with less familiar people.\nExample:\nAfter an office party, Tanaka and Yamada are getting into a taxi to head to the second venue. They see Sato approaching and invite her to share the ride.\nTanaka:\n佐藤さんも乗りませんか。\nTranscription:\nSatō san mo norimasen ka.\nTranslation:\nWhy don’t you ride with us, Ms. Sato?\nSato:\nあ、乗ります。どうもありがとう。\nTranscription:\nA, norimasu. Dōmo arigatō.\nTranslation:\nOh, I will. Thank you very much.\nOften a positive acceptance will also end in\n-ます\n(\ntranscribed\n-masu\n)\n.\n\nTip\nIn this case, the invitation could be issued using just the volitional, though it would sound pushy or very familiar.\n🆗\n佐藤さんも乗り\nましょう\n。\nTranscription:\nSatō san mo nori\nmashō\n.\nBut you\ncannot\nissue this type of invitation using a positive question.\n❌\n佐藤さんも乗り\nましょうか\n。\nTranscription:\nSatō san mo nori\nmashō ka\n.\nWhen issuing a\nfirst\nor\npotentially unexpected\ninvitation, using a negative question can show respect for the listener’s desires.\nThis especially applies to\n“join-me”\ntype\ninvitations.\nExample:\nKimura and his coworkers have been planning a social event. They want to include Tom, who is new to the office, but not sure if he is interested.\nKimura:\n金曜日にお花見をするんですけど、トムさんも参加し\nませんか\n。\nTranscription:\nKin’yōbi ni ohanami o suru n desu kedo, Tomu san mo sanka shi\nmasen ka\n.\nTranslation:\nWe are having a cherry-blossom-viewing picnic on Friday. Won’t you join us too, Tom?\nTom:\nお花見？いいですね！ぜひ参加させてください。\nTranscription:\nOhanami? Ii desu ne! Zehi sanka sasete kudasai.\nTranslation:\nCherry-blossom viewing? Sounds good! Let me join you by all means.\n\nImportant\nThis “join-me” invitation\ncannot\nbe formed using a positive question:\n❌\nトムさんも参加しましょ\nうか\n。\nTranscription:\nTomu san mo sanka shimash\nō ka\n.\nIt also\nwould not generally\nbe formed using just the volitional, since that would sound pushy.\n🆗\nトムさんも参加しましょ\nう\n。\nTranscription:\nTomu san mo sanka shimash\nō\n.\nHowever it can also be used in\n“let’s” type\ninvitations, as below.\nExample:\nMichiko wants to invite her new friend Satsuki to go hiking, but not sure if Satsuki is willing to do so.\nMichiko:\n明日ひまなら、いっしょにハイキングに行かない？\nTranscription:\nAshita hima nara, issho ni haikingu ni ikanai?\nTranslation:\nIf you are free tomorrow, why don’t we go hiking together?\nSatsuki:\nハイキング？うん、いいよ。\n行こう\n。\nTranscription:\nHaikingu? Un, ii yo.\nIkō\n.\nTranslation:\nHiking? Sure.\nLet’s go\n.\n\nImportant\nEven though\n〜ませんか\n(\ntranscribed\n-masen ka\n)\nis a more polite form of invitation, it is still grammatically familiar, so it is most fitting when the listener is socially equal to the speaker.  If inviting a superior to do something, the humble request form\n〜ていただけませんか\n(\ntranscribed\n-te itadakemasen ka\n)\nwill generally sound better.\n❓\n先生もいらっしゃい\nませんか\n。\nTranscription:\nSensei mo irasshai\nmasen ka\n.\nTranslation:\nWhy don’t you join us, Professor?\nIt might seem polite enough, but it shows that the speaker is talking to the professor as if the professor is their equal.\n✅\n先生もおい\nでいただけませんか\n。\nTranscription:\nSensei mo oi\nde itadakemasen ka\n.\nTranslation:\nCould you please join us too, Professor?\nA humble expression\nCheck out our post on\nmaking requests in Japanese\nto learn more!\nWhen invitations with 〜ませんか [masen ka] or 〜ないか [nai ka] sound unnatural\nEven though invitations that use negative questions are broadly more polite, there are some occasions where you cannot or should not issue an invitation ending with\n〜ませんか\n(\ntranscribed\nmasen ka\n)\nor\n〜ないか\n(\ntranscribed\nnai ka\n)\n. Let’s take a closer look!\n〜ませんか\n(\ntranscribed\n-masen ka\n)\nand\nないか\n(\ntranscribed\n-nai ka\n)\ndon’t sound natural when there is a prior\nexpectation\nthat you and the listener would do something.\nExample:\nThe teacher comes into the classroom and sees all the students at their seats.\n✅\nでは、クラスを始め\nましょう\n/ クラスを始め\nましょうか\n。\nTranscription:\nDewa, kurasu o hajime\nmashō\n/ kurasu o\nmashō ka\n.\nTranslation:\nWell then, let’s start the class / shall we start the class?\n❓\nでは、クラスを始め\nませんか\n。\nTranscription:\nDewa, kurasu o hajime\nmasen ka\n.\n〜ませんか\n(\ntranscribed\n-masen ka\n)\nand\n〜ないか\n(\ntranscribed\n-nai ka\n)\ncannot be used to\nprompt the listener to continue\nthe act already started, as the example below. In such cases, we should use\n〜ましょう\n(\ntranscribed\n-mashō\n)\nor\n〜(よ)う\n(\ntranscribed\nyō\n)\n.\nExample:\nYoko and John are already on the way to a party when John suddenly stops and starts debating if going to the party is a good idea. Yoko prompts him to get going.\n✅\n何よ、今さら。さあ、行こ\nう\n。\nTranscription:\nNani yo, ima sara. Sā, ik\nō\n.\nTranslation:\nToo late for that. Now, let’s go.\n❓\nさあ、行こ\nうか\n。\nTranscription:\nSā, ik\nō ka\n.\nTranslation:\nNow, shall we go?\n❌\nさあ、行か\nない？\nTranscription:\nSā, ika\nnai?\nTranslation:\nNow, why don’t we go?\n〜ませんか\n(\ntranscribed\n-masen ka\n)\nand\n〜ないか\n(\ntranscribed\n-nai ka\n)\nwon’t sound right when the situation is urgent\n. Use\n〜ましょう\n(\ntranscribed\n-mashō\n)\nor\n〜(よ)う\n(\ntranscribed\nyō\n)\ninstead.\nExample:\nA group of friends are running through the airport, trying to catch a flight to Okinawa.\n✅\n飛行機出ちゃうよ。急ご\nう\n。\nTranscription:\nHikōki dechau yo. Isog\nō\n.\nTranslation:\nOur flight is gonna depart (without us). Let’s hurry.\n❓\n急ご\nうか\n。\nTranscription:\nIsog\nō ka\n.\nTranslation:\nShall we hurry?\n❌\n急が\nない？\nTranscription:\nIsoga\nnai?\nTranslation:\nWhy don’t we hurry?\n〜ませんか\n(\ntranscribed\n-masen ka\n)\nand\n〜ないか\n(\ntranscribed\n-nai ka\n)\ncannot be used\nalong with question words\n, to ask about a shared plan. In such cases, we need to use\n〜ましょうか\n(\ntranscribed\n-mashō ka\n)\nor\n〜(よ)うか\n(\ntranscribed\nyō ka\n)\n:\nExample:\nSachiko is at a bakery with her daughter to buy some sweets for the family gathering.\n✅\nドーナツ、いくつ買お\nうか\n。\nTranscription:\nDōnatsu, ikutsu ka\nō ka\n.\nTranslation:\nHow many doughnuts shall we buy?\n❌\nドーナツ、いくつ買わ\nない\n？\nTranscription:\nDōnatsu, ikutsu kawa\nnai\n?\nHow to decline an invitation in Japanese?\nWe’ve already seen several ways to respond positively to an invitation, but how should you decline one?\nParagraph contentIn Japanese, a straight “No” is considered rude, in a polite conversation. Instead, it’s better to use vague phrases such as\nはちょっと...\n(\ntranscribed\nwa chotto…\n,\nmeaning\n“\na little bit...\n”\n)\nto politely decline an invitation. See an example below!\nSpeaker A:\n土曜日、映画を見に行きませんか。\nTranscription:\nDoyōbi, eiga o mi ni ikimasen ka.\nTranslation:\nWhy don’t we go to see a movie on Saturday?\nSpeaker B:\nすみません、土曜日\nはちょっと\n・・・。\nTranscription:\nSumimasen, doyōbi\nwa chotto\n….\nTranslation:\nI’m sorry, Saturday is\na little bit\n…\nTo sum up\nIn this post, we have covered how to express invitation in Japanese, using three sentence patterns:\n〜ましょう /〜(よ)う\n,\n〜ましょうか / 〜(よ)うか\n, and\n〜ませんか / 〜ないか\n. Here's a quick review table:\nVolitional form\nPositive question\nNegative question\nPolite style\nmasu-stem\n\nましょう\nTranscription:\nmashō\nmasu-stem\n\nましょうか\nTranscription:\nmashō ka\nmasu-stem\n\nませんか\nTranscription:\nmasen ka\nPlain style\nVolitional form\nVolitional form\n\nか\nTranscription:\nka\nV-nai\n\nない (か)\nTranscription:\nnai (ka)\nEnglish\n“Let’s….”\n“Shall we…?”\n“Won’t you…?”\n“Why don’t we…?”\n“Let’s” type?\n✅\n✅\n✅\n“Join-me” type?\n✅\n(might sound pushy)\n❌\n✅\nTone\nStraightforward; enthusiastic\nSofter than\nましょう\n(\ntranscribed\nmashō\n)\nPolite; considerate\n(but not for superiors)\nQuestion words?\nN/A\n✅\n❌\nPreferred when…\nthere is prior agreement or it’s a habitual action\nprompting to resume the action\nurgent situation\nasking the listener’s volition on accepting the invitation\nprompting to do an action anticipated by the listener\nquestion words are used\ninitial invitation\nshowing our respect to the listener’s volition\ninviting the listener to join in the action the speaker has already started\nDon’t forget to try our\nJapanese invitation activities\nto practice what we covered in this post. Happy learning!\nOn this page\n\nWhat are invitation sentences in Japanese?\nInvitations with 〜(よ)う [yō] or 〜ましょう [mashō]\nWhen to use 〜ましょう [-mashō] or 〜 (よ)う [-yō] for invitations?\nHow to soften invitations with 〜ましょう[-mashō] or 〜 (よ)う [-yō]?\nInvitations with 〜ましょうか [mashō ka] or 〜(よ)うか [yō ka]\nInvitations with 〜ませんか [masen ka] and 〜ないか [nai ka]\nWhen to use 〜ませんか [-masen ka] and 〜ないか [-nai ka] for invitations?\nWhen invitations with 〜ませんか [masen ka] or 〜ないか [nai ka] sound unnatural\nHow to decline an invitation in Japanese?\nTo sum up\n\nJump to Resources\nOn this page\nWhat are invitation sentences in Japanese?\nInvitations with 〜(よ)う [yō] or 〜ましょう [mashō]\nWhen to use 〜ましょう [-mashō] or 〜 (よ)う [-yō] for invitations?\nHow to soften invitations with 〜ましょう[-mashō] or 〜 (よ)う [-yō]?\nInvitations with 〜ましょうか [mashō ka] or 〜(よ)うか [yō ka]\nInvitations with 〜ませんか [masen ka] and 〜ないか [nai ka]\nWhen to use 〜ませんか [-masen ka] and 〜ないか [-nai ka] for invitations?\nWhen invitations with 〜ませんか [masen ka] or 〜ないか [nai ka] sound unnatural\nHow to decline an invitation in Japanese?\nTo sum up\n\nJump to Resources\nMeet the Author\nMayumi Ito\nA former university Japanese instructor with MAs in English and education, Mayumi has worked on various Mango Japanese projects since 2008.\nResources\nPractice and study\n\n\"Invitations: How to say “let’s…” in Japanese?\"\nPractice\n\nRelated articles\nverbs\nJapanese\nThe volitional form: How to state intentions or suggest actions in Japanese?\ngrammar tips\nparticles\nJapanese\nよ [yo] and ね [ne]: How to use these sentence-ending particles in Japanese?\nparticles\nsentence structure\nJapanese\nか [ka], かな [kana], and っけ [kke]: How to use these Japanese sentence-ending particles?\nparticles\nsentence structure\nJapanese\nHow to ask questions in Japanese?\nverbs\nJapanese\nWhat are the verb groups in Japanese?\nsentence structure\nJapanese\nHow to make requests in Japanese?\nMango\nMANGO\nMango\nMANGO\nAbout Mango\nCompany\nCareers\nResearch & Efficacy\nAffiliates\nMango For\nEducators\nBusinesses\nLibraries\nHigher Ed\nGovernment\nHomeschool\nAccount\nLogin\nSign-Up\nFind Mango\nSales & Support\nGet a Quote\nContact Us\nHelp Center\nDownload on the\nApp Store\nGET IT ON\nGoogle Play\nLegal Center\nTerms\nPrivacy\nCookies\nPrivacy Rights\n© 2026 Mango Languages, All rights reserved",
