      "sourceText": "つもりだ (JLPT N5) | Bunpro\nLoading user data...\nBunpro\nGrammar Info\nN5 Lesson 10: 12/12\nつもりだ\nTo plan to, To intend to\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nつもりだ\nTo plan to, To intend to\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nDetails\nExamples\nResources\nReady to transform your studies?\nLearn N5 in under a month!\nTry now, no credit card required!\nTry Bunpro\nLearn More\nStructure\nStandard\nPolite\nVerb［る］\n+\nつもり\n+\nだ\nVerb［ない］\n+\nつもり\n+\nだ\n(*)\nVerb +\nつもり\n+\nは\n(1)\n+ ない\n(**)\n(1)\nが\n(*)\nTo intend not to\n(**)\nTo have no intention of\nDetails\nPart of Speech\nNoun\nWord Type\nAuxiliary Verb\nRegister\nStandard\nRare Kanji\n積もり\nAbout つもりだ\nつもり\nだ\n(or\nです\n) is a grammar point that can have several meanings in Japanese, but is often translated simply as 'intend to (A)', or 'plan to (A)'. It can be used after the plain (dictionary) form of verbs, or after the ない form.\nIn the last two sentences, there is quite a large difference in nuance, and can be thought of as similar to the difference between 'to intend not to' ない\nつもり\n, and 'to have no intention of'\nつもり\nは\nない in English. The former will mean that (A) is being actively avoided, while the latter will indicate that one simply has no plans to (A).\nFun Fact\nThe literal meaning of\nつもり\ncomes from the verb\n積\n(\nつ\n)\nもる, which means 'to pile up', or 'to load'. Due to this,\nつもり\nis regularly used to describe people that are doing something that is out of character for them, meaning that they have 'piled up' whatever feeling was required to act that way.\nIn this example, the nuance of\nつもり\nis 'so you're really going to act like you're apologizing?!' However, the literal meaning is closer to 'so you've really piled up enough (nerve) in order to act like that's an apology?!'\nCaution\nThe\nは\nin\nつもり\nは\nない is interchangeable with\nが\nor じゃ (usually では is not used). The difference in nuance is that\nが\ngives the impression of being 'uninterested' in (A), while じゃ simply expresses that (A) is not the intended result.\nTopics\nThat Is My Intention But...\nN5 Topic\nIn this Topic, we will be learning about two unrelated yet relatively easy words. First, we'll learn about けれども, which is a more formal version of けど, meaning 'but'. Second, we'll learn about つもり, which is used to say that one 'intends' to do something.\n1m\nSynonyms\nExpand All\nよていだ\nよていだ\nPlan to, Intend to\nN4 Grammar\nBoth つもりだ and よていだ express future intentions or plans, but with different nuances. つもりだ emphasizes personal intent or decision, such as 'I intend to' or 'I hope to,' without necessarily implying a set timeframe. よていだ focuses more on planning or scheduling, suggesting that the action is expected to happen at a specific time.\nRelated\nExpand All\nつもりで\nWith the intention of doing, Instead of..., Act as if or pretend, Believing that..., Thinking that...\nN2 Grammar\nつもり basically means 'intention.' When used at the end of a sentence in the form つもりだ, it expresses future plans, similar to 'I intend to' or 'I hope to.' In the form つもりで, it connects to another part of the sentence and means doing something with a certain intention or belief in mind. When preceded by a past tense verb, つもり can also mean 'pretending' or 'acting as if... (though it's not actually the case).'\n〜ようと思う・〜おうと思う\nう・よう + とおもう\nI think I will, I think I shall, Going to, Gonna (Volitional + と思う)\nN4 Grammar\nBoth つもりだ and 〜ようと思う・〜おうと思う can both be used to express intentions or plans for the future. つもりだ feels slightly more 'official' or definite, similar to the English 'I intend to' or 'I hope to'. In contrast, 〜ようと思う・〜おうと思う has a more casual and less definitive feel, similar to the English 'I'm thinking of (doing something)'.\nはずだ\nはずだ\nBound to (be), Expected to (be), Should be (able)\nN4 Grammar\nつもりだ is used to express personal intentions or plans, similar to 'I hope to' or 'I intend to' in English. In contrast, はずだ expresses an expectation or belief that something will happen, based on logic or evidence. While つもりだ is subjective and often refers to the speaker's own plans, はずだ is more objective and is used for expectations about others or events outside the speaker's control.\nVocab Coverage\nAll Bunpro Vocab that appears on this item.\nExpand List\nKnowledge Check\nExamples\n--:--\nSentence\nTranslation\n日本\n(\nにほん\n)\nに\n行\n(\nい\n)\nく\nつもり\nだ。\nI\nintend\nto go to Japan.\n日本語\n(\nにほんご\n)\nを\n勉強\n(\nべんきょう\n)\nする\nつもりだ\n。\nI\nintend\nto study Japanese.\n明日\n(\nあした\n)\n早\n(\nはや\n)\nく\n起\n(\nお\n)\nき\nる\nつもりです\n。\nI\nintend\nto wake up early tomorrow.\n車\n(\nくるま\n)\nで\n行\n(\nい\n)\nく\nつもりです\n。\nI\nintend\nto go by car.\nスポーツが\n上手\n(\nじょうず\n)\nになる\nつもりだった\n。\nI\nintended\nto become good at sports.\nGet more example sentences!\nPremium users get access to 12 example sentences on all Grammar Points.\nTry Bunpro\nSelf-Study Sentences\nStudy your own way!\nAdd sentences and study them alongside Bunpro sentences.\nTry Bunpro\nOnline\nMore about つもりだ\nLearn Japanese Adventure\nOffline\n[DBJG] A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar\nPage 503\nGenki I\nPage 196\nGenki I 2nd Edition\nPage 234\nGenki I 3rd Edition\nPage 236\nみんなの日本語 II\nPage 42 [CH 31]\nTrack Resources!\nBunpro tracks all of the resources you’ve visited, and offers relevant bookmarks of physical books to help with offline tracking.\nTry Bunpro\nつもりだ – Grammar Discussion\nMost Recent Replies (17 in total)\njugglenutz\nWhen using a -する noun/verb, should するbe included or omitted?\nEx: 自転車屋を連絡（する？）つもりです\nFuga\nHey @jugglenutz !\nSince this grammar point is used after verbs and ない forms, you do need to include する after する verbs!\nEx:自転車屋に連絡するつもりです。\nUsing つもり right after nouns will make the sentence sound very unnatural.\nEx:自転車屋に連絡つもりです。(Unnatural)\nI hope this answers your question!\njugglenutz\nPerfect, thanks so much.\nGot questions about つもりだ?\nJoin us to discuss, ask, and learn together!\nJoin the Discussion\nBunpro\nSimplifying Japanese\nOfficial Apps\nLearn More\nJoin us!\nCompany\nNews\nCareers\nAbout Us